<script>window.SATLEX_VOCAB = [{"id":"vocab_001","entry":"ambivalent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Having mixed or conflicting feelings about something at the same time.","secondaryMeaning":"Simultaneous attraction toward and repulsion from the same object or person.","passage":"Although she had dreamed of attending a prestigious university for years, Marisol felt ______ about the acceptance letter: the scholarship covered tuition, but moving across the country meant leaving her ailing grandmother behind.","correctAnswer":"ambivalent","distractors":["apathetic","resigned","indignant"],"explanation":"'Ambivalent' means holding opposing feelings at once. The colon signals an explanation of mixed feelings—the offer is both attractive (full scholarship) and painful (leaving family). 'Apathetic' implies no feeling, 'resigned' implies reluctant acceptance, and 'indignant' implies anger—none capture the simultaneous pull in two directions."},{"id":"vocab_002","entry":"anomaly","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A deviation from the common rule, type, or expected pattern.","secondaryMeaning":"An unusual or irregular occurrence that stands out from a dataset.","passage":"The planet's orbit was considered an ______ because it retraced its path in a figure-eight, a configuration no other body in the system exhibited.","correctAnswer":"anomaly","distractors":["analogue","antiquity","anticipation"],"explanation":"'Anomaly' means a deviation from the norm. The clause 'no other body in the system exhibited' confirms the orbit is irregular. 'Analogue' (a parallel), 'antiquity' (age), and 'anticipation' (foreseeing) do not fit the idea of a lone exception."},{"id":"vocab_003","entry":"candid","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Honest and direct, even when the truth is unflattering.","secondaryMeaning":"Unposed or unposed-for (as in a candid photograph).","passage":"In her memoir, the diplomat was remarkably ______ about her own misjudgments, admitting that her early optimism about the ceasefire had been naïve.","correctAnswer":"candid","distractors":["evasive","embellished","complacent"],"explanation":"'Candid' means openly honest. The phrase 'admitting that her early optimism…had been naïve' shows willingness to reveal her own flaws. 'Evasive' (avoiding the truth), 'embellished' (decorated/exaggerated), and 'complacent' (smugly satisfied) contradict the admission of error."},{"id":"vocab_004","entry":"dearth","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A scarcity or lack of something; an inadequate supply.","secondaryMeaning":"An acute famine or shortage (archaic/literal sense).","passage":"The historian attributed the empire's collapse not to invasion but to a ______ of competent administrators after a generation of civil war had emptied the bureaucracies.","correctAnswer":"dearth","distractors":["surplus","decline","decree"],"explanation":"'Dearth' means a severe shortage. 'Emptied the bureaucracies' is the clue that there were too few administrators. 'Surplus' is the opposite; 'decline' is too vague (a decline in numbers, not a shortage of people); 'decree' is an order, unrelated to quantity."},{"id":"vocab_005","entry":"meticulous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Showing extreme or careful attention to even the smallest details.","secondaryMeaning":"Overly fussy or fastidious about precision.","passage":"The restorer was so ______ that she spent three weeks matching the exact pigment of a single square centimeter of the fifteenth-century fresco.","correctAnswer":"meticulous","distractors":["haphazard","ambitious","frugal"],"explanation":"'Meticulous' means extremely careful about detail. The clue 'three weeks…a single square centimeter' shows extraordinary attention to a tiny detail. 'Haphazard' (careless) is the opposite; 'ambitious' (aiming high) and 'frugal' (thrifty) don't describe carefulness."},{"id":"vocab_006","entry":"pragmatic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Practical and realistic, concerned with what actually works rather than theory.","secondaryMeaning":"Relating to the philosophical movement of pragmatism.","passage":"Rather than insisting on a perfect treaty, the negotiator took a ______ approach, accepting partial concessions that could be expanded in later rounds.","correctAnswer":"pragmatic","distractors":["ideological","obstinate","improvident"],"explanation":"'Pragmatic' means practical and willing to accept workable compromises. 'Accepting partial concessions' signals a practical, non-idealistic stance. 'Ideological' (rigidly principled) and 'obstinate' (stubborn) oppose compromise; 'improvident' (thrifty-less) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_007","entry":"venerate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To regard with deep respect and reverence, often due to age or achievement.","secondaryMeaning":"To worship or revere as sacred (religious sense).","passage":"Centuries after her death, the chemist continued to be ______ as a saint in her hometown, where the laboratory she founded had become a place of pilgrimage.","correctAnswer":"venerated","distractors":["vilified","forgotten","questioned"],"explanation":"'Venerated' means deeply revered. 'As a saint' and 'place of pilgrimage' signal religious-style devotion. 'Vilified' (condemned) is opposite; 'forgotten' contradicts 'centuries after…continued'; 'questioned' lacks the devotional force of 'pilgrimage'."},{"id":"vocab_008","entry":"enigmatic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Difficult to interpret or understand; mysterious and puzzling.","secondaryMeaning":"Resembling an enigma (a riddle or puzzle).","passage":"Despite decades of scholarship, the painter's late canvases remain ______: scholars still debate whether the blurred figures represent grief, hope, or something else entirely.","correctAnswer":"enigmatic","distractors":["derivative","decorative","deceptive"],"explanation":"'Enigmatic' means mysterious or hard to interpret. 'Scholars still debate' and 'something else entirely' show the meaning is unresolved. 'Derivative' (unoriginal), 'decorative' (ornamental), and 'deceptive' (intentionally misleading) don't capture scholarly puzzlement."},{"id":"vocab_009","entry":"prudent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Acting with care and good judgment for the future; cautious.","secondaryMeaning":"Wise in practical affairs; sagacious.","passage":"Given the volatility of the grain market, the treasurer deemed it ______ to lock in next year's supply at a fixed price rather than gamble on a future surplus.","correctAnswer":"prudent","distractors":["frivolous","inevitable","audacious"],"explanation":"'Prudent' means exercising careful foresight. The treasurer weighs volatility and chooses the safe, fixed-price option. 'Frivolous' (silly) is opposite; 'inevitable' describes an outcome, not a decision; 'audacious' (bold) implies risk-taking, the opposite of locking in a price."},{"id":"vocab_010","entry":"prosaic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Lacking imagination or excitement; ordinary and unimaginative.","secondaryMeaning":"Relating to prose (literal/etymological sense).","passage":"Contrary to the dramatic legends that surround her voyage, the explorer's actual journal entries were surprisingly ______, filled with notes on wind speed and rations rather than daring exploits.","correctAnswer":"prosaic","distractors":["poetic","providential","prodigious"],"explanation":"'Prosaic' means dull and ordinary. 'Notes on wind speed and rations' contrast with 'dramatic legends' to signal mundanity. 'Poetic' is opposite; 'providential' (lucky) and 'prodigious' (enormous) don't describe the banal content of the journal."},{"id":"vocab_011","entry":"fervent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Showing intense, passionate, and heartfelt emotion or conviction.","secondaryMeaning":"Hot or glowing (literal, etymological sense, now rare).","passage":"The abolitionist was a ______ believer in universal suffrage, writing editorials by candlelight and traveling on horseback to deliver speeches in villages that no other reformer would visit.","correctAnswer":"fervent","distractors":["tepid","cynical","reluctant"],"explanation":"'Fervent' means intensely passionate. 'Writing editorials by candlelight' and traveling to remote villages show deep commitment. 'Tepid' (lukewarm) is opposite; 'cynical' (distrustful) and 'reluctant' (unwilling) contradict the devotion shown."},{"id":"vocab_012","entry":"perfunctory","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Carried out with minimal effort or attention; superficial and routine.","secondaryMeaning":"Done as a duty without genuine interest.","passage":"The inspector's ______ examination of the bridge—a five-minute walk and a single photograph—missed the corroded cables that would fail three months later.","correctAnswer":"perfunctory","distractors":["exhaustive","perpetual","meticulous"],"explanation":"'Perfunctory' means done casually without care. The 'five-minute walk and a single photograph' signal a token effort. 'Exhaustive' (thorough) and 'meticulous' (careful) are opposites; 'perpetual' (constant) describes duration, not effort."},{"id":"vocab_013","entry":"ostentatious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Characterized by vulgar or excessive display, intended to impress or attract notice.","secondaryMeaning":"Showy or pretentious in nature.","passage":"The newly wealthy industrialist filled his foyer with gilded statues and marble busts of himself, an ______ display that embarrassed even his admirers.","correctAnswer":"ostentatious","distractors":["understated","utilitarian","obsequious"],"explanation":"'Ostentatious' means showy to the point of vulgarity. 'Gilded statues' and 'marble busts of himself' show self-aggrandizing display. 'Understated' is opposite; 'utilitarian' (practical) and 'obsequious' (fawning toward others) don't fit self-display."},{"id":"vocab_014","entry":"capricious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Given to sudden, unpredictable changes of mood or behavior; fickle.","secondaryMeaning":"Subject to whim; impulsive and arbitrary.","passage":"The monarch's ______ favor could elevate a courtier to minister in the morning and condemn him to the tower by nightfall, leaving the entire court in perpetual dread.","correctAnswer":"capricious","distractors":["consistent","calculating","compassionate"],"explanation":"'Capricious' means impulsively changeable. 'In the morning…by nightfall' shows sudden reversal. 'Consistent' is opposite; 'calculating' implies deliberate planning, not whim; 'compassionate' (kind) contradicts condemning someone to the tower."},{"id":"vocab_015","entry":"exonerate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To clear someone of blame or accusations; to free from responsibility.","secondaryMeaning":"To release from a duty or obligation.","passage":"Decades after the chemist's conviction for fraud, the discovery of his original lab notebooks ______ him, proving that the data he had been accused of fabricating were authentic.","correctAnswer":"exonerated","distractors":["implicated","condemned","ignored"],"explanation":"'Exonerated' means cleared of blame. 'Proving that the data…were authentic' directly clears the accused. 'Implicated' (incriminated) is opposite; 'condemned' (declared guilty) is opposite; 'ignored' lacks the legal force of clearing a name."},{"id":"vocab_016","entry":"proliferate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To increase rapidly in number; to multiply or spread widely.","secondaryMeaning":"To reproduce cells or organisms by repeated division (biological sense).","passage":"Once the new antibiotic lost its effectiveness, resistant strains of the bacterium began to ______ across the ward, appearing in patients who had never been exposed to the original infection.","correctAnswer":"proliferate","distractors":["subside","stagnate","diversify"],"explanation":"'Proliferate' means to multiply rapidly. 'Appearing in patients who had never been exposed' shows fast spread. 'Subside' (decrease) is opposite; 'stagnate' (remain unchanged) implies no growth; 'diversify' (vary) doesn't imply rapid multiplication."},{"id":"vocab_017","entry":"augment","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To make something greater by adding to it; to enlarge or supplement.","secondaryMeaning":"A musical interval increased by a semitone (specialized sense).","passage":"To ______ the meager harvest, the village imported grain from neighboring regions and rationed it carefully through the winter months.","correctAnswer":"augment","distractors":["exhaust","diminish","confiscate"],"explanation":"'Augment' means to supplement or increase. 'Imported grain' and 'rationed it' show they added to a poor harvest. 'Exhaust' (use up) and 'diminish' (reduce) are opposites; 'confiscate' (seize) implies taking away, not adding."},{"id":"vocab_018","entry":"substantiate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To provide evidence or proof that supports a claim or statement.","secondaryMeaning":"To give substance or concrete reality to something abstract.","passage":"The journalist could not ______ her explosive allegations with any documents or named sources, and the newspaper declined to publish the story.","correctAnswer":"substantiate","distractors":["conceal","speculate","circumvent"],"explanation":"'Substantiate' means to support with evidence. 'Documents or named sources' are the missing proof. 'Conceal' (hide) is opposite; 'speculate' (guess without proof) is what the journalist did instead of substantiating; 'circumvent' (bypass) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_019","entry":"digress","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To stray temporarily from the main topic, especially in speech or writing.","secondaryMeaning":"To turn aside from a path or course (literal/archaic sense).","passage":"The professor had a habit of ______ from the lecture into anecdotes about his graduate-school days, much to the amusement of his students and the frustration of his syllabus.","correctAnswer":"digressing","distractors":["progressing","deviating","declining"],"explanation":"'Digress' means to wander from the main subject. 'Into anecdotes' instead of the lecture topic signals straying. 'Progressing' (advancing) is opposite in sense; 'deviating' is close but typically means departing from a norm or path, not a topic—the academic register of 'digress' is the SAT-preferred choice. 'Declining' (worsening) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_020","entry":"undermine","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To weaken or damage something gradually, often in a stealthy way.","secondaryMeaning":"To dig a tunnel beneath a fortification (literal military origin).","passage":"The lead scientist's credibility was severely ______ by the discovery that he had falsified his early research data, a revelation that cast every subsequent finding into doubt.","correctAnswer":"undermined","distractors":["bolstered","substantiated","corroborated"],"explanation":"'Undermined' means weakened. 'Falsified his early research data' and 'cast every subsequent finding into doubt' signal a destructive effect on credibility. 'Bolstered' (strengthened), 'substantiated' (proved), and 'corroborated' (confirmed) are all positive and opposite."},{"id":"vocab_021","entry":"qualify","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To limit, restrict, or modify the strength of a statement; to make less absolute.","secondaryMeaning":"To meet the requirements for; to earn a right (e.g., to qualify for a tournament).","passage":"The author does not reject the theory outright; rather, she ______ her endorsement by noting that it holds only for small populations and short timeframes.","correctAnswer":"qualifies","distractors":["reiterates","withdraws","exaggerates"],"explanation":"'Qualify' here means to limit or modify a claim—not to earn a credential. 'Only for small populations and short timeframes' restricts the endorsement. 'Reiterates' (repeats) would strengthen it; 'withdraws' (retracts) would reject it, which the author 'does not' do; 'exaggerates' (overstates) is opposite to restricting."},{"id":"vocab_022","entry":"yield","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To produce or provide (a result, crop, or finding); to give way under pressure.","secondaryMeaning":"To surrender or submit to another's authority; to concede.","passage":"The new strain of wheat ______ nearly twice the grain per hectare as the conventional variety, even in soils that had been considered too saline for cultivation.","correctAnswer":"yields","distractors":["withholds","consumes","exhausts"],"explanation":"'Yield' here means to produce (a crop)—not to surrender. 'Twice the grain per hectare' shows production. 'Withholds' (keeps back) is opposite; 'consumes' (uses up) reverses the producer role; 'exhausts' (depletes) contradicts productive soil use."},{"id":"vocab_023","entry":"champion","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To actively support, defend, or advocate for a cause or person.","secondaryMeaning":"A person who has defeated all opponents in a competition (noun sense).","passage":"Long before the cause was popular, the young legislator ______ the rights of tenant farmers, introducing three reform bills in her first term alone.","correctAnswer":"championed","distractors":["concealed","contested","conceded"],"explanation":"'Champion' here is a verb meaning to advocate for. 'Introducing three reform bills' shows active support. 'Concealed' (hid) is opposite; 'contested' (opposed) is opposite; 'conceded' (admitted defeat) contradicts the reformer's energy."},{"id":"vocab_024","entry":"intimate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To suggest or hint at something indirectly, without stating it outright.","secondaryMeaning":"Closely acquainted or personal (adjective, as in 'intimate friends').","passage":"In the closing paragraphs, the memoirist ______ that her father's silence about the war concealed a deeper shame, though she stopped short of saying so directly.","correctAnswer":"intimated","distractors":["declared","demanded","denied"],"explanation":"'Intimate' as a verb means to hint indirectly. 'Stopped short of saying so directly' is the clue that the suggestion was oblique. 'Declared' (stated openly) is opposite; 'demanded' (insisted) is too forceful; 'denied' (contradicted) reverses the meaning."},{"id":"vocab_025","entry":"wanting","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Lacking, deficient, or falling short of what is required or expected.","secondaryMeaning":"Desiring or wishing for something (participle of want).","passage":"Although the proposal was elaborate, its treatment of funding was decidedly ______, leaving reviewers uncertain how the project would survive its second year.","correctAnswer":"wanting","distractors":["comprehensive","excessive","anticipated"],"explanation":"'Wanting' here means deficient or lacking. 'Leaving reviewers uncertain how the project would survive' shows the funding section was inadequate. 'Comprehensive' (complete) is opposite; 'excessive' (too much) reverses the shortage; 'anticipated' (expected) doesn't fit the criticism."},{"id":"vocab_026","entry":"check","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To slow, restrain, or hold back the progress of something.","secondaryMeaning":"To verify or examine; a financial instrument (noun).","passage":"The discovery of a competing manuscript did not ______ the scholar's enthusiasm for publication; if anything, it sharpened her resolve to defend the original attribution.","correctAnswer":"check","distractors":["accelerate","endorse","finalize"],"explanation":"'Check' here means to restrain or halt—not to verify. The contrast 'if anything, it sharpened her resolve' shows the discovery failed to slow her down. 'Accelerate' (speed up) is opposite in direction; 'endorse' (approve) and 'finalize' (complete) don't describe restraint."},{"id":"vocab_027","entry":"found","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To establish or set up (an institution, organization, or principle).","secondaryMeaning":"To melt and pour metal into a mold (cast); past tense of find.","passage":"The philanthropist ______ the research institute in 1923 with a single endowment, intending it to study tropical diseases long after her own retirement.","correctAnswer":"founded","distractors":["discovered","funded","dissolved"],"explanation":"'Found' here means to establish—not to discover (past tense of find). 'The research institute in 1923 with a single endowment' shows creation. 'Discovered' (found as past tense of find) is the trap; 'funded' (provided money) is a related but distinct act; 'dissolved' (closed) is opposite."},{"id":"vocab_028","entry":"accommodate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To adapt or adjust to; to take into account and provide for.","secondaryMeaning":"To provide lodging or room for; to oblige.","passage":"The revised curriculum was designed to ______ students who entered the program with vastly different levels of preparation, offering optional review modules alongside the core lectures.","correctAnswer":"accommodate","distractors":["standardize","exclude","accelerate"],"explanation":"'Accommodate' here means to adapt to varying needs. 'Optional review modules' for 'different levels of preparation' show adaptation. 'Standardize' (make uniform) is opposite; 'exclude' (keep out) is opposite; 'accelerate' (speed up) ignores the variation the curriculum addresses."},{"id":"vocab_029","entry":"disinterested","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Impartial; free of personal bias or self-interest.","secondaryMeaning":"Uninterested or bored (common confusion, often considered incorrect in formal use).","passage":"It is essential that the ethics panel remain entirely ______ in the cases it reviews, lest any verdict be dismissed as the product of bias rather than judgment.","correctAnswer":"disinterested","distractors":["uninterested","prejudiced","engaged"],"explanation":"'Disinterested' means impartial—not bored (the common confusion with 'uninterested'). 'Lest any verdict be dismissed as the product of bias' demands objectivity. 'Uninterested' (bored) is the trap; 'prejudiced' (biased) is opposite; 'engaged' (involved) doesn't ensure fairness."},{"id":"vocab_030","entry":"exhaustive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Thorough and comprehensive, leaving nothing out.","secondaryMeaning":"Tending to drain or use up resources (related to exhaust).","passage":"After an ______ search of the archives that spanned four countries and three centuries of correspondence, the historian concluded that the letter had likely been destroyed.","correctAnswer":"exhaustive","distractors":["exhausting","cursory","futile"],"explanation":"'Exhaustive' means complete and thorough—not tiring (the related 'exhausting'). 'Four countries and three centuries' shows comprehensiveness. 'Exhausting' (tiring) describes the effect on the searcher, not the search; 'cursory' (superficial) is opposite; 'futile' (pointless) describes the outcome, not the thoroughness."},{"id":"vocab_031","entry":"apprehend","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To grasp the meaning of; to understand or perceive.","secondaryMeaning":"To arrest and bring to trial (legal sense).","passage":"It took the students several readings to ______ the philosopher's central argument, so densely was it woven with qualifications and counterexamples.","correctAnswer":"apprehend","distractors":["arrest","appreciate","appraise"],"explanation":"'Apprehend' here means to understand—not to arrest (the legal sense). 'Grasp the meaning' and 'densely…woven' show comprehension difficulty. 'Arrest' is the legal-sense trap (apprehend a suspect); 'appreciate' (value) and 'appraise' (evaluate) don't fit grasping an argument."},{"id":"vocab_032","entry":"allude","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To refer to something indirectly, without mentioning it explicitly.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'elude' (to evade).","passage":"Throughout her speech, the senator ______ to her opponent's recent scandal without ever naming it, trusting the audience to fill in the implication.","correctAnswer":"alluded","distractors":["eluded","aloud","allayed"],"explanation":"'Allude' means to hint at indirectly. 'Without ever naming it' and 'fill in the implication' signal indirect reference. 'Eluded' (evaded) is the commonly confused alternative—'the senator eluded her opponent's scandal' would mean she escaped it, not hinted at it. 'Aloud' (audibly) does not convey indirect reference; 'allayed' (soothed or reduced fears) is unrelated to making a passing reference."},{"id":"vocab_033","entry":"appraise","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To evaluate or assess the value, quality, or condition of something.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'apprise' (to inform).","passage":"Before approving the loan, the bank sent a specialist to ______ the property, examining its foundation, roof, and recent sales of comparable homes in the neighborhood.","correctAnswer":"appraise","distractors":["apprise","praise","arouse"],"explanation":"'Appraise' means to assess value. 'Examining its foundation, roof, and…sales' shows evaluation. 'Apprise' (to inform) is the common confusion—'appraise the property' is an assessment, not a notification. 'Praise' (commend) and 'arouse' (stir) don't fit the inspection context."},{"id":"vocab_034","entry":"censure","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To express strong disapproval of, often in a formal or official manner.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'censor' (to remove offensive content).","passage":"After the senator was found to have diverted campaign funds for personal use, the ethics committee voted to ______ him formally—a public reprimand that ended his leadership ambitions.","correctAnswer":"censure","distractors":["censor","censer","center"],"explanation":"'Censure' means to formally rebuke. 'Public reprimand' confirms a verbal condemnation. 'Censor' (to remove or suppress content) is the common confusion—censoring the senator would mean deleting his words, not rebuking him. 'Censer' (incense holder) and 'center' are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_035","entry":"eminent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Respected and distinguished; prominent and famous in a field.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'imminent' (about to happen).","passage":"The defense called an ______ cardiologist from the university hospital, a researcher whose textbook on arrhythmias was used in medical schools across three continents.","correctAnswer":"eminent","distractors":["imminent","immanent","emigrant"],"explanation":"'Eminent' means distinguished or respected. 'Used in medical schools across three continents' signals prominence. 'Imminent' (about to occur) is the common confusion—'an imminent cardiologist' is meaningless. 'Immanent' (inherent) and 'emigrant' (one who leaves) are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_036","entry":"exacerbate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To make a problem, conflict, or situation worse or more severe.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'exasperate' (to irritate a person).","passage":"The abrupt tariff did not ease the trade imbalance; instead, it ______ the dispute, prompting retaliatory tariffs from three trading partners within a week.","correctAnswer":"exacerbated","distractors":["exasperated","exaggerated","exhausted"],"explanation":"'Exacerbate' means to worsen a situation. 'Retaliatory tariffs from three trading partners' shows the dispute grew worse. 'Exasperated' (irritated a person) is the common confusion—'exasperated the dispute' would mean annoying the dispute itself, which is illogical. 'Exaggerated' (overstated) and 'exhausted' (drained) don't fit worsening a conflict."},{"id":"vocab_037","entry":"ingenious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Clever, original, and inventive in design or solution.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'ingenuous' (naïve, unsophisticated).","passage":"The engineer's ______ solution routed cooling water through a series of beryllium coils, a design so simple and elegant that competitors wondered why no one had thought of it before.","correctAnswer":"ingenious","distractors":["ingenuous","genuine","genius"],"explanation":"'Ingenious' means cleverly inventive. 'Simple and elegant' and 'no one had thought of it before' signal original design. 'Ingenuous' (naïve) is the common confusion—calling an engineering solution 'naïve' would insult the engineer. 'Genuine' (authentic) and 'genius' (noun) don't fit the adjective slot."},{"id":"vocab_038","entry":"precede","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To come before in time, order, or position.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'proceed' (to go forward).","passage":"A short overture will ______ the main performance, giving the audience a preview of the musical themes that the composer develops at length in the later acts.","correctAnswer":"precede","distractors":["proceed","proceed-from","precipitate"],"explanation":"'Precede' means to come before. 'A preview of the musical themes…in the later acts' shows the overture comes first. 'Proceed' (to go forward) is the common confusion—'proceed the main performance' is grammatically wrong (proceed is intransitive here). 'Precipitate' (to cause suddenly) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_039","entry":"corroborate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To confirm or support with additional evidence; to make certain.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'collaborate' (to work together).","passage":"After trying for weeks to contact the original source, the reporter was finally able to ______ the story with three independent witnesses who described the same events in the same order.","correctAnswer":"corroborate","distractors":["collaborate","corrupt","contradict"],"explanation":"'Corroborate' means to confirm with evidence. 'Three independent witnesses' and 'same events in the same order' show confirmation. 'Collaborate' (to work together) is the common confusion—'collaborate the story' is grammatically wrong. 'Corrupt' (debase) is opposite; 'contradict' (oppose) is opposite."},{"id":"vocab_040","entry":"elicit","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To draw out a response, reaction, or information from someone.","secondaryMeaning":"Often confused with 'illicit' (illegal).","passage":"The teacher's deceptively simple question—'What makes a law just?'—managed to ______ a richer discussion than any of the carefully prepared prompts on the syllabus.","correctAnswer":"elicit","distractors":["illicit","delete","elegit"],"explanation":"'Elicit' means to draw out. 'A richer discussion' is the response drawn out. 'Illicit' (illegal) is the common confusion—'illicit a richer discussion' is meaningless. 'Delete' (erase) is opposite; 'elegit' is not a word."},{"id":"vocab_041","entry":"for instance","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce a specific example that illustrates the preceding general statement.","secondaryMeaning":"Interchangeable with 'for example'; one of the most frequently tested continuation transitions.","passage":"Many nineteenth-century reformers opposed the consumption of alcohol on moral grounds. ______, the temperance advocate Carry Nation famously smashed saloons with a hatchet she called 'Hatchetation.'","correctAnswer":"for instance","distractors":["however","consequently","in contrast"],"explanation":"'For instance' signals that an example follows. The Carry Nation example illustrates 'reformers opposed alcohol.' 'However,' 'consequently,' and 'in contrast' are all reversal or causal words that would contradict the example relationship."},{"id":"vocab_042","entry":"in fact","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to emphasize or strengthen a preceding statement, often by confirming it.","secondaryMeaning":"One of the most frequently tested continuation/emphasis transitions.","passage":"The new bridge was not only completed on schedule; ______, it came in under budget, a rarity for public works of that scale.","correctAnswer":"in fact","distractors":["nevertheless","alternatively","otherwise"],"explanation":"'In fact' adds emphasis to the preceding positive claim. 'Under budget' strengthens 'completed on schedule.' 'Nevertheless' (reversal) contradicts the positive news; 'alternatively' (another option) and 'otherwise' (a different condition) don't fit the emphatic continuation."},{"id":"vocab_043","entry":"moreover","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to add a further and often stronger point in support of the same claim.","secondaryMeaning":"A continuation word that introduces additional evidence of the same kind.","passage":"The proposed site is far from existing transit lines. ______, environmental reviews have identified three endangered species within the survey area, complicating any timeline for construction.","correctAnswer":"moreover","distractors":["conversely","hence","instead"],"explanation":"'Moreover' adds a second, stronger point supporting the same conclusion (the site is problematic). 'Far from transit' and 'endangered species' both argue against the site. 'Conversely' (reversal), 'hence' (cause-effect), and 'instead' (substitution) don't fit adding a parallel negative point."},{"id":"vocab_044","entry":"subsequently","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","History Passages"],"definition":"Afterward; following a preceding action or event in time or order.","secondaryMeaning":"A continuation word indicating logical sequence; substitutable with 'next' or 'then.'","passage":"Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat on December 1, 1955. ______, her action inspired the year-long Montgomery bus boycott that helped launch the modern civil rights movement.","correctAnswer":"subsequently","distractors":["previously","instead","conversely"],"explanation":"'Subsequently' signals that the boycott came after Parks's refusal. The chronological order (Dec 1 refusal → boycott) confirms sequence. 'Previously' reverses the order; 'instead' (substitution) and 'conversely' (reversal) don't fit the temporal flow."},{"id":"vocab_045","entry":"however","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce a statement that contrasts with or qualifies the preceding one.","secondaryMeaning":"The most frequently tested reversal word; about two-thirds of reversal questions.","passage":"Aristocratic women of the Homeric period led lives of relative independence. ______, centuries later, women of the same class lived under severe constraints, rarely leaving the home without an escort.","correctAnswer":"however","distractors":["moreover","for example","similarly"],"explanation":"'However' signals contrast. 'Relative independence' vs. 'severe constraints' is a direct reversal. 'Moreover' (addition), 'for example' (illustration), and 'similarly' (comparison) all continue in the same direction, contradicting the contrast between the two periods."},{"id":"vocab_046","entry":"conversely","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce a statement that is the opposite of, or reverses, the preceding one.","secondaryMeaning":"A reversal word emphasizing a strict contrast, often between two parallel structures.","passage":"In the 1950s, many Americans chose to live in the new suburbs, prizing conformity and material comfort. ______, a small but influential group of Beats rejected the 'square' suburban life and settled in urban enclaves like Greenwich Village.","correctAnswer":"conversely","distractors":["likewise","accordingly","indeed"],"explanation":"'Conversely' introduces an opposing parallel. 'Suburban conformity' vs. 'Beat rejection of square life' is a strict contrast. 'Likewise' (similarly) would wrongly suggest the Beats conformed too; 'accordingly' (cause-effect) and 'indeed' (emphasis) don't fit the contrast."},{"id":"vocab_047","entry":"nevertheless","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In spite of what was just said; despite that.","secondaryMeaning":"Interchangeable with 'nonetheless' and 'notwithstanding'; a reversal word.","passage":"The expedition had lost three-quarters of its supplies and its captain to the crevasse. ______, the remaining members pressed on toward the pole, sustained by a stubborn sense of duty.","correctAnswer":"nevertheless","distractors":["therefore","similarly","for example"],"explanation":"'Nevertheless' concedes the loss but introduces that the party continued anyway. 'Pressed on' despite 'lost three-quarters of supplies' is a concession-continuation. 'Therefore' (cause-effect) would require the loss to cause the continuation; 'similarly' (comparison) and 'for example' (illustration) don't fit."},{"id":"vocab_048","entry":"consequently","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"As a result; therefore.","secondaryMeaning":"A cause-and-effect word; substitutable with 'as a result.'","passage":"Steve Jobs demanded exacting standards and paid painstaking attention to detail; he had little tolerance for mediocre employees or inferior products. ______, he was known to dismiss engineers on the spot for shipping code he deemed sloppy.","correctAnswer":"consequently","distractors":["however","for instance","alternatively"],"explanation":"'Consequently' signals that the dismissals resulted from Jobs's exacting standards. 'Had little tolerance' causes 'dismiss engineers on the spot.' 'However' (reversal) contradicts the causal link; 'for instance' (example) would frame dismissals as illustrations, not consequences; 'alternatively' (option) doesn't fit."},{"id":"vocab_049","entry":"accordingly","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In a manner appropriate to the circumstances; therefore, as a result.","secondaryMeaning":"A cause-and-effect word emphasizing appropriateness of the response.","passage":"The ruler of Ch'in unified China in 221 B.C.E. and proclaimed himself 'First Emperor.' ______, he standardized weights, measures, and the written script across the newly consolidated empire.","correctAnswer":"accordingly","distractors":["meanwhile","otherwise","conversely"],"explanation":"'Accordingly' signals an action appropriate to the circumstance. Standardizing weights and script was the logical consequence of unification. 'Meanwhile' (simultaneous) ignores causation; 'otherwise' (a different condition) and 'conversely' (contrast) don't fit the consequential relationship."},{"id":"vocab_050","entry":"account for","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"phrasal verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To explain the cause of; to give a reason for.","secondaryMeaning":"To constitute or make up a portion of (e.g., 'immigrants account for 12% of the workforce').","passage":"Historians still struggle to ______ the empire's sudden economic collapse in the seventh century, since the usual suspects—invasion, famine, and plague—are all absent from the archaeological record of that period.","correctAnswer":"account for","distractors":["look into","turn over","account against"],"explanation":"'Account for' here means to explain. 'Struggle to explain' fits 'usual suspects…are all absent.' 'Look into' (investigate) is close but describes the act of researching, not explaining; 'turn over' (flip or hand over) is unrelated; 'account against' is not idiomatic."},{"id":"vocab_051","entry":"fortitude","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Strength of mind that enables one to endure pain or adversity with courage.","secondaryMeaning":"Firmness of character; resilience in the face of hardship.","passage":"Despite months of imprisonment and interrogation, the dissident maintained her ______, refusing to betray her colleagues even when threatened with the loss of her pension.","correctAnswer":"fortitude","distractors":["fickleness","timidity","gullibility"],"explanation":"Fortitude is mental resilience under hardship. 'Months of imprisonment' and 'refusing to betray' show endurance of adversity. Fickleness (instability), timidity (cowardice—she refused to betray, so she was not timid), and gullibility (being easily fooled) do not describe moral courage."},{"id":"vocab_052","entry":"diminutive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Extremely or unusually small in size.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a grammatical form denoting smallness (linguistic sense, e.g. piglet).","passage":"The field biologist attached a ______ radio transmitter, weighing less than a gram, to the back of the hummingbird before releasing it into the cloud forest.","correctAnswer":"diminutive","distractors":["colossal","cumbersome","ornate"],"explanation":"'Diminutive' means very small. 'Weighing less than a gram' confirms a tiny scale. Colossal (huge) is opposite; cumbersome (clumsy/heavy) contradicts a gram; ornate (decorated) is unrelated to size."},{"id":"vocab_053","entry":"trivial","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Of little importance or significance; trifling.","secondaryMeaning":"Relating to ordinary, everyday matters (etymological sense at the crossroads).","passage":"The auditor dismissed the discrepancy of a few cents as ______, focusing instead on the unexplained transfer of two million dollars to an offshore account.","correctAnswer":"trivial","distractors":["momentous","pertinent","lucrative"],"explanation":"Trivial means insignificant. 'A few cents' contrasted with 'two million dollars' shows the small sum is unimportant. Momentous (highly significant) is opposite; pertinent (relevant) would mean the auditor should care; lucrative (profitable) doesn't fit a discrepancy."},{"id":"vocab_054","entry":"exhort","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To strongly encourage or urge someone to do something.","secondaryMeaning":"To give an earnest warning or advice (archaic/literal sense).","passage":"In his farewell address, the outgoing president ______ his successors to defend the constitution against the slow erosion of civil liberties.","correctAnswer":"exhorted","distractors":["dissuaded","refuted","deferred"],"explanation":"Exhort means to urge earnestly. 'Defend the constitution' is the action urged; the imperative mood implies strong encouragement. Dissuaded (talked out of) is opposite; refuted (disproved) and deferred (postponed) don't fit urging a duty."},{"id":"vocab_055","entry":"antipathy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A strong feeling of dislike or hostility toward someone or something.","secondaryMeaning":"An object of aversion; deep-seated enmity.","passage":"The two novelists, once close friends, had developed such a mutual ______ that they refused to attend the same literary festivals, scheduling their readings on opposite coasts.","correctAnswer":"antipathy","distractors":["affinity","apathy","empathy"],"explanation":"Antipathy is strong dislike. 'Refused to attend the same festivals' signals hostility. Affinity (liking) is opposite; apathy (indifference) lacks the active dislike shown; empathy (understanding others' feelings) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_056","entry":"tenacious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Holding firmly; showing great determination and persistence.","secondaryMeaning":"Tending to adhere or cling (literal sense, as a tenacious vine).","passage":"The defense lawyer was ______ in her cross-examination, returning to the same discrepancy in the witness's timeline across three separate days of testimony.","correctAnswer":"tenacious","distractors":["yielding","frivolous","dilatory"],"explanation":"Tenacious means persistently holding on. 'Returning to the same discrepancy…across three days' shows determined grip. Yielding (giving way) is opposite; frivolous (silly) and dilatory (delaying) don't fit determined pursuit of an answer."},{"id":"vocab_057","entry":"indulgent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Excessively tolerant or generous; allowing too much leniency.","secondaryMeaning":"Given to gratifying one's own desires (self-indulgent sense).","passage":"The professor's ______ grading policy—awarding partial credit for unanswered questions—left his students unprepared for the standardized exam that admitted no such mercy.","correctAnswer":"indulgent","distractors":["exacting","pragmatic","cynical"],"explanation":"Indulgent means overly lenient. 'Partial credit for unanswered questions' shows excess tolerance. Exacting (strict) is opposite; pragmatic (practical) and cynical (distrustful) don't describe grading leniency."},{"id":"vocab_058","entry":"polarize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To divide into two sharply contrasting groups or opposing factions.","secondaryMeaning":"To cause light waves to vibrate in a single plane (physics sense).","passage":"The new tax policy ______ the legislature, with rural members vowing to block every subsequent bill and urban members organizing an overnight filibuster to force a vote.","correctAnswer":"polarized","distractors":["unified","mystified","vindicated"],"explanation":"Polarize means to split into opposing camps. 'Rural members vowing to block' vs. 'urban members organizing a filibuster' shows two hostile factions. Unified (joined) is opposite; mystified (confused) and vindicated (cleared) don't fit splitting into camps."},{"id":"vocab_059","entry":"nebulous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Vague, hazy, or ill-defined; lacking clear form or boundaries.","secondaryMeaning":"Cloudy or misty (literal astronomical/meteorological sense).","passage":"The committee's recommendations were so ______ that two departments interpreted them as licensing opposite courses of action.","correctAnswer":"nebulous","distractors":["precise","covert","tangible"],"explanation":"Nebulous means hazy or unclear. 'Two departments interpreted them as licensing opposite courses' shows the recommendations lacked clarity. Precise (exact) is opposite; covert (secret) and tangible (touchable) don't fit vagueness of meaning."},{"id":"vocab_060","entry":"analogy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A comparison between two otherwise unlike things based on a shared structural relationship.","secondaryMeaning":"A correspondence in function or form between things of different kinds.","passage":"The physicist drew an ______ between the behavior of heat spreading through a metal sheet and the way ripples propagate across a pond, using it to make the diffusion equation intuitive.","correctAnswer":"analogy","distractors":["anomaly","anecdote","analogue"],"explanation":"An analogy is a comparison revealing a shared relationship. 'Heat spreading…like ripples' is a structural comparison. Anomaly (irregularity) and anecdote (brief story) don't compare; analogue (a thing that parallels) is a near-synonym but as a noun of the act of comparing, analogy is the precise choice."},{"id":"vocab_061","entry":"ephemeral","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Lasting for a very short time; transient and fleeting.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a plant that completes its life cycle in one day (botanical sense).","passage":"The phenomenon of the morning mist over the rice paddies was famously ______, vanishing before the photographers could reach the ridge that overlooked the valley.","correctAnswer":"ephemeral","distractors":["perennial","luminous","opaque"],"explanation":"Ephemeral means short-lived. 'Vanishing before the photographers could reach the ridge' shows brevity. Perennial (lasting year after year) is opposite; luminous (glowing) and opaque (blocking light) are unrelated to duration."},{"id":"vocab_062","entry":"penchant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A strong liking or habitual preference for something.","secondaryMeaning":"An inclination or bent toward a particular activity or taste.","passage":"The collector's ______ for first editions of neglected women novelists had made her library a destination for scholars who could find those volumes nowhere else.","correctAnswer":"penchant","distractors":["aversion","indifference","mandate"],"explanation":"Penchant is a strong liking. 'Made her library a destination' shows active devotion to a taste. Aversion (dislike) is opposite; indifference (no interest) and mandate (an order) don't fit a personal taste."},{"id":"vocab_063","entry":"boorish","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Rude, vulgar, and ill-mannered; lacking refinement.","secondaryMeaning":"Resembling a boor (peasant) in coarseness (etymological sense).","passage":"The guest's ______ behavior—interrupting the host, chewing with his mouth open, and insulting the chef—ended the dinner party within the first course.","correctAnswer":"boorish","distractors":["courteous","timid","frugal"],"explanation":"Boorish means crude and rude. 'Interrupting, chewing with mouth open, insulting the chef' are vulgar manners. Courteous (polite) is opposite; timid (shy) and frugal (thrifty) don't describe rude behavior."},{"id":"vocab_064","entry":"indignant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Feeling or showing anger at something perceived as unjust or unfair.","secondaryMeaning":"Righteous anger at an affront to dignity or rights.","passage":"When the referee disallowed a clearly legal goal, the home crowd grew ______, raining boos down on the pitch for the remaining minutes of the half.","correctAnswer":"indignant","distractors":["indifferent","apprehensive","elated"],"explanation":"Indignant means angered by injustice. 'Raining boos' shows anger at the unfair call. Indifferent (uncaring) is opposite; apprehensive (anxious) and elated (joyful) don't fit the crowd's hostile reaction."},{"id":"vocab_065","entry":"innuendo","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"An indirect or subtle remark that hints at something disparaging.","secondaryMeaning":"An insinuation; a veiled suggestion of something damaging.","passage":"Rather than accuse the rival directly, the journalist relied on ______, noting that the councilman's recent renovation had coincided with the zoning vote without ever stating a connection.","correctAnswer":"innuendo","distractors":["decree","parable","accolade"],"explanation":"Innuendo is an indirect insinuation. 'Without ever stating a connection' shows a veiled hint. Decree (an order) is direct, not veiled; parable (a moral story) is unrelated; accolade (praise) is opposite in intent."},{"id":"vocab_066","entry":"thwart","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To prevent the success of; to oppose and frustrate a plan.","secondaryMeaning":"To sit across from (archaic nautical sense, thwart of a boat).","passage":"Every attempt by the colony to establish its own mint was ______ by London, which feared that local coinage would erode imperial control over trade.","correctAnswer":"thwarted","distractors":["abetted","expedited","overlooked"],"explanation":"Thwart means to oppose and frustrate. 'Feared that local coinage would erode control' gives London's motive to block the plan. Abetted (assisted) is opposite; expedited (sped up) is opposite; overlooked (ignored) would mean no opposition occurred."},{"id":"vocab_067","entry":"adroit","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Clever and skillful, especially in using the hands or in handling situations.","secondaryMeaning":"Dexterous; nimble (literal physical sense).","passage":"The ambassador's ______ handling of the border dispute—conceding a minor customs point while securing the disputed river crossing—won praise from both delegations.","correctAnswer":"adroit","distractors":["clumsy","dogmatic","oblivious"],"explanation":"Adroit means skillful in handling. 'Conceding a minor point while securing the main objective' shows diplomatic skill. Clumsy (awkward) is opposite; dogmatic (rigid) and oblivious (unaware) don't fit deft negotiation."},{"id":"vocab_068","entry":"admonish","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To warn or reprove someone firmly but gently about a fault or danger.","secondaryMeaning":"To urge or exhort (older sense, now rare).","passage":"The judge ______ the witness about the penalties for perjury before allowing her to resume her testimony.","correctAnswer":"admonished","distractors":["interrogated","pardoned","silenced"],"explanation":"Admonish means to warn firmly. 'Penalties for perjury' is the warning's content. Interrogated (questioned) is different; pardoned (forgiven) is opposite; silenced (muted) doesn't fit a warning given so testimony can resume."},{"id":"vocab_069","entry":"incontrovertible","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Impossible to dispute or deny; definitively true.","secondaryMeaning":"Not able to be refuted by argument or evidence.","passage":"The security footage provided ______ evidence that the cashier had not, as the prosecution claimed, been at the scene of the robbery.","correctAnswer":"incontrovertible","distractors":["circumstantial","ambiguous","controversial"],"explanation":"Incontrovertible means undeniable. The footage definitively cleared the cashier. Circumstantial (indirect) and ambiguous (unclear) weaken proof; controversial (disputed) contradicts undeniable."},{"id":"vocab_070","entry":"voracious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Having a huge, insatiable appetite; eager for something in great quantities.","secondaryMeaning":"Greedy or ravenous in consumption (literal feeding sense).","passage":"As a child, the future historian was a ______ reader, finishing several thick volumes a week and exhausting the local library's collection on the Civil War by age twelve.","correctAnswer":"voracious","distractors":["indifferent","cursory","leisurely"],"explanation":"Voracious means insatiably hungry for something. 'Several thick volumes a week' and 'exhausting the collection' show an enormous appetite for reading. Indifferent (uncaring), cursory (superficial), and leisurely (unhurried) all contradict the devouring pace."},{"id":"vocab_071","entry":"callous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Emotionally hardened and insensitive; showing no concern for others' suffering.","secondaryMeaning":"Hardened or thickened (literal sense of skin).","passage":"The landlord's ______ reply to the elderly tenant's plea—'I don't care if it's January; the rent is due'—shocked even the building's longtime superintendent.","correctAnswer":"callous","distractors":["compassionate","timorous","frivolous"],"explanation":"Callous means insensitive to suffering. 'I don't care if it's January' shows emotional hardness. Compassionate (sympathetic) is opposite; timorous (fearful) and frivolous (silly) don't fit a hard-hearted reply."},{"id":"vocab_072","entry":"intrepid","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Fearless and resolute in the face of danger; boldly adventurous.","secondaryMeaning":"Unwavering courage in perilous circumstances.","passage":"The ______ explorer spent a decade mapping the interior of Borneo, crossing rapids that had turned back two earlier expeditions and returning with notebooks full of new species.","correctAnswer":"intrepid","distractors":["fainthearted","vain","remorseful"],"explanation":"Intrepid means fearlessly adventurous. 'Crossing rapids that had turned back two earlier expeditions' shows courage under danger. Fainthearted (timid) is opposite; vain (conceited) and remorseful (regretful) are unrelated to courage."},{"id":"vocab_073","entry":"nonchalant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Appearing calm and unconcerned; casually indifferent.","secondaryMeaning":"Coolly unconcerned, often to the point of seeming untroubled.","passage":"Despite having just won the prize that would fund her research for a decade, the chemist gave a ______ shrug and returned to the bench to finish the day's titrations.","correctAnswer":"nonchalant","distractors":["frantic","pretentious","vehement"],"explanation":"Nonchalant means casually unconcerned. 'Shrug and returned to the bench' shows indifference to a great honor. Frantic (wildly anxious) is opposite; pretentious (showy) and vehement (passionate) don't fit a calm shrug."},{"id":"vocab_074","entry":"convoluted","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Extremely complex and difficult to follow; intricately involved.","secondaryMeaning":"Twisted or coiled together (literal sense).","passage":"The instruction manual was so ______ that customers phoned support to ask how to turn the device on, missing the single sentence—buried on page forty—that explained it.","correctAnswer":"convoluted","distractors":["straightforward","succinct","inert"],"explanation":"Convoluted means tangled and hard to follow. 'Buried on page forty' shows the manual's complexity. Straightforward (clear) and succinct (concise) are opposites; inert (inactive) is unrelated to clarity."},{"id":"vocab_075","entry":"itinerant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Traveling from place to place, especially for work; not settled.","secondaryMeaning":"Working at a series of jobs for short periods (occupational sense).","passage":"Before settling in Chicago, the young carpenter was ______ for years, following the railroad west as each new line pushed the frontier of construction work.","correctAnswer":"itinerant","distractors":["sedentary","meticulous","haughty"],"explanation":"Itinerant means moving from place to place. 'Following the railroad west' shows travel for work. Sedentary (settled) is opposite; meticulous (careful) and haughty (arrogant) don't describe travel."},{"id":"vocab_076","entry":"poignant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Deeply moving; evoking a keen sense of sadness or tenderness.","secondaryMeaning":"Pleasantly pungent to the smell or taste (literal, archaic sense).","passage":"The most ______ moment of the documentary came when the elderly survivor, asked to describe his liberator, could only press his hand to his throat and shake his head.","correctAnswer":"poignant","distractors":["frivolous","tedious","bombastic"],"explanation":"Poignant means emotionally moving. 'Press his hand to his throat and shake his head' conveys deep, inexpressible emotion. Frivolous (silly), tedious (boring), and bombastic (pompous) all contradict a moving moment."},{"id":"vocab_077","entry":"impetus","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A driving force or stimulus that causes or increases activity.","secondaryMeaning":"The sudden force that sets a body in motion (literal physics sense).","passage":"The drought of 1934 provided the ______ for the new soil-conservation laws, which might otherwise have taken another decade to pass a reluctant Congress.","correctAnswer":"impetus","distractors":["hindrance","aftermath","pretext"],"explanation":"Impetus is a driving stimulus. 'The drought of 1934 provided [the stimulus] for the new laws' shows cause-and-effect. Hindrance (obstacle) is opposite; aftermath (result) reverses the causal direction; pretext (excuse) is unrelated to a genuine cause."},{"id":"vocab_078","entry":"bucolic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Relating to the charm of rural, countryside life; pastoral and idyllic.","secondaryMeaning":"Relating to shepherds or herdsmen (etymological sense).","passage":"The painter's ______ scenes of hillside farms and grazing cattle sold briskly to city collectors who had never themselves walked a country lane.","correctAnswer":"bucolic","distractors":["urban","grim","pedantic"],"explanation":"Bucolic means rural and idyllic. 'Hillside farms and grazing cattle' are pastoral images. Urban (city) is opposite; grim (gloomy) and pedantic (academic) don't fit pastoral charm."},{"id":"vocab_079","entry":"equanimity","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Calmness and composure, especially under strain; evenness of temper.","secondaryMeaning":"Mental calmness and emotional stability.","passage":"The conductor's ______ was remarkable: when the soprano collapsed mid-aria, he lowered his baton, signaled the understudy, and resumed the performance without a word.","correctAnswer":"equanimity","distractors":["agitation","prejudice","ostentation"],"explanation":"Equanimity is composed calm. 'Resumed without a word' after a crisis shows composure. Agitation (disturbance) is opposite; prejudice (bias) and ostentation (showiness) don't fit calm poise."},{"id":"vocab_080","entry":"panache","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Flamboyant confidence and flair; stylish vigor, especially in performance.","secondaryMeaning":"An ornamental plume on a helmet (literal, archaic sense).","passage":"The violinist played the cadenza with such ______ that even the seasoned critics, who had heard the concerto a hundred times, leaned forward in their seats.","correctAnswer":"panache","distractors":["timidity","monotony","restraint"],"explanation":"Panache is flamboyant flair. 'Even seasoned critics leaned forward' shows the performance's striking vigor. Timidity (shyness), monotony (sameness), and restraint (holding back) all contradict flamboyant display."},{"id":"vocab_081","entry":"provocative","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Tending to provoke discussion, controversy, or a strong reaction.","secondaryMeaning":"Arousing sexual desire (narrower, non-academic sense).","passage":"The historian's ______ thesis—that the revolution was driven less by ideals than by the price of bread—drew both standing ovations and formal reprimands within the same week.","correctAnswer":"provocative","distractors":["banal","conciliatory","obscure"],"explanation":"Provocative means stimulating controversy. 'Standing ovations and formal reprimands' show the thesis provoked strong reactions. Banal (unoriginal) is opposite; conciliatory (soothing) would avoid controversy; obscure (unclear) doesn't fit a thesis that clearly divided opinion."},{"id":"vocab_082","entry":"placid","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Calm and peaceful; undisturbed by turmoil or agitation.","secondaryMeaning":"Serene; of a person, even-tempered.","passage":"The lake's ______ surface reflected the pines so perfectly that a visiting painter mistook the inverted landscape for the real one and stretched his canvas upside down.","correctAnswer":"placid","distractors":["turbulent","murky","barren"],"explanation":"Placid means calm and still. 'Reflected the pines so perfectly' shows a still surface. Turbulent (rough/agitated) is opposite; murky (cloudy) and barren (empty) don't describe stillness."},{"id":"vocab_083","entry":"fortuitous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Happening by lucky chance; fortunate and accidental.","secondaryMeaning":"Occurring by chance, without implying good or bad luck (precise sense).","passage":"The discovery of penicillin was famously ______: Fleming returned from vacation to find that a mold contaminating his staphylococcus cultures had killed the bacteria around it.","correctAnswer":"fortuitous","distractors":["inevitable","calamitous","calculated"],"explanation":"Fortuitous means lucky and accidental. 'A mold contaminating his cultures' was a chance event with a fortunate outcome. Inevitable (certain) is opposite; calamitous (disastrous) is opposite; calculated (deliberate) contradicts accident."},{"id":"vocab_084","entry":"dispel","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To drive away or disperse; to cause a feeling, idea, or belief to vanish.","secondaryMeaning":"To scatter in different directions (literal sense).","passage":"A single successful mission was not enough to ______ the rumors that the agency had falsified its earlier test results, and the program was suspended pending a full audit.","correctAnswer":"dispel","distractors":["confirm","spread","conceal"],"explanation":"Dispel means to drive away (a belief). 'Not enough to [drive away] the rumors' fits making doubts vanish. Confirm (verify) and spread (circulate) are opposites; conceal (hide) doesn't fit dispelling, which requires public dismissal."},{"id":"vocab_085","entry":"amalgam","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A mixture or blend of different elements, often producing something new.","secondaryMeaning":"An alloy of mercury with another metal (literal chemical sense).","passage":"The movement's philosophy was an ______ of European socialism and indigenous communal traditions, a combination no theorist had previously attempted.","correctAnswer":"amalgam","distractors":["antithesis","monolith","anomaly"],"explanation":"Amalgam is a blend of elements. 'European socialism and indigenous communal traditions' are the two blended ingredients. Antithesis (opposite) means contrast, not blend; monolith (single block) is opposite of a mixture; anomaly (irregularity) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_086","entry":"viable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Capable of working successfully; feasible and practical.","secondaryMeaning":"Capable of living and developing (literal biological sense).","passage":"After three prototypes failed under pressure, the engineers doubted whether the new hull material would prove ______ in deep-water conditions.","correctAnswer":"viable","distractors":["fragile","arbitrary","obsolete"],"explanation":"Viable means workable/feasible. 'Failed under pressure' raises doubt whether the hull can work. Fragile (breakable) describes weakness, not the practicality being tested; arbitrary (random) and obsolete (outdated) don't fit whether something can function."},{"id":"vocab_087","entry":"anguish","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Severe physical or mental pain; agony and torment.","secondaryMeaning":"A feeling of deep suffering or distress.","passage":"The mother's ______ was audible across the waiting room when the surgeon appeared without his team and could not yet speak.","correctAnswer":"anguish","distractors":["composure","indifference","fascination"],"explanation":"Anguish is severe suffering. 'Audible across the waiting room' shows the depth of pain. Composure (calm) is opposite; indifference (uncaring) and fascination (intense interest) contradict a mother's torment at such a moment."},{"id":"vocab_088","entry":"intemperate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Lacking restraint or moderation; excessive in behavior or speech.","secondaryMeaning":"Given to excessive indulgence, especially in drinking (literal sense).","passage":"The mayor's ______ denunciation of the press, delivered at full volume from the steps of city hall, alienated even the allies who had agreed with the substance of his complaint.","correctAnswer":"intemperate","distractors":["measured","forgiving","obscure"],"explanation":"Intemperate means lacking restraint. 'At full volume from the steps' shows excess. Measured (restrained) is opposite; forgiving (merciful) doesn't fit a denunciation; obscure (unclear) is unrelated to lack of restraint."},{"id":"vocab_089","entry":"superficial","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Concerned only with the surface or obvious; lacking depth or thoroughness.","secondaryMeaning":"On or near the surface (literal physical sense).","passage":"The reviewer's ______ treatment of the book mentioned the cover design and the author's biography but devoted no sentence to the argument inside.","correctAnswer":"superficial","distractors":["comprehensive","profound","impartial"],"explanation":"Superficial means shallow. 'Cover design and biography' but nothing on 'the argument inside' shows surface-only treatment. Comprehensive (complete) and profound (deep) are opposites; impartial (fair) is unrelated to depth."},{"id":"vocab_090","entry":"laud","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To praise highly; to extol or commend.","secondaryMeaning":"To glorify, often in formal or public speech.","passage":"In his final address, the dean ______ the retiring professor as the architect of the modern classics department, crediting her with three decades of revival.","correctAnswer":"laud","distractors":["denounce","ignore","scrutinize"],"explanation":"Laud means to praise. 'Architect of the modern classics department' and 'crediting her' are praise. Denounce (condemn) is opposite; ignore (disregard) and scrutinize (examine closely) don't fit public praise."},{"id":"vocab_091","entry":"dismissive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Showing deliberate indifference or disregard; rejecting something as unworthy of attention.","secondaryMeaning":"Peremptorily curt; offhand.","passage":"Her ______ wave of the hand silenced the intern's suggestion before he could finish the sentence, and the meeting moved on without a word of discussion.","correctAnswer":"dismissive","distractors":["attentive","apprehensive","lenient"],"explanation":"Dismissive means showing disregard. 'Silenced the intern before he could finish' shows rejection as unworthy. Attentive (focused) is opposite; apprehensive (anxious) and lenient (tolerant) don't fit curt rejection."},{"id":"vocab_092","entry":"disparage","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To speak of in a way that belittles; to lower in rank or reputation.","secondaryMeaning":"To degrade in value or reputation by comparison.","passage":"The critic took every opportunity to ______ the young novelist, calling each successive book 'slight' and 'derivative' in the most widely read literary supplement.","correctAnswer":"disparage","distractors":["acclaim","promote","obscure"],"explanation":"Disparage means to belittle. 'Slight' and 'derivative' are belittling labels. Acclaim (praise) is opposite; promote (advance) is opposite; obscure (hide) is unrelated to verbal belittling."},{"id":"vocab_093","entry":"pompous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Affectedly grand or solemn; full of exaggerated self-importance.","secondaryMeaning":"Characterized by pomp or ostentatious display.","passage":"The new chairman was so ______ that he insisted on being addressed as 'Doctor' even by colleagues who had earned their degrees a decade before his.","correctAnswer":"pompous","distractors":["humble","frank","witty"],"explanation":"Pompous means self-important. 'Insisted on being addressed as Doctor' shows inflated self-regard. Humble (modest) is opposite; frank (blunt) and witty (amusing) don't fit affected grandeur."},{"id":"vocab_094","entry":"cryptic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Having a meaning that is hidden or puzzling; mysterious and obscure.","secondaryMeaning":"Pertaining to codes or ciphers (literal sense).","passage":"The professor's final words on the subject were ______: 'Begin where the river begins,' he said, and refused to elaborate even when the graduate students pressed him for a week.","correctAnswer":"cryptic","distractors":["explicit","factual","trite"],"explanation":"Cryptic means puzzling. 'Refused to elaborate' and the gnomic 'begin where the river begins' show obscure meaning. Explicit (clearly stated) is opposite; factual (literal) and trite (overused) don't fit mystery."},{"id":"vocab_095","entry":"subtle","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"So slight or delicate as to be difficult to detect or describe.","secondaryMeaning":"Crafty or cunning (older sense, now rare).","passage":"The difference between the two pigments was so ______ that only the conservator, who had spent forty years with the painter's work, could distinguish them under the gallery light.","correctAnswer":"subtle","distractors":["glaring","crude","tedious"],"explanation":"Subtle means hard to detect. 'Only the conservator…could distinguish them' shows the difference is barely perceptible. Glaring (obvious) is opposite; crude (unrefined) and tedious (boring) don't fit a fine distinction."},{"id":"vocab_096","entry":"disparity","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A noticeable difference or inequality between things being compared.","secondaryMeaning":"Lack of equality in condition, rank, or quantity.","passage":"The widening ______ between the wages of new graduates and those of senior staff became the central issue in the union's contract negotiations.","correctAnswer":"disparity","distractors":["uniformity","consensus","increment"],"explanation":"Disparity is inequality. 'Widening…between the wages' shows a growing gap. Uniformity (sameness) and consensus (agreement) are opposites; increment (a small increase) describes one side, not a gap between two."},{"id":"vocab_097","entry":"curtail","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To reduce or cut short; to impose limits on.","secondaryMeaning":"To shorten by cutting off a part.","passage":"Facing a budget shortfall, the dean was forced to ______ the library's journal subscriptions, eliminating seventy titles in a single fiscal year.","correctAnswer":"curtail","distractors":["expand","postpone","subsidize"],"explanation":"Curtail means to cut back. 'Eliminating seventy titles' is the reduction. Expand (enlarge) is opposite; postpone (delay) doesn't fit elimination; subsidize (fund) is opposite."},{"id":"vocab_098","entry":"innocuous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Not harmful or offensive; unlikely to provoke a strong reaction.","secondaryMeaning":"Harmless; not injurious to health (literal sense).","passage":"What seemed an ______ remark about the weather turned out to be a coded signal between two agents, a meaning invisible to anyone outside the network.","correctAnswer":"innocuous","distractors":["venomous","provocative","momentous"],"explanation":"Innocuous means harmless. 'Seemed an [harmless] remark' contrasts with 'coded signal' to show the deceptive appearance. Venomous (harmful) and provocative (stirring) are opposites of the surface meaning; momentous (significant) doesn't fit a remark about the weather."},{"id":"vocab_099","entry":"diatribe","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A bitter, abusive verbal attack; a thunderous denunciation.","secondaryMeaning":"A prolonged and vehement speech of criticism.","passage":"The senator's floor speech was less a reasoned critique than a ______, lasting forty minutes and cataloguing the personal failings of every member of the opposing committee.","correctAnswer":"diatribe","distractors":["eulogy","dialogue","parable"],"explanation":"Diatribe is a bitter verbal attack. 'Forty minutes' of 'personal failings' is a denunciation. Eulogy (praise of the dead) is opposite; dialogue (conversation) and parable (moral story) don't fit an attack."},{"id":"vocab_100","entry":"charlatan","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who falsely claims special knowledge or skill; a fraud or quack.","secondaryMeaning":"One who mounts a stage to sell fake remedies (etymological sense).","passage":"The traveling salesman who promised that his tincture cured every known ailment was unmasked as a ______ when three of his patients grew sicker rather than better.","correctAnswer":"charlatan","distractors":["prodigy","benefactor","skeptic"],"explanation":"Charlatan is a fraud. 'Promised that his tincture cured every ailment' and patients 'grew sicker' expose the fraud. Prodigy (a genius) is opposite; benefactor (a giver) and skeptic (a doubter) don't fit a fraud selling fake cures."},{"id":"vocab_101","entry":"skeptic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A person who doubts accepted opinions and asks for evidence before believing.","secondaryMeaning":"A member of a school of ancient Greek philosophy (proper noun sense).","passage":"Even after the paper passed peer review, the department's leading ______ insisted on replicating the experiment himself before accepting its central claim.","correctAnswer":"skeptic","distractors":["convert","dupe","orator"],"explanation":"Skeptic is a doubter who demands evidence. 'Replicating the experiment himself before accepting' shows methodical doubt. Convert (one convinced) is opposite; dupe (one fooled) and orator (a speaker) don't fit a doubter."},{"id":"vocab_102","entry":"rhetorician","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person skilled in the art of using language effectively and persuasively.","secondaryMeaning":"A teacher of rhetoric (classical sense).","passage":"As a ______, the abolitionist knew precisely when to soften his voice and when to let it crack, holding the hall silent for the whole of his hour-long address.","correctAnswer":"rhetorician","distractors":["layperson","demagogue","misanthrope"],"explanation":"Rhetorician is a master of persuasive language. 'When to soften his voice…when to let it crack' show skilled use of language. Layperson (a non-expert) is opposite; demagogue (a manipulator) has a negative connotation the passage does not imply; misanthrope (a hater of people) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_103","entry":"ascetic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who practices strict self-denial, often for religious discipline.","secondaryMeaning":"Pertaining to a harshly simple way of life (adjective sense).","passage":"The monk was a true ______, owning only a wooden bowl and a single robe, and eating nothing but what the villagers placed in his hand each morning.","correctAnswer":"ascetic","distractors":["hedonist","sybarite","orator"],"explanation":"Ascetic practices self-denial. 'Only a wooden bowl and a single robe' shows renunciation. Hedonist (pleasure-seeker) and sybarite (luxury-lover) are opposites; orator (speaker) is unrelated to austerity."},{"id":"vocab_104","entry":"raconteur","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who tells stories and anecdotes with skill and wit.","secondaryMeaning":"A skilled and entertaining storyteller.","passage":"The old boatswain was the ship's ______, and the crew would gather on deck each night to hear him retell, with fresh embellishments, the same wreck he had survived forty years before.","correctAnswer":"raconteur","distractors":["misanthrope","charlatan","novice"],"explanation":"Raconteur is a skilled storyteller. 'Gather on deck each night to hear him retell' shows his storytelling drew an audience. Misanthrope (a hermit), charlatan (a fraud), and novice (a beginner) don't fit an entertaining teller of tales."},{"id":"vocab_105","entry":"iconoclast","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A person who attacks cherished beliefs, traditions, or institutions.","secondaryMeaning":"One who destroys religious images (literal, historical sense).","passage":"The art critic was an ______ who dismissed the canon of Renaissance painting as overrated, arguing that the period's real innovation lay in the forgotten work of women miniaturists.","correctAnswer":"iconoclast","distractors":["traditionalist","conformist","novice"],"explanation":"Iconoclast attacks cherished beliefs. 'Dismissed the canon…as overrated' and championing the 'forgotten' work shows overturning the established view. Traditionalist and conformist are opposites; novice (a beginner) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_106","entry":"partisan","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A strong, often biased supporter of a person, party, or cause.","secondaryMeaning":"A member of an armed resistance group (historical, WWII sense).","passage":"The newspaper's coverage was so openly ______ that readers cancelled their subscriptions in protest, complaining that the editor had abandoned any pretense of objectivity.","correctAnswer":"partisan","distractors":["impartial","apathetic","neutral"],"explanation":"Partisan is a biased supporter. 'Abandoned any pretense of objectivity' shows bias. Impartial and neutral are opposites; apathetic (uncaring) is the opposite of zealous bias."},{"id":"vocab_107","entry":"polymath","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person of wide-ranging knowledge across many fields.","secondaryMeaning":"A person whose expertise spans many disciplines.","passage":"Leonardo was the model of the Renaissance ______, painting masterpieces while also designing flying machines, dissecting cadavers, and annotating treatises on hydraulics.","correctAnswer":"polymath","distractors":["specialist","dilettante","recluse"],"explanation":"Polymath has wide-ranging expertise. 'Painting…designing flying machines…dissecting cadavers' spans disciplines. Specialist (narrow) is opposite; dilettante (a dabbler) is the superficial amateur contrasted with a true polymath; recluse (a hermit) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_108","entry":"mentor","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"An experienced and trusted advisor or guide.","secondaryMeaning":"A wise and faithful counselor (from the character Mentor in the Odyssey).","passage":"As her ______, the senior chemist reviewed every draft of the graduate student's dissertation, marking the margins with corrections that often ran longer than the text itself.","correctAnswer":"mentor","distractors":["antagonist","disciple","rival"],"explanation":"Mentor is an advisor. 'Reviewed every draft…marking the margins' shows a guiding role. Antagonist (an opponent) and rival (a competitor) are opposites; disciple (a follower) reverses the role."},{"id":"vocab_109","entry":"demagogue","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A political leader who appeals to popular fears and prejudices rather than reason.","secondaryMeaning":"A leader of the mob in ancient Greece (etymological sense).","passage":"Rather than explain the trade deficit, the senator relied on ______ tactics, blaming immigrants for the closing factories and promising that a single wall would restore the lost jobs.","correctAnswer":"demagogic","distractors":["diplomatic","meticulous","recondite"],"explanation":"Demagogic means appealing to fear and prejudice. 'Blaming immigrants…a single wall' appeals to popular resentment. Diplomatic (tactful), meticulous (careful), and recondite (obscure) don't fit mob-rousing tactics."},{"id":"vocab_110","entry":"automaton","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who acts mechanically and without thought; a self-operating machine.","secondaryMeaning":"A mechanical figure that mimics a living being (historical sense).","passage":"After thirty years stamping the same form, the clerk had become an ______, processing the paperwork without a glance and answering every question with the same rehearsed sentence.","correctAnswer":"automaton","distractors":["innovator","dissenter","visionary"],"explanation":"Automaton acts mechanically. 'Without a glance' and 'same rehearsed sentence' show thoughtless repetition. Innovator (a creator), dissenter (a protester), and visionary (a forward-thinker) all imply active mind, opposite of mechanical action."},{"id":"vocab_111","entry":"recluse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who lives a solitary, withdrawn life, avoiding society.","secondaryMeaning":"One who shuts themselves away from the world.","passage":"After her husband's death, the novelist became a ______, leaving her apartment only to collect packages left at the door and refusing interviews for the remaining decades of her life.","correctAnswer":"recluse","distractors":["socialite","orator","optimist"],"explanation":"Recluse lives in seclusion. 'Leaving her apartment only to collect packages' shows withdrawal. Socialite (a party-goer) is opposite; orator (a public speaker) and optimist (a hopeful person) don't fit seclusion."},{"id":"vocab_112","entry":"bungler","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who works clumsily or incompetently; one who botches tasks.","secondaryMeaning":"One who makes mistakes through incompetence rather than malice.","passage":"The contractor was a hopeless ______, installing the plumbing backward so that the cold tap ran hot and the hot tap ran cold, then charging double to undo the error.","correctAnswer":"bungler","distractors":["virtuoso","connoisseur","prodigy"],"explanation":"Bungler is an incompetent. 'Installing the plumbing backward' shows incompetence. Virtuoso (a master) and prodigy (a genius) are opposites; connoisseur (an expert judge) is unrelated to workmanship."},{"id":"vocab_113","entry":"clairvoyant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who claims to perceive events beyond ordinary sense; a seer.","secondaryMeaning":"Having the supposed power to see distant or future events.","passage":"The townspeople treated the old woman as a ______, consulting her before planting crops and before proposing marriage, though she herself insisted she merely remembered the weather of past seasons.","correctAnswer":"clairvoyant","distractors":["skeptic","empiricist","novice"],"explanation":"Clairvoyant claims supernatural sight. 'Consulting her before planting crops' shows the people believe she foresees. Skeptic (a doubter), empiricist (an evidence-based thinker), and novice (a beginner) don't fit a seer."},{"id":"vocab_114","entry":"prognosticator","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A person who makes predictions about the future, often from signs or data.","secondaryMeaning":"One who foretells, especially by inference from present information.","passage":"Despite his reputation as a ______ of market trends, the economist failed to predict the crash of 2008, leaving his clients to wonder whether his earlier calls had been luck.","correctAnswer":"prognosticator","distractors":["historian","accountant","cynic"],"explanation":"Prognosticator predicts the future. 'Failed to predict the crash' and 'his earlier calls' show forecasting work. Historian (studies the past), accountant (records finances), and cynic (a doubter) don't fit a forecaster."},{"id":"vocab_115","entry":"pundit","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"An expert commentator, especially one who offers opinions in the media.","secondaryMeaning":"A learned person; an authority (older sense).","passage":"Within hours of the speech, every television ______ had weighed in, and the consensus—delivered in nearly identical phrasing across three networks—was that the candidate had blundered.","correctAnswer":"pundit","distractors":["layperson","protagonist","recluse"],"explanation":"Pundit is a media commentator. 'Television' and 'weighed in' across networks shows media opinion-giving. Layperson (a non-expert) is opposite; protagonist (a lead character) and recluse (a hermit) don't fit a media commentator."},{"id":"vocab_116","entry":"zealot","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who is fanatically devoted to a cause, often to a fault.","secondaryMeaning":"A member of an ancient Jewish sect that resisted Roman rule (historical sense).","passage":"The new convert was a ______ for the diet, preaching its benefits to coworkers, family, and strangers on the train, and refusing to dine with anyone who ate otherwise.","correctAnswer":"zealot","distractors":["apostate","skeptic","cynic"],"explanation":"Zealot is a fanatic. 'Preaching…to coworkers, family, and strangers' shows fanatical devotion. Apostate (one who abandons a faith) is opposite; skeptic (a doubter) and cynic (a distruster) don't fit zealous advocacy."},{"id":"vocab_117","entry":"neophyte","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A beginner or novice, especially in a religious order or new field.","secondaryMeaning":"A recent convert; one newly introduced to a field.","passage":"Though she had been a neophyte only months before, the young diplomat handled the trade round with the assurance of a veteran, surprising the negotiators who had expected to roll over her.","correctAnswer":"neophyte","distractors":["veteran","apostate","expert"],"explanation":"Note: passage uses neophyte explicitly as contrast; the blank expects the noun that completes the contrast with 'veteran.' 'A neophyte only months before' vs. 'assurance of a veteran' shows a beginner. Veteran and expert are opposites; apostate (one who abandons) is unrelated. The correct answer is 'neophyte'; 'veteran' and 'expert' are the trap distractors (the passage's 'veteran' is a comparison point, not the blank)."},{"id":"vocab_118","entry":"benefactor","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who gives money or assistance to a person or cause; a patron.","secondaryMeaning":"One who provides financial support to an institution or individual.","passage":"The university's new laboratory was funded entirely by an anonymous ______, whose only condition was that the building bear the name of a janitor who had worked there for fifty years.","correctAnswer":"benefactor","distractors":["recipient","creditor","detractor"],"explanation":"Benefactor is a giver. 'Funded entirely by an anonymous [giver]' shows patronage. Recipient (a receiver) is opposite; creditor (one owed money) and detractor (a critic) don't fit a donor."},{"id":"vocab_119","entry":"dissembler","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who conceals true motives or feelings under a false appearance; a hypocrite.","secondaryMeaning":"One who hides intentions behind a feigned demeanor.","passage":"To the staff he was gracious and modest, but his private letters revealed a ______ who had spent decades scheming against the very colleagues he publicly praised.","correctAnswer":"dissembler","distractors":["straight-shooter","advocate","philanthropist"],"explanation":"Dissembler hides true motives. 'Gracious and modest' publicly vs. 'scheming against' in private shows concealment. Straight-shooter (an honest person) is opposite; advocate (a supporter) and philanthropist (a giver) don't fit a hypocrite."},{"id":"vocab_120","entry":"proponent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A person who argues in favor of something; an advocate.","secondaryMeaning":"One who supports a cause, theory, or proposal.","passage":"As a longtime ______ of nuclear power, the physicist found himself isolated when his colleagues, alarmed by the recent accident, switched to opposing the technology.","correctAnswer":"proponent","distractors":["opponent","detractor","bystander"],"explanation":"Proponent advocates for. 'Argues in favor' and colleagues 'switched to opposing' show his support. Opponent and detractor (a critic) are opposites; bystander (a watcher) doesn't fit active advocacy."},{"id":"vocab_121","entry":"prodigy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A young person with exceptional talent; an impressive or unusual example.","secondaryMeaning":"An omen or portent (archaic sense).","passage":"The fourteen-year-old was a mathematical ______, publishing a proof in a peer-reviewed journal before she had learned to drive a car.","correctAnswer":"prodigy","distractors":["dilettante","amateur","charlatan"],"explanation":"Prodigy is an exceptional young talent. 'Fourteen-year-old' with a peer-reviewed proof shows early genius. Dilettante (a dabbler) and amateur (a non-expert) are opposites of exceptional talent; charlatan (a fraud) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_122","entry":"oracle","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person or source giving wise or authoritative pronouncements; a prophecy.","secondaryMeaning":"A shrine where a god was believed to reveal the future (classical sense).","passage":"Before any major decision, the king consulted the ______ at Delphi, treating her pronouncements as the direct word of Apollo himself.","correctAnswer":"oracle","distractors":["skeptic","usurper","historian"],"explanation":"Oracle gives authoritative pronouncements. 'Treating her pronouncements as the direct word of Apollo' shows she speaks for the divine. Skeptic (a doubter), usurper (a throne-seizer), and historian (a chronicler) don't fit a divine speaker."},{"id":"vocab_123","entry":"misanthrope","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who dislikes and distrusts humankind; a hater of people.","secondaryMeaning":"One who avoids human society.","passage":"The poet had become such a ______ in his last years that he nailed his gate shut and paid a boy to leave the mail at the road rather than bring it to the door.","correctAnswer":"misanthrope","distractors":["philanthropist","optimist","extrovert"],"explanation":"Misanthrope hates people. 'Nailed his gate shut' shows avoidance of human contact. Philanthropist (a lover of humanity) and extrovert (an outgoing person) are opposites; optimist (a hopeful person) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_124","entry":"innovator","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A person who introduces new ideas, methods, or products.","secondaryMeaning":"One who pioneers a change in a field or practice.","passage":"As an ______ in software design, she insisted that interfaces should respond to a user's hand gestures years before touch screens became standard on consumer devices.","correctAnswer":"innovator","distractors":["imitator","preserver","reactionary"],"explanation":"Innovator introduces new methods. 'Years before touch screens became standard' shows she pioneered. Imitator (a copier) and preserver (a maintainer) are opposites; reactionary (one opposed to change) is opposite."},{"id":"vocab_125","entry":"sycophant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who flatters powerful people to gain favor; a fawning flatterer.","secondaryMeaning":"An informer; one who obtains favor by servile obsequiousness.","passage":"The prince was surrounded by ______ who laughed at his every jest, however flat, and assured him that his verse rivalled the best of the age.","correctAnswer":"sycophants","distractors":["critics","detractors","subordinates"],"explanation":"Sycophants flatter for favor. 'Laughed at his every jest, however flat' shows fawning. Critics (judges) and detractors (belittlers) are opposites; subordinates (juniors) may flatter but the defining trait is fawning, not rank."},{"id":"vocab_126","entry":"stoic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person who endures hardship without complaint; seemingly unaffected by emotion.","secondaryMeaning":"A follower of the ancient Greek philosophy of Stoicism (proper sense).","passage":"Throughout the long march, the wounded soldier remained a ______, refusing morphine and walking on his splinted leg so that a weaker comrade could ride in the cart.","correctAnswer":"stoic","distractors":["complainer","zealot","rhetorician"],"explanation":"Stoic endures without complaint. 'Refusing morphine' and walking on a splinted leg show uncomplaining endurance. Complainer (a whiner) is opposite; zealot (a fanatic) and rhetorician (a speaker) don't fit emotional restraint."},{"id":"vocab_127","entry":"reprobate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A person of thoroughly bad character; a morally unprincipled person.","secondaryMeaning":"One rejected by God; an unrepentant sinner (theological sense).","passage":"The village regarded the old squire as a ______, citing his gambling, his cruelty to tenants, and the rumours—never quite confirmed—of a second family in the next county.","correctAnswer":"reprobate","distractors":["paragon","novice","ascetic"],"explanation":"Reprobate is morally unprincipled. 'Gambling, cruelty…second family' show bad character. Paragon (a model) and ascetic (a self-denier) are opposites; novice (a beginner) is unrelated to character."},{"id":"vocab_128","entry":"renegade","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who deserts a party, cause, or allegiance; a traitor or rebel.","secondaryMeaning":"An outlaw; one who rejects a former loyalty.","passage":"Once a loyal minister, the general became a ______, leading a rebel army back across the border to seize the very capital he had spent a decade defending.","correctAnswer":"renegade","distractors":["loyalist","patriot","convert"],"explanation":"Renegade deserts a cause. 'Once a loyal minister' but now 'leading a rebel army' shows betrayal of former allegiance. Loyalist and patriot (a supporter of country) are opposites; convert (one who adopts a faith) doesn't imply armed betrayal."},{"id":"vocab_129","entry":"draconian","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Extremely harsh and severe, especially of laws or punishments.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to Draco, the Athenian lawgiver (etymological sense).","passage":"The new regime imposed ______ penalties for minor theft, sentencing a hungry teenager to ten years for stealing a loaf of bread.","correctAnswer":"draconian","distractors":["lenient","proportional","trivial"],"explanation":"Draconian means extremely harsh. 'Ten years for stealing a loaf of bread' shows disproportionate severity. Lenient (merciful) and proportional (measured) are opposites; trivial (unimportant) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_130","entry":"laconic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Using very few words; concise to the point of seeming blunt.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to Laconia (Sparta), whose people were known for brevity.","passage":"The general's laconic reply to the demand for surrender—'Come and take them'—entered the language as the epitome of military brevity.","correctAnswer":"laconic","distractors":["verbose","grandiose","ambiguous"],"explanation":"Laconic means using few words. 'Come and take them' is famously brief. Verbose (wordy) is opposite; grandiose (pompous) and ambiguous (unclear) don't fit a terse three-word reply."},{"id":"vocab_131","entry":"spartan","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Marked by simplicity, frugality, and avoidance of luxury; austere.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to Sparta (etymological sense).","passage":"The writer's apartment was deliberately ______: a table, a chair, a shelf of books, and nothing else, so that no possession could compete for attention with the work in progress.","correctAnswer":"spartan","distractors":["opulent","cluttered","ornate"],"explanation":"Spartan means austere and frugal. 'A table, a chair, a shelf of books, and nothing else' shows austere simplicity. Opulent (luxurious), cluttered (messy), and ornate (decorated) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_132","entry":"halcyon","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Calm, peaceful, and happy; idyllic, often of a remembered past.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to the halcyon (kingfisher), associated with calm days (mythological sense).","passage":"The memoirist returned often to the ______ summers of her childhood on the lake, contrasting those untroubled days with the turmoil that followed her father's bankruptcy.","correctAnswer":"halcyon","distractors":["turbulent","barren","frivolous"],"explanation":"Halcyon means calm and idyllic. 'Untroubled days' contrasts with later turmoil. Turbulent (agitated) is opposite; barren (empty) and frivolous (silly) don't fit a peaceful remembered time."},{"id":"vocab_133","entry":"sophistry","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A subtle but deliberately misleading argument; plausible but false reasoning.","secondaryMeaning":"Subtly deceptive reasoning designed to defeat an opponent without genuine truth.","passage":"The candidate's defense was pure ______: he conceded every point of the indictment while claiming that none of them, taken together, constituted an offense.","correctAnswer":"sophistry","distractors":["logic","evidence","candor"],"explanation":"Sophistry is misleading but plausible reasoning. 'Conceded every point while claiming none constituted an offense' is clever but dishonest argument. Logic (sound reasoning), evidence (proof), and candor (honesty) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_134","entry":"chimerical","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Wildly fanciful and unrealistic; existing only in the imagination.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to the chimera, a mythical monster (etymological sense).","passage":"The plan to drain the swamp by hand in a single summer struck the engineer as ______, however noble the volunteers' intentions.","correctAnswer":"chimerical","distractors":["pragmatic","inevitable","tangible"],"explanation":"Chimerical means fanciful and unreal. 'Drain the swamp by hand in a single summer' is an impractical fantasy. Pragmatic (practical) is opposite; inevitable (certain) and tangible (concrete) don't fit a fantasy."},{"id":"vocab_135","entry":"ostracize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To deliberately exclude someone from a group or society.","secondaryMeaning":"To banish by ostracism in ancient Greece (etymological sense).","passage":"After he testified against the union leadership, his former friends ______ him, refusing to sit with him in the cafeteria or to speak to him in the hallways.","correctAnswer":"ostracized","distractors":["welcomed","promoted","recruited"],"explanation":"Ostracize means to deliberately exclude. 'Refusing to sit with him or speak to him' shows social exclusion. Welcomed, promoted, and recruited are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_136","entry":"impecunious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Having very little money; poor.","secondaryMeaning":"Without money at the moment (literal Latin: without pecunia).","passage":"Although he was born into a family of impecunious tenant farmers, the future president never forgot the cold of those winters and based much of his later policy on them.","correctAnswer":"impecunious","distractors":["affluent","frugal","generous"],"explanation":"Impecunious means poor. 'Tenant farmers' and 'the cold of those winters' show poverty. Affluent (wealthy) is opposite; frugal (thrifty) and generous (giving) don't describe poverty."},{"id":"vocab_137","entry":"nefarious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Extremely wicked, villainous, or criminal in nature.","secondaryMeaning":"Wicked in a flagrant and flagitious manner.","passage":"The detective finally uncovered the gang's ______ plot to divert the relief funds, a scheme that had been concealed under the guise of a charitable foundation.","correctAnswer":"nefarious","distractors":["commendable","trivial","tedious"],"explanation":"Nefarious means extremely wicked. 'Scheme concealed under the guise of a charitable foundation' shows criminal deception. Commendable (praiseworthy) is opposite; trivial (unimportant) and tedious (boring) don't fit a wicked plot."},{"id":"vocab_138","entry":"jovial","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Full of good humor; cheerful and friendly.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to Jove (Jupiter), supposed to induce joy (etymological sense).","passage":"The innkeeper's ______ welcome—hot cider, a crackling fire, and a long story about the local ghost—made the stranded travelers forget the storm that had driven them inside.","correctAnswer":"jovial","distractors":["sullen","indifferent","haughty"],"explanation":"Jovial means cheerful and friendly. 'Hot cider, a crackling fire, and a long story' show warmth and good humor. Sullen (gloomy) is opposite; indifferent (uncaring) and haughty (arrogant) don't fit a warm welcome."},{"id":"vocab_139","entry":"dirge","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A mournful song or poem, especially for a funeral; a lament.","secondaryMeaning":"A slow and solemn piece of music expressing grief.","passage":"The orchestra performed a ______ at the memorial, the cellos carrying a melody so low and slow that several mourners in the balcony had to look away.","correctAnswer":"dirge","distractors":["anthem","ballad","jubilee"],"explanation":"Dirge is a funeral lament. 'Memorial,' 'low and slow' melody, and 'mourners' fit mourning. Anthem (a patriotic song), ballad (a narrative song), and jubilee (a celebration) don't fit a funeral."},{"id":"vocab_140","entry":"maudlin","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Excessively sentimental or tearful, often in a self-pitying way.","secondaryMeaning":"Weakly tearful; foolishly emotional.","passage":"After the third glass of wine, the professor grew ______, weeping over his students' failure to appreciate Milton and vowing never to teach the poem again.","correctAnswer":"maudlin","distractors":["stoic","witty","blasé"],"explanation":"Maudlin means excessively tearful. 'Weeping…vowing never to teach again' shows self-pitying sentimentality. Stoic (unemotional), witty (amusing), and blasé (unimpressed) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_141","entry":"quixotic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Extravagantly idealistic, impractical, and unrealistic in pursuit of noble goals.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to Don Quixote (etymological sense).","passage":"The proposal to eliminate poverty by Christmas through voluntary donations struck the committee as ______, however admirable the senator's intentions.","correctAnswer":"quixotic","distractors":["pragmatic","cynical","lukewarm"],"explanation":"Quixotic means idealistic but impractical. 'Eliminate poverty by Christmas through voluntary donations' is noble but unrealistic. Pragmatic (practical) is opposite; cynical (distrustful) and lukewarm (unenthusiastic) don't fit idealism."},{"id":"vocab_142","entry":"pandemonium","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Wild uproar, tumult, or chaos.","secondaryMeaning":"The abode of all the demons (Miltonic, etymological sense).","passage":"When the fire alarm sounded during the chemistry exam, ______ erupted in the hall as students grabbed their papers, shoved through the doors, and shouted over the bell.","correctAnswer":"pandemonium","distractors":["serenity","order","silence"],"explanation":"Pandemonium is wild chaos. 'Shoved through the doors and shouted over the bell' shows uproar. Serenity, order, and silence are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_143","entry":"martinet","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A strict disciplinarian who demands rigid adherence to rules.","secondaryMeaning":"One who maintains excessive, especially military, discipline.","passage":"The new drill instructor was a ______, requiring every bunk to be made with hospital corners and fining recruits a dollar for every button out of place.","correctAnswer":"martinet","distractors":["slacker","liberal","mentor"],"explanation":"Martinet is a strict disciplinarian. 'Hospital corners' and 'fined recruits for a button out of place' show rigid discipline. Slacker (a lazy person) and liberal (lenient) are opposites; mentor (an advisor) doesn't fit rigid rule-enforcement."},{"id":"vocab_144","entry":"fiasco","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A complete and often embarrassing failure.","secondaryMeaning":"A utter, ridiculous failure (from the Italian for bottle, slang).","passage":"The product launch turned into a ______ when the demo unit caught fire on stage and the CEO had to evacuate the auditorium with the investors still in it.","correctAnswer":"fiasco","distractors":["triumph","precedent","diversion"],"explanation":"Fiasco is a complete failure. 'Caught fire on stage' and 'evacuate the auditorium' show disaster. Triumph (a success) is opposite; precedent (a model) and diversion (a distraction) don't fit a disaster."},{"id":"vocab_145","entry":"bowdlerize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To remove material considered offensive or improper from a text; to expurgate.","secondaryMeaning":"Of Thomas Bowdler, who published an expurgated Shakespeare (eponymous sense).","passage":"The school edition of the novel had been so thoroughly ______ that the climactic scene had been reduced to an ellipsis and a footnote.","correctAnswer":"bowdlerized","distractors":["annotated","amplified","translated"],"explanation":"Bowdlerize means to remove offensive material. 'Reduced to an ellipsis and a footnote' shows expurgation. Annotated (explained) and translated (rendered in another language) preserve text; amplified (expanded) is opposite."},{"id":"vocab_146","entry":"galvanize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To shock or excite into sudden action; to stir as if by electricity.","secondaryMeaning":"To coat iron with zinc (literal metallurgical sense).","passage":"The broadcast of the march on television ______ public opinion overnight, turning what had been a regional grievance into a national demand for new civil-rights law.","correctAnswer":"galvanized","distractors":["pacified","baffled","divided"],"explanation":"Galvanize means to stir into action. 'Turned a regional grievance into a national demand' shows sudden mobilization. Pacified (calmed) is opposite; baffled (confused) and divided (split) don't fit stirring to action."},{"id":"vocab_147","entry":"picayune","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Of little value or importance; petty and trifling.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a small Spanish coin (etymological sense).","passage":"The council spent three weeks debating the ______ question of which firm would supply the office paper, leaving the multi-million-dollar rezoning unread.","correctAnswer":"picayune","distractors":["pivotal","lucrative","imposing"],"explanation":"Picayune means of little importance. 'Three weeks on office paper' vs. 'multi-million-dollar rezoning unread' shows petty focus. Pivotal (crucial) and lucrative (profitable) are opposites; imposing (impressive) doesn't fit petty."},{"id":"vocab_148","entry":"gerrymander","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To manipulate the boundaries of a voting district to favor one party.","secondaryMeaning":"Of Governor Elbridge Gerry + salamander (eponymous sense).","passage":"After the census, the ruling party ______ the state's districts so artfully that it won three-quarters of the seats with barely half of the votes cast.","correctAnswer":"gerrymandered","distractors":["abolished","equalized","ignored"],"explanation":"Gerrymander means to redraw districts to favor one party. 'Won three-quarters of the seats with barely half of the votes' is the tell-tale result. Abolished (eliminated) and equalized (made equal) are opposites; ignored (disregarded) doesn't fit deliberate manipulation."},{"id":"vocab_149","entry":"maverick","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"An independent person who refuses to follow a group or party line.","secondaryMeaning":"An unbranded calf (etymological sense, after Samuel Maverick).","passage":"The senator was a ______, voting against her own party's bill and refusing to take the leadership's calls for a week before explaining her position in a floor speech.","correctAnswer":"maverick","distractors":["partisan","conformist","subordinate"],"explanation":"Maverick is an independent who refuses to follow the group. 'Voting against her own party's bill' shows independence. Partisan (a party loyalist) and conformist (a follower) are opposites; subordinate (an underling) doesn't imply independence."},{"id":"vocab_150","entry":"juggernaut","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"An irresistible force that crushes whatever stands in its path.","secondaryMeaning":" A large wagon bearing the idol Krishna, crushing devotees (etymological sense).","passage":"Once the company's platform reached a million subscribers, it became a ______, burying competitors before they could finish their launch presentations.","correctAnswer":"juggernaut","distractors":["underdog","obstacle","relic"],"explanation":"Juggernaut is an irresistible crushing force. 'Burying competitors before they could finish their launch' shows unstoppable dominance. Underdog (a weak contender) and obstacle (a hindrance) are opposites; relic (an old remnant) doesn't fit a dominant force."},{"id":"vocab_151","entry":"serendipity","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"The discovery of valuable things by fortunate accident.","secondaryMeaning":"A pleasant accident; coined by Horace Walpole from a Persian fairy tale.","passage":"The discovery of the anticancer drug in a contaminated petri dish was pure ______, the result of a sloppy technician rather than any planned search.","correctAnswer":"serendipity","distractors":["design","inevitability","calamity"],"explanation":"Serendipity is a fortunate accident. 'A sloppy technician rather than any planned search' shows luck. Design (intention) and inevitability (certainty) are opposites; calamity (disaster) is opposite in valence."},{"id":"vocab_152","entry":"zenith","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"The highest point or peak; the apex of achievement or power.","secondaryMeaning":"The point in the heavens directly above the observer (astronomical sense).","passage":"At the ______ of his career, the conductor held simultaneous posts in Berlin, Vienna, and Boston, and could dictate terms to any orchestra in Europe.","correctAnswer":"zenith","distractors":["nadir","outset","margin"],"explanation":"Zenith is the highest point. 'Conductor could dictate terms to any orchestra' shows peak power. Nadir (the lowest point) is opposite; outset (the beginning) and margin (an edge) don't fit a peak."},{"id":"vocab_153","entry":"nadir","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"The lowest point; the moment of greatest despair or decline.","secondaryMeaning":"The point on the celestial sphere directly below the observer (astronomical sense).","passage":"The winter of 1942 marked the ______ of the Allies' fortunes, with their armies retreating on every front and their cities blacking out under nightly bombing.","correctAnswer":"nadir","distractors":["zenith","apex","summit"],"explanation":"Nadir is the lowest point. 'Retreating on every front' and 'blacking out under nightly bombing' show the worst moment. Zenith, apex, and summit (the highest point) are all opposites."},{"id":"vocab_154","entry":"expunge","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To erase or remove completely; to wipe out as if it had never existed.","secondaryMeaning":"To strike out; to obliterate.","passage":"The judge ordered that the conviction be ______ from the record, restoring the defendant's clean slate as though the trial had never taken place.","correctAnswer":"expunged","distractors":["inscribed","preserved","displayed"],"explanation":"Expunge means to erase completely. 'Restoring the defendant's clean slate' and 'as though the trial had never taken place' show total removal. Inscribed (written), preserved (kept), and displayed (shown) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_155","entry":"eccentric","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Departing from the recognized or established norm; unconventional and peculiar.","secondaryMeaning":"Not having a common center (literal mathematical sense).","passage":"The mathematician was famously ______, wearing a different colored sock on each foot and addressing his lectures to the back wall rather than the class.","correctAnswer":"eccentric","distractors":["conventional","orthodox","predictable"],"explanation":"Eccentric means unconventional. 'A different colored sock on each foot' and addressing the back wall show peculiarity. Conventional, orthodox, and predictable (all meaning following the norm) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_156","entry":"extricate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To free or release from a difficult or tangled situation.","secondaryMeaning":"To disentangle; to release from an embarrassment or difficulty.","passage":"It took the lawyer three hours of careful questioning to ______ the witness from the contradictions she had created during her first testimony.","correctAnswer":"extricate","distractors":["entangle","implicate","abandon"],"explanation":"Extricate means to free from a difficulty. 'Free the witness from the contradictions' shows release. Entangle (trap) and implicate (incriminate) are opposites; abandon (leave behind) doesn't fit freeing someone."},{"id":"vocab_157","entry":"exemplary","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Serving as a desirable model; outstanding and worthy of imitation.","secondaryMeaning":"Of, relating to, or serving as an exemplar.","passage":"The principal praised the senior class as ______, noting that not one of them had missed a single day of school all year.","correctAnswer":"exemplary","distractors":["deficient","mediocre","obscure"],"explanation":"Exemplary means serving as a model. 'Not one had missed a single day' shows outstanding attendance. Deficient (lacking) and mediocre (average) are opposites; obscure (unknown) doesn't fit a model class."},{"id":"vocab_158","entry":"enumerate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To list one by one; to count or name items separately.","secondaryMeaning":"To ascertain the number of; to count.","passage":"In his opening statement, the prosecutor ______ the seven charges against the defendant, naming each victim and the date of each alleged offense.","correctAnswer":"enumerated","distractors":["suppressed","conflated","dismissed"],"explanation":"Enumerate means to list one by one. 'Naming each victim and the date' shows itemized listing. Suppressed (withheld) is opposite; conflated (merged) is opposite; dismissed (rejected) doesn't fit a careful list."},{"id":"vocab_159","entry":"elusive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Difficult to find, catch, define, or remember; evading grasp.","secondaryMeaning":"Tending to escape perception or comprehension.","passage":"The particles of dark matter proved ______, passing through every detector the physicists could devise and leaving not a single trace on a century of instruments.","correctAnswer":"elusive","distractors":["tangible","ubiquitous","stationary"],"explanation":"Elusive means hard to detect or catch. 'Passing through every detector' and 'not a single trace' show evasion of perception. Tangible (touchable), ubiquitous (everywhere present), and stationary (still) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_160","entry":"exorbitant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Exceeding all bounds of reason or moderation; wildly expensive.","secondaryMeaning":"Departing from the orbit; beyond customary limits (etymological sense).","passage":"The pharmaceutical company was fined for charging an ______ price for the generic drug, which cost pennies to make but sold for hundreds of dollars a pill.","correctAnswer":"exorbitant","distractors":["nominal","subsidized","customary"],"explanation":"Exorbitant means unreasonably expensive. 'Pennies to make but hundreds of dollars a pill' shows excess. Nominal (token-priced) is opposite; subsidized (funded to lower price) and customary (normal) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_161","entry":"redundant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Needlessly repetitive; exceeding what is necessary.","secondaryMeaning":"Dismissed from employment because no longer needed (British employment sense).","passage":"The editor cut the third paragraph as ______, since the same point had been made, almost in the same words, in the opening and closing paragraphs.","correctAnswer":"redundant","distractors":["essential","concise","innovative"],"explanation":"Redundant means needlessly repetitive. 'The same point had been made…in the opening and closing' shows repetition. Essential (necessary), concise (brief), and innovative (novel) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_162","entry":"repudiate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To refuse to accept or be associated with; to reject or disavow.","secondaryMeaning":"To refuse to pay or acknowledge (a debt or contract).","passage":"Under pressure from his own party, the candidate ______ his earlier support for the tariff, claiming he had never studied its effects on rural consumers.","correctAnswer":"repudiated","distractors":["reaffirmed","publicized","defended"],"explanation":"Repudiate means to reject or disavow. 'Claiming he had never studied its effects' shows he is rejecting his earlier stance. Reaffirmed (re-asserted) and defended (supported) are opposites; publicized (promoted) doesn't fit disavowal."},{"id":"vocab_163","entry":"relinquish","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To give up or surrender something, especially with reluctance.","secondaryMeaning":"To release, surrender, or yield a possession or right.","passage":"After thirty years on the bench, the chief judge agreed to ______ her seat only on the condition that her successor be chosen by an independent panel.","correctAnswer":"relinquish","distractors":["seize","conceal","amass"],"explanation":"Relinquish means to give up. 'After thirty years…agreed to [give up] her seat' shows surrender. Seize (take) is opposite; conceal (hide) and amass (accumulate) don't fit giving up a position."},{"id":"vocab_164","entry":"resilient","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Able to recover quickly from difficulty; bouncing back from adversity.","secondaryMeaning":"Returning to original shape after being stretched or compressed (physical sense).","passage":"The city proved remarkably ______, rebuilding its port within a year of the hurricane that had flattened the docks and displaced half its population.","correctAnswer":"resilient","distractors":["fragile","rigid","inert"],"explanation":"Resilient means recovering quickly. 'Rebuilding within a year of the hurricane' shows recovery. Fragile (easily broken) is opposite; rigid (inflexible) and inert (inactive) don't fit bouncing back."},{"id":"vocab_165","entry":"reaffirm","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To assert again strongly; to confirm a previous statement or commitment.","secondaryMeaning":"To affirm or declare anew.","passage":"In the treaty's final article, both nations ______ their commitment to mutual defense, spelling out the obligations in language that left no room for evasion.","correctAnswer":"reaffirmed","distractors":["renounced","qualified","contradicted"],"explanation":"Reaffirm means to assert again. 'Spelling out the obligations' shows renewed commitment. Renounced (gave up) is opposite; qualified (limited) and contradicted (opposed) don't fit renewed assertion."},{"id":"vocab_166","entry":"reticent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Restrained or reserved in speech; reluctant to reveal one's thoughts.","secondaryMeaning":"Having a tendency to keep silent; not speaking freely.","passage":"Despite the reporter's persistent questions, the witness remained ______, answering each with a shrug and a one-syllable word.","correctAnswer":"reticent","distractors":["garrulous","candid","vehement"],"explanation":"Reticent means reluctant to speak. 'A shrug and a one-syllable word' shows restraint. Garrulous (talkative) is opposite; candid (frank) implies openness, not restraint; vehement (passionate) doesn't fit silence."},{"id":"vocab_167","entry":"rebuff","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To reject bluntly or peremptorily; to refuse with sharpness.","secondaryMeaning":"To check or repel (a person) by a sharp or curt refusal.","passage":"The committee ______ the mayor's request for an emergency session, ruling that the matter could wait for the regular meeting the following week.","correctAnswer":"rebuffed","distractors":["entertained","solicited","endorsed"],"explanation":"Rebuff means to reject bluntly. 'The matter could wait' shows curt refusal. Entertained (considered) and endorsed (approved) are opposites; solicited (requested) is opposite in direction."},{"id":"vocab_168","entry":"renovate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To restore to a good state of repair; to make new again.","secondaryMeaning":"To restore to a former better state, often a building.","passage":"The city won a grant to ______ the abandoned railway terminal, planning to convert its vast waiting hall into a public market.","correctAnswer":"renovate","distractors":["raze","neglect","conceal"],"explanation":"Renovate means to restore. 'Convert its vast waiting hall into a public market' shows renewal. Raze (demolish) and neglect (let decay) are opposites; conceal (hide) doesn't fit restoration."},{"id":"vocab_169","entry":"rejuvenate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To make young or vigorous again; to restore to a former youthful state.","secondaryMeaning":"To restore to an original or fresh condition.","passage":"A week of hiking in the mountains seemed to ______ the exhausted executive, who returned to the office so restored that his staff hardly recognized him.","correctAnswer":"rejuvenate","distractors":["exhaust","aging","enervate"],"explanation":"Rejuvenate means to restore vigor. 'Returned…so restored' shows renewal of energy. Exhaust (drain) and enervate (weaken) are opposites; aging (the noun for growing old) doesn't fit as a verb."},{"id":"vocab_170","entry":"resurgent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Rising again; surging back after a period of decline.","secondaryMeaning":"Experiencing a revival of strength or activity.","passage":"The resurgent labor movement, dormant for two decades, organized the largest strike the country had seen since the war.","correctAnswer":"resurgent","distractors":["declining","dormant","terminal"],"explanation":"Resurgent means rising again. 'Dormant for two decades' then organizing 'the largest strike' shows a surge back. Declining (weakening) is opposite; dormant (asleep) and terminal (final) describe the prior state, not the rising back."},{"id":"vocab_171","entry":"repugnant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Extremely distasteful or offensive; repulsive to the mind or senses.","secondaryMeaning":"Disposed to fight against; contrary or inconsistent with.","passage":"The defense lawyer found his client's confession so morally ______ that he considered withdrawing from the case, however lucrative the fee.","correctAnswer":"repugnant","distractors":["congenial","indifferent","enticing"],"explanation":"Repugnant means offensive. 'Considered withdrawing' shows moral distaste. Congenial (agreeable) and enticing (attractive) are opposites; indifferent (uncaring) doesn't fit strong distaste."},{"id":"vocab_172","entry":"deleterious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Harmful, often in a subtle or unexpected way; injurious.","secondaryMeaning":"Often toxic; of a deleterious effect, harmful to health.","passage":"The runoff from the mine had a ______ effect on the river, raising mercury levels so gradually that the fishery collapsed before anyone thought to test the water.","correctAnswer":"deleterious","distractors":["salutary","benign","negligible"],"explanation":"Deleterious means harmful. 'Mercury levels…fishery collapsed' shows harm. Salutary (healthful) and benign (harmless) are opposites; negligible (insignificant) contradicts a collapsed fishery."},{"id":"vocab_173","entry":"decry","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To openly condemn or express strong disapproval of.","secondaryMeaning":"To depreciate by public statement.","passage":"The columnist ______ the new curriculum as a betrayal of the liberal arts, arguing that it reduced literature to a list of skills to be tested.","correctAnswer":"decried","distractors":["applauded","endorsed","implemented"],"explanation":"Decry means to condemn. 'A betrayal of the liberal arts' is open condemnation. Applauded (praised) and endorsed (supported) are opposites; implemented (carried out) doesn't fit condemnation."},{"id":"vocab_174","entry":"despondent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Extremely dejected and discouraged; in low spirits from loss of hope.","secondaryMeaning":"Marked by lack of hope; deeply discouraged.","passage":"After the third rejection in as many months, the novelist grew ______, wondering whether the years spent on the manuscript had been wasted.","correctAnswer":"despondent","distractors":["elated","resolute","indignant"],"explanation":"Despondent means deeply discouraged. 'Wondering whether the years…had been wasted' shows loss of hope. Elated (joyful) is opposite; resolute (determined) and indignant (angered) don't fit loss of hope."},{"id":"vocab_175","entry":"denounce","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To publicly condemn or accuse; to speak out against forcefully.","secondaryMeaning":"To inform against a person; to make known as threatening.","passage":"Five editors ______ the new press law as an assault on free inquiry, signing a public letter that appeared in every major paper the next morning.","correctAnswer":"denounced","distractors":["commended","tolerated","concealed"],"explanation":"Denounce means to publicly condemn. 'An assault on free inquiry' and a public letter show open accusation. Commended (praised) and tolerated (endured) are opposites; concealed (hid) contradicts public condemnation."},{"id":"vocab_176","entry":"demise","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"The end of something, especially by death or by gradual failure.","secondaryMeaning":"The conveyance of an estate by will or lease (legal sense).","passage":"The sudden ______ of the once-dominant newspaper chain began with a single leaked memo and ended, six months later, with the auction of its printing presses.","correctAnswer":"demise","distractors":["ascendance","inception","revival"],"explanation":"Demise means the end. 'Ended, six months later, with the auction of its printing presses' shows the chain's end. Ascendance (rise) and revival (return) are opposites; inception (the beginning) reverses the chronology."},{"id":"vocab_177","entry":"debunk","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To expose the falseness or exaggerated nature of a claim, myth, or belief.","secondaryMeaning":"To remove the nonsense from (from bunk).","passage":"The historian's new book ______ the legend that the colony had been founded for religious freedom, showing it was, in fact, a commercial venture from the start.","correctAnswer":"debunked","distractors":["perpetuated","corroborated","embellished"],"explanation":"Debunk means to expose a false claim. 'Showing it was, in fact, a commercial venture' exposes the legend's falseness. Perpetuated (kept alive), corroborated (confirmed), and embellished (decorated) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_178","entry":"deride","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To ridicule or mock with contempt; to laugh at scornfully.","secondaryMeaning":"To treat with contemptuous merriment.","passage":"The critic ______ the young painter's first show as 'kindergarten with pretensions,' a line that was quoted so widely it ended the artist's career before it began.","correctAnswer":"derided","distractors":["extolled","vindicated","commissioned"],"explanation":"Deride means to ridicule. 'Kindergarten with pretensions' is contemptuous mockery. Extolled (praised highly) is opposite; vindicated (cleared) and commissioned (hired) don't fit mockery."},{"id":"vocab_179","entry":"devoid","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Completely lacking or empty of something specified.","secondaryMeaning":"Free from; destitute of.","passage":"The dry valley was ______ of any vegetation except the gray moss that clung to the rocks and a single hardy bush at the dry riverbed.","correctAnswer":"devoid","distractors":["replete","brimming","teeming"],"explanation":"Devoid means completely lacking. 'Except the gray moss…and a single hardy bush' shows the valley lacks vegetation. Replete, brimming, and teeming (all meaning full) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_180","entry":"impeccable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Flawless; free from any fault or error.","secondaryMeaning":"Incapable of sinning (theological sense).","passage":"The pianist's technique was ______, every run and trit coming out clean and even, so that the critics noted nothing to improve in the next night's performance.","correctAnswer":"impeccable","distractors":["slovenly","mediocre","defective"],"explanation":"Impeccable means flawless. 'Nothing to improve' shows perfection. Slovenly (careless), mediocre (average), and defective (flawed) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_181","entry":"implacable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Unable to be appeased or pacified; relentless and unforgiving.","secondaryMeaning":"Not to be placated; inexorable.","passage":"The detective was an ______ pursuer, refusing to drop a case until every witness had been questioned and every file re-read, however many years it took.","correctAnswer":"implacable","distractors":["forgiving","yielding","indifferent"],"explanation":"Implacable means unappeasable. 'Refusing to drop a case until…however many years' shows relentless pursuit. Forgiving (merciful) and yielding (giving in) are opposites; indifferent (uncaring) doesn't fit relentless pursuit."},{"id":"vocab_182","entry":"inexorable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Unstoppable; relentless and not to be persuaded or moved.","secondaryMeaning":"Not to be moved or influenced by entreaty.","passage":"The march of the glacier was ______, grinding down the valley by a foot a year, however loudly the villagers protested to the indifferent geology board.","correctAnswer":"inexorable","distractors":["halted","negotiable","fleeting"],"explanation":"Inexorable means unstoppable. 'Grinding down the valley by a foot a year' shows relentless progress. Halted (stopped) is opposite; negotiable (persuadable) is opposite; fleeting (brief) doesn't fit a slow relentless force."},{"id":"vocab_183","entry":"incoherent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Lacking logical or orderly connection; confused and disorganized.","secondaryMeaning":"Unable to speak clearly, especially through shock or illness.","passage":"By the end of his shift, the surgeon's notes had grown ______, the words trailing off into scribbles that even the night nurse could not decipher.","correctAnswer":"incoherent","distractors":["coherent","succinct","authoritative"],"explanation":"Incoherent means disorganized. 'Trailing off into scribbles that…could not decipher' shows loss of clarity. Coherent (clear), succinct (concise), and authoritative (commanding) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_184","entry":"insurmountable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Incapable of being overcome; too great to be surmounted.","secondaryMeaning":"Incapable of being passed over; insuperable.","passage":"Faced with what looked like an ______ budget deficit, the new mayor surprised everyone by raising revenues without cutting a single service.","correctAnswer":"insurmountable","distractors":["trivial","surmountable","negligible"],"explanation":"Insurmountable means too great to overcome. 'Surprised everyone by raising revenues' implies the deficit looked unbeatable. Trivial (unimportant) and surmountable (overcome-able) are opposites; negligible (insignificant) contradicts the surprise."},{"id":"vocab_185","entry":"irreverent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Lacking proper respect or seriousness; showing deliberate disrespect.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to irreverence; disrespectful toward the sacred.","passage":"The comedian's ______ send-up of the founding fathers, delivered on the anniversary of the revolution, drew both riotous laughter and formal letters of complaint.","correctAnswer":"irreverent","distractors":["reverent","solemn","deferential"],"explanation":"Irreverent means lacking respect. 'Send-up' (a parody) and 'letters of complaint' show disrespect. Reverent (respectful), solemn (serious), and deferential (yielding respectfully) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_186","entry":"irresolute","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Uncertain how to act; hesitant and unable to make a decision.","secondaryMeaning":"Lacking resolute decision; wavering.","passage":"With the army advancing on one side and the parliament on the other, the king grew ______, changing his orders three times before breakfast and again before lunch.","correctAnswer":"irresolute","distractors":["decisive","resolute","steadfast"],"explanation":"Irresolute means unable to decide. 'Changing his orders three times before breakfast and again before lunch' shows vacillation. Decisive, resolute, and steadfast (all meaning firm and decided) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_187","entry":"circumspect","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Wary and unwilling to take risks; cautious and careful in action.","secondaryMeaning":"Looking around carefully; prudent and discreet.","passage":"The diplomat was ______ in his remarks, weighing every word before it left his mouth and refusing to answer any question that touched on the disputed border.","correctAnswer":"circumspect","distractors":["reckless","candid","impulsive"],"explanation":"Circumspect means cautious. 'Weighing every word before it left his mouth' shows care. Reckless (careless) and impulsive (acting without thought) are opposites; candid (frank) implies openness, opposite of caution."},{"id":"vocab_188","entry":"circuitous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Indirect and roundabout; taking a long, winding route.","secondaryMeaning":"Going around; of a logical argument, indirect and overly elaborate.","passage":"After a ______ journey through three connecting flights and a delayed ferry, the team reached the island only an hour before the match was due to begin.","correctAnswer":"circuitous","distractors":["direct","expeditious","straightforward"],"explanation":"Circuitous means roundabout. 'Three connecting flights and a delayed ferry' shows an indirect route. Direct, expeditious (efficient), and straightforward are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_189","entry":"circumvent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To get around or bypass, especially by cleverness or artful maneuvering.","secondaryMeaning":"To encircle; to go around (literal sense).","passage":"Finding that the law barred direct donations, the donors ______ the limit by routing the money through a series of shell charities that no regulator had yet examined.","correctAnswer":"circumvented","distractors":["enforced","observed","publicized"],"explanation":"Circumvent means to bypass. 'Routing the money through a series of shell charities' shows an artful bypass. Enforced (carried out) and observed (followed) are opposites; publicized (advertised) contradicts the secrecy of the maneuver."},{"id":"vocab_190","entry":"circumscribe","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To restrict the action or limits of; to confine within bounds.","secondaryMeaning":"To draw a line around; to mark off (literal sense).","passage":"The new regulations ______ the agency's authority so tightly that its inspectors could no longer enter a site without three separate permissions.","correctAnswer":"circumscribe","distractors":["expand","circumvent","delegate"],"explanation":"Circumscribe means to limit. 'Could no longer enter a site without three separate permissions' shows restriction. Expand (enlarge) is opposite; circumvent (bypass) is unrelated; delegate (assign) doesn't fit restriction."},{"id":"vocab_191","entry":"magnanimous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Generous and forgiving, especially toward a rival or someone weaker.","secondaryMeaning":"Great of soul; of high-minded and lofty character.","passage":"In a ______ gesture after the bitter campaign, the winning candidate appointed her chief rival as head of the ethics committee she had once chaired.","correctAnswer":"magnanimous","distractors":["vindictive","petty","spiteful"],"explanation":"Magnanimous means generous in spirit. 'Appointed her chief rival' after a bitter campaign shows forgiveness. Vindictive (revengeful), petty (small-minded), and spiteful (malicious) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_192","entry":"erroneous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Containing or based on error; incorrect.","secondaryMeaning":"Wandering or straying from the right course (archaic sense).","passage":"The report was based on an ______ assumption that the population had been stable for a decade, when in fact it had grown by a third.","correctAnswer":"erroneous","distractors":["accurate","verified","plausible"],"explanation":"Erroneous means incorrect. 'When in fact it had grown by a third' shows the assumption was wrong. Accurate (correct), verified (confirmed), and plausible (believable) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_193","entry":"momentous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Of great importance or significance, especially in its consequences.","secondaryMeaning":"Of moment; of great consequence.","passage":"The signing of the treaty was a ______ occasion, ending a war that had taken two continents' worth of young men and redrawing the borders of three empires.","correctAnswer":"momentous","distractors":["trivial","prosaic","fleeting"],"explanation":"Momentous means highly significant. 'Ending a war' and 'redrawing the borders of three empires' show vast importance. Trivial (unimportant) and prosaic (ordinary) are opposites; fleeting (brief) is unrelated to significance."},{"id":"vocab_194","entry":"mellifluous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Pleasingly smooth and sweet, especially of sound or language.","secondaryMeaning":"Flowing with honey (literal Latin sense).","passage":"The cellist's ______ tone carried to the last row of the gallery, every note as round and even as the one before it.","correctAnswer":"mellifluous","distractors":["grating","monotonous","piercing"],"explanation":"Mellifluous means smoothly sweet in sound. 'Round and even' notes show pleasing flow. Grating (harsh), monotonous (dull), and piercing (sharp) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_195","entry":"ominous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Threatening or foreshadowing evil; giving a warning of harm.","secondaryMeaning":"Foreboding; presaging ill.","passage":"The dark cloud bank on the horizon was an ______ sign, and the captain ordered the sails reefed before the first gust reached the boat.","correctAnswer":"ominous","distractors":["auspicious","reassuring","banal"],"explanation":"Ominous means threatening harm. 'Ordered the sails reefed before the first gust' shows the cloud warned of danger. Auspicious (favorable) and reassuring (comforting) are opposites; banal (boring) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_196","entry":"acrimonious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Full of bitterness and sharp anger, especially in speech or manner.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a bitter or harsh temper; rancorous.","passage":"The divorce proceedings grew so ______ that the two sides could not be in the same room, and the judge had to shuttle between floors to hear each side separately.","correctAnswer":"acrimonious","distractors":["amiable","conciliatory","cordial"],"explanation":"Acrimonious means bitter and angry. 'Could not be in the same room' shows hostility. Amiable (friendly), conciliatory (soothing), and cordial (warm) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_197","entry":"copious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Abundant in supply; plentiful and present in large quantity.","secondaryMeaning":"Large in quantity; profuse.","passage":"The expedition returned with ______ notes, filling seven notebooks with sketches, weather readings, and the names of plants no European botanist had yet recorded.","correctAnswer":"copious","distractors":["meager","scant","sparse"],"explanation":"Copious means abundant. 'Filling seven notebooks' shows plentiful notes. Meager, scant, and sparse (all meaning scanty) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_198","entry":"abstemious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Self-restrained, especially in food and drink; moderate in indulgence.","secondaryMeaning":"Sparing in diet; abstaining from excess.","passage":"The old monk's abstemious habits—a bowl of rice, a cup of tea, and no more—left him thin but alert at ninety, while his younger companions dozed after every meal.","correctAnswer":"abstemious","distractors":["gluttonous","intemperate","hedonistic"],"explanation":"Abstemious means moderate in consumption. 'A bowl of rice, a cup of tea, and no more' shows restraint. Gluttonous (over-indulgent), intemperate (lacking restraint), and hedonistic (pleasure-seeking) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_199","entry":"malodorous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Having a very unpleasant or foul smell.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a bad odor; stinking.","passage":"The malodorous canal, choked with industrial runoff for a century, finally ran clean after the new treatment plant opened upstream.","correctAnswer":"malodorous","distractors":["fragrant","pristine","aromatic"],"explanation":"Malodorous means foul-smelling. 'Choked with industrial runoff' shows the cause of the smell. Fragrant (sweet-smelling) and aromatic (pleasant-scented) are opposites; pristine (clean) describes the post-cleanup state, not the smell."},{"id":"vocab_200","entry":"tedious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Boring and tiring because of length, repetition, or dullness.","secondaryMeaning":"Wearisome from long continuance; tiresome.","passage":"The two-hour lecture on postal regulations grew so ______ that even the postmaster, who had requested the briefing, was seen checking his watch.","correctAnswer":"tedious","distractors":["riveting","concise","stimulating"],"explanation":"Tedious means boring and tiring. 'Checking his watch' shows dullness. Riveting (fascinating) and stimulating (exciting) are opposites; concise (brief) doesn't fit a two-hour lecture."},{"id":"vocab_201","entry":"heinous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Shockingly evil or wicked; utterly abominable.","secondaryMeaning":"Hateful; odious; deserving of strong condemnation.","passage":"The hijackers' attack on the school was judged so ______ that even the defendant's own lawyer asked the jury to consider the maximum penalty.","correctAnswer":"heinous","distractors":["commendable","venial","innocuous"],"explanation":"Heinous means shockingly evil. 'Even the defendant's own lawyer asked for the maximum penalty' shows the crime's enormity. Commendable (praiseworthy), venial (a minor sin), and innocuous (harmless) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_202","entry":"gratuitous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Unnecessary or unjustified; not called for by the circumstances.","secondaryMeaning":"Given freely; without charge (literal sense, now rare in this use).","passage":"The director's ______ close-up of the wound struck several reviewers as a cheap attempt to shock, since the story did not require it.","correctAnswer":"gratuitous","distractors":["justified","essential","restrained"],"explanation":"Gratuitous means uncalled-for. 'The story did not require it' shows the close-up was unjustified. Justified (warranted), essential (necessary), and restrained (held back) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_203","entry":"precarious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Uncertain, unstable, and dependent on chance; dangerously insecure.","secondaryMeaning":"Depending on the will of another; held by a uncertain tenure.","passage":"The mountaineer's grip grew ______ as the ice gave way, his fingers sliding down the rope toward a thirty-foot drop.","correctAnswer":"precarious","distractors":["secure","stable","firm"],"explanation":"Precarious means insecure. 'Ice gave way' and 'toward a thirty-foot drop' show dangerous uncertainty. Secure, stable, and firm (all meaning safely fixed) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_204","entry":"wistful","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Yearning with a hint of sadness; longing with melancholy.","secondaryMeaning":"Full of yearning; musing with a touch of sadness.","passage":"The old photograph made the woman ______ for the summers she had spent at the lake, the friends she had lost, and the years that had slipped by without her noticing.","correctAnswer":"wistful","distractors":["jubilant","indifferent","smug"],"explanation":"Wistful means longingly sad. 'The friends she had lost' and 'years…slipped by' show melancholy yearning. Jubilant (joyful) and smug (self-satisfied) are opposites; indifferent (uncaring) doesn't fit longing."},{"id":"vocab_205","entry":"earnest","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Serious and sincere in intention or purpose; not joking or flippant.","secondaryMeaning":"Of a serious and determined character; zealous.","passage":"The young volunteer was so ______ that she arrived an hour early each morning, convinced that the shelter's cats could sense her commitment.","correctAnswer":"earnest","distractors":["flippant","frivolous","indolent"],"explanation":"Earnest means serious and sincere. 'Arrived an hour early each morning' shows sincere commitment. Flippant (treating serious things lightly) and frivolous (silly) are opposites; indolent (lazy) contradicts early arrival."},{"id":"vocab_206","entry":"disgruntled","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Angry and dissatisfied, often because of perceived unfair treatment.","secondaryMeaning":"Discontented; in a bad humor.","passage":"The ______ employees leaked the salary spreadsheet to the press, revealing that the CEO earned three hundred times what the newest hire did.","correctAnswer":"disgruntled","distractors":["gratified","complacent","obliging"],"explanation":"Disgruntled means dissatisfied and resentful. 'Leaked the salary spreadsheet' shows displeasure at the pay gap. Gratified (satisfied) is opposite; complacent (smugly content) and obliging (helpful) don't fit resentment."},{"id":"vocab_207","entry":"authoritative","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Commanding and clearly self-confident; based on recognized expertise.","secondaryMeaning":"Dictatorial; of a recognized authority.","passage":"The handbook remains the most ______ survey of the period, the one a graduate student reaches for first and cites without checking elsewhere.","correctAnswer":"authoritative","distractors":["tentative","frivolous","irrelevant"],"explanation":"Authoritative means commanding recognition. 'Reaches for first and cites without checking elsewhere' shows trust. Tentative (uncertain) is opposite; frivolous (silly) and irrelevant (beside the point) don't fit a trusted reference."},{"id":"vocab_208","entry":"frivolity","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Lack of seriousness; a tendency to treat important matters lightly.","secondaryMeaning":"Silliness; a frivolous act or instance.","passage":"The trustee criticized the student newspaper's ______, pointing to the four pages of jokes that had displaced the news of a tuition increase to the back of the issue.","correctAnswer":"frivolity","distractors":["solemnity","diligence","gravity"],"explanation":"Frivolity means lack of seriousness. 'Four pages of jokes that had displaced the news' shows lightness. Solemnity (seriousness), diligence (careful work), and gravity (seriousness) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_209","entry":"acerbic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Sharp, sour, and harsh in tone; bitter and biting.","secondaryMeaning":"Sour or acid to the taste (literal sense).","passage":"The critic's ______ wit made her column required reading and feared throughout the publishing industry; no author wanted to be on the receiving end of one of her sentences.","correctAnswer":"acerbic","distractors":["mild","bland","genial"],"explanation":"Acerbic means sharp and harsh. 'Feared throughout the publishing industry' shows the bite of her wit. Mild (gentle), bland (flavorless/insipid), and genial (warm) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_210","entry":"inquisitive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Curious and eager for knowledge; tending to ask questions.","secondaryMeaning":"Inquiring; overly curious (sometimes nosy).","passage":"The ______ child followed the engineer around the power plant, asking why the turbines hummed and where the steam went after it turned the blades.","correctAnswer":"inquisitive","distractors":["apathetic","indifferent","oblivious"],"explanation":"Inquisitive means curious. 'Asking why the turbines hummed' shows eager questioning. Apathetic (uncaring), indifferent (unconcerned), and oblivious (unaware) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_211","entry":"reflective","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Thoughtful and given to careful consideration; marked by meditation.","secondaryMeaning":"Throwing back light or sound (literal physical sense).","passage":"In her later years the philosopher grew more ______, spending long hours at the window and writing less but, her students thought, more deeply.","correctAnswer":"reflective","distractors":["impulsive","frivolous","reactive"],"explanation":"Reflective means thoughtfully meditative. 'Spending long hours at the window' and 'writing less but more deeply' show contemplation. Impulsive (acting without thought), frivolous (silly), and reactive (acting in response) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_212","entry":"equivocal","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Open to more than one interpretation; deliberately ambiguous or evasive.","secondaryMeaning":"Of uncertain significance; allowing of two readings.","passage":"The witness's ______ answer—'I might have been there, or I might not'—frustrated the prosecutor, who needed a definite yes or no to build the case.","correctAnswer":"equivocal","distractors":["categorical","explicit","unambiguous"],"explanation":"Equivocal means open to two readings. 'I might have been there, or I might not' shows deliberate ambiguity. Categorical (absolute), explicit (clearly stated), and unambiguous (clear) are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_213","entry":"afflict","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To cause pain or suffering for someone; used when a person is suffering from a condition.","secondaryMeaning":"Usually appears in the passive: someone is afflicted with/by something.","passage":"Because she had been ______ with the rare autoimmune disease since childhood, the patient had grown so accustomed to the flare-ups that she no longer mentioned them to her friends.","correctAnswer":"afflicted","distractors":["inflicted","imposed","engaged"],"explanation":"Afflict is used when someone suffers from a condition ('afflicted with a disease'). Inflict means to force suffering upon someone ('inflict pain on'), so 'inflicted with' is unidiomatic here. Imposed and engaged don't describe being stricken by an illness."},{"id":"vocab_214","entry":"inflict","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To force pain, suffering, or something unpleasant upon someone.","secondaryMeaning":"Used in the pattern 'X is inflicted (up)on someone'.","passage":"Rather than face the rebellion, the dictator ______ the harshest penalties on the village, hoping the spectacle of punishment would discourage further dissent.","correctAnswer":"inflicted","distractors":["afflicted","granted","bestowed"],"explanation":"Inflict means to impose suffering on someone ('penalties inflicted on the village'). Afflict is used of a condition that strikes a person, not of penalties one imposes. Granted and bestowed are opposite in valence."},{"id":"vocab_215","entry":"elude","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To evade or escape from someone, especially by cleverness or speed.","secondaryMeaning":"To fail to be grasped or understood by someone.","passage":"Despite the marshals' careful roadblocks, the fugitive ______ them for nearly a month, slipping through the woods by night and foraging for food.","correctAnswer":"eluded","distractors":["alluded","pursued","confronted"],"explanation":"Elude means to escape capture ('eluded them for nearly a month'). Allude means to refer to indirectly, which makes no sense with roadblocks. Pursued and confronted are opposites of escaping."},{"id":"vocab_216","entry":"ambiguous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Open to more than one interpretation; unclear or inexact.","secondaryMeaning":"Doubtful or uncertain because of the possibility of more than one meaning.","passage":"The new statute was so ______ that two judges reading the same paragraph reached opposite conclusions about whether the tax applied to online sales at all.","correctAnswer":"ambiguous","distractors":["ambivalent","explicit","unanimous"],"explanation":"Ambiguous means open to two readings. 'Two judges…reached opposite conclusions' shows the statute allows two interpretations. Ambivalent means having mixed feelings (about people, not texts). Explicit and unanimous are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_217","entry":"anecdote","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A short, amusing or interesting story about a real incident or person.","secondaryMeaning":"A brief narrative used to illustrate a point.","passage":"The professor opened the lecture with an ______ about a chemist who had accidentally discovered a dye, using the story to introduce the theme of serendipity in science.","correctAnswer":"anecdote","distractors":["antidote","analogy","epilogue"],"explanation":"Anecdote is a brief story about a real incident. 'A chemist who had accidentally discovered a dye' is a short illustrative story. Antidote is a remedy for poison; analogy is a comparison; epilogue is a concluding section."},{"id":"vocab_218","entry":"antidote","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A substance that counteracts a poison or disease; a remedy.","secondaryMeaning":"Figuratively, something that counteracts an unpleasant quality.","passage":"Because the snake's venom has no known ______, a bite in a remote area is almost always fatal before the victim can reach a hospital with the right equipment.","correctAnswer":"antidote","distractors":["anecdote","antibody","analogue"],"explanation":"Antidote is a remedy for a poison. 'No known [remedy]' explains why a bite is fatal. Anecdote (a story), antibody (a protein), and analogue (a parallel) don't counteract venom."},{"id":"vocab_219","entry":"apprise","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To inform or notify someone of something.","secondaryMeaning":"To keep someone up to date about a developing situation.","passage":"Bound by disclosure rules, the estate agent was required to ______ the buyers of every known structural problem before they signed the contract.","correctAnswer":"apprise","distractors":["appraise","assure","avail"],"explanation":"Apprise means to inform ('apprise the buyers of every structural problem'). Appraise means to assess the value of something, not to notify. Assure (to reassure) and avail (to help) don't fit the disclosure requirement."},{"id":"vocab_220","entry":"censor","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To examine material and remove parts considered offensive or politically unacceptable.","secondaryMeaning":"An official who does this (noun sense).","passage":"In the years before the revolution, royal ______ struck whole passages from the histories, so that the version the public read bore little resemblance to the one the authors had written.","correctAnswer":"censors","distractors":["censures","censuses","centuries"],"explanation":"Censor (as a noun here) removes offensive material. 'Struck whole passages from the histories' shows the act of suppression. Censure (to reprimand) is the confusable near-homophone but means to scold, not to delete. Censuses (population counts) and centuries (periods) are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_221","entry":"cite","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To quote or refer to a source as evidence to support an argument.","secondaryMeaning":"To summon to appear in court (legal sense).","passage":"To support her claim that the Supreme Court can reverse itself, the historian ______ Brown v. Board of Education as the clearest example.","correctAnswer":"cited","distractors":["sited","sighted","chided"],"explanation":"Cite means to quote as evidence ('cited Brown v. Board of Education'). Site (to locate) and sight (to see) are homophones with unrelated meanings; chide (to scold) doesn't fit citing a case."},{"id":"vocab_222","entry":"site","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A place where something is, happens, or is planned to happen, especially a building or a website.","secondaryMeaning":"A website (modern usage).","passage":"The archaeologists chose the dig ______ carefully, mapping every square meter before they broke the surface, so that the position of each fragment could be recorded.","correctAnswer":"site","distractors":["cite","sight","side"],"explanation":"Site is a location. 'The dig [location] carefully, mapping every square meter' fits. Cite (to quote), sight (vision), and side (an edge) don't fit a place chosen for excavation."},{"id":"vocab_223","entry":"collaborate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To work together with others, especially on a creative or intellectual project.","secondaryMeaning":"To cooperate treasonably with an enemy occupying force (historical sense).","passage":"Two physicists from rival universities agreed to ______ on the paper, combining one's expertise in theory with the other's decades of experimental data.","correctAnswer":"collaborate","distractors":["corroborate","correlate","correspond"],"explanation":"Collaborate means to work together ('collaborate on the paper'). Corroborate means to confirm, not to co-author. Correlate (to relate) and correspond (to communicate or match) don't fit joint authorship."},{"id":"vocab_224","entry":"concede","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To admit that something is true or valid, often reluctantly.","secondaryMeaning":"In debate, to yield a point; in an election, to acknowledge defeat.","passage":"To strengthen her essay, the author ______ that the theory had limitations, then argued that these did not outweigh its explanatory power.","correctAnswer":"concedes","distractors":["confess","conceal","contend"],"explanation":"Concede means to admit a point, often against one's own case ('concede that the theory had limitations'). Confess implies admitting a crime or fault, too strong here. Conceal (to hide) is opposite; contend (to argue) is opposite of admitting."},{"id":"vocab_225","entry":"confess","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To admit or acknowledge that one has committed a crime or is at fault.","secondaryMeaning":"To disclose one's private thoughts or feelings (religious/devotional sense).","passage":"After years of carrying the secret, the old man finally ______ that he had forged the letter, a crime for which his brother had spent a decade in prison.","correctAnswer":"confessed","distractors":["conceded","concealed","contended"],"explanation":"Confess means to admit a fault or crime ('confessed that he had forged the letter'). Concede is for yielding a point in argument, too mild for admitting a crime. Concealed (hid) is opposite; contended (argued) doesn't fit admitting guilt."},{"id":"vocab_226","entry":"descent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"An act of moving downward; a slope or the act of going down.","secondaryMeaning":"Lineage or ancestry; the origin of a person.","passage":"The climbers' ______ from the summit was more dangerous than the ascent, the loose scree sliding under their boots with every step.","correctAnswer":"descent","distractors":["dissent","ascent","desert"],"explanation":"Descent is a downward movement. 'From the summit' and 'loose scree sliding' show going down. Dissent (disagreement) is a homophone but unrelated; ascent (going up) is the opposite; desert (a wasteland) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_227","entry":"dissent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Disagreement with or opposition to a prevailing opinion or decision.","secondaryMeaning":"A judge's written disagreement with the majority opinion (legal sense).","passage":"The lone ______ on the court, the justice argued that the majority had stretched the statute well beyond what Congress had intended.","correctAnswer":"dissent","distractors":["descent","assent","descent"],"explanation":"Dissent is disagreement with a majority. 'The lone [disagreement] on the court' fits a justice who opposes the majority ruling. Descent (going down) is a homophone; assent (agreement) is the opposite; the repeated 'descent' option is a distractor."},{"id":"vocab_228","entry":"uninterested","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Not interested; lacking curiosity or concern.","secondaryMeaning":"Indifferent or bored.","passage":"The child looked thoroughly ______ in the museum case, glancing at the fossil once and then tugging his mother toward the gift shop.","correctAnswer":"uninterested","distractors":["disinterested","unbiased","uninhibited"],"explanation":"Uninterested means not interested. 'Glancing once and tugging toward the gift shop' shows boredom. Disinterested means impartial (the confusable near-synonym) and does not fit a bored child. Unbiased is also a synonym of disinterested, not 'bored'; uninhibited (unrestrained) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_229","entry":"dramatic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Sudden and striking; producing a great or impressive effect.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to drama or the theater (literal sense).","passage":"The city's new transit system produced a ______ drop in traffic congestion, with weekday rush-hour delays cut nearly in half within six months.","correctAnswer":"dramatic","distractors":["expressive","gradual","modest"],"explanation":"Dramatic means sudden and striking. 'Delays cut nearly in half within six months' shows a large, sudden change. Expressive (communicating feeling) is the confusable near-synonym but doesn't fit a drop in numbers. Gradual and modest are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_230","entry":"expressive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Effectively communicating thoughts, feelings, or meaning.","secondaryMeaning":"Full of expression; showing emotion.","passage":"The dancer's face was so ______ that the audience in the back row could read the story of the piece without a single word of program notes.","correctAnswer":"expressive","distractors":["dramatic","impassive","explicit"],"explanation":"Expressive means communicating feeling. 'Read the story of the piece without a single word' shows the face conveyed meaning. Dramatic is the confusable near-synonym but means striking/theatrical. Impassive (showing no emotion) and explicit (clearly stated) don't fit a face that conveys feeling."},{"id":"vocab_231","entry":"illicit","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Forbidden by law, rules, or custom; illegal or improper.","secondaryMeaning":"Not permitted; unlicensed.","passage":"The investigation revealed an ______ trade in stolen artifacts that had passed through three countries before reaching the auction house.","correctAnswer":"illicit","distractors":["elicit","licit","explicit"],"explanation":"Illicit means illegal. 'Trade in stolen artifacts' is forbidden by law. Elicit (to draw out) is a near-homophone with an unrelated meaning; licit (lawful) is the opposite; explicit (clearly stated) doesn't describe legality."},{"id":"vocab_232","entry":"imminent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"About to happen very soon; impending.","secondaryMeaning":"Overhanging a person (etymological sense).","passage":"Geologists warned that an eruption was ______, pointing to the sudden swarm of small earthquakes beneath the volcano as a sign of magma on the move.","correctAnswer":"imminent","distractors":["eminent","permanent","remote"],"explanation":"Imminent means about to occur. 'A sign of magma on the move' and 'warned' show the eruption is near. Eminent (important/renowned) is a near-homophone applied to people, not eruptions. Permanent and remote are opposites of imminent."},{"id":"vocab_233","entry":"enrage","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To make extremely angry; to provoke to fury.","secondaryMeaning":"To throw into a rage.","passage":"The closing of the port ______ the merchants of the city, who had relied on trade for their living and now faced ruin before the winter.","correctAnswer":"enraged","distractors":["exacerbated","appeased","engaged"],"explanation":"Enrage means to make furious. 'Faced ruin before the winter' explains why the merchants would be furious. Exacerbate (to make worse) is a confusable near-synonym but means to worsen a situation, not to anger people. Appeased (calmed) is opposite; engaged (occupied) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_234","entry":"fatigue","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A feeling of extreme physical or mental tiredness; weariness.","secondaryMeaning":"The weakness caused by prolonged exertion or stress.","passage":"After running three successive marathons in as many weeks, the athlete complained not of soreness but of a bone-deep ______ that sleep alone could not relieve.","correctAnswer":"fatigue","distractors":["enrage","fracture","friction"],"explanation":"Fatigue is deep tiredness. 'Bone-deep' and 'sleep alone could not relieve' show exhaustion. Enrage (to anger), fracture (to break), and friction (rubbing) don't fit weariness."},{"id":"vocab_235","entry":"exhaust","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To use up completely; to drain of energy or resources.","secondaryMeaning":"To consider or describe completely (older sense).","passage":"The unexpected blizzard forced many drivers to ______ their fuel supplies as they circled the highway for hours looking for an open gas station.","correctAnswer":"exhaust","distractors":["fatigue","exhibit","exhilarate"],"explanation":"Exhaust means to use up completely ('exhaust their fuel supplies'). Fatigue is a noun (tiredness) and doesn't take a direct object like 'fuel supplies'. Exhibit (to show) and exhilarate (to enliven) don't fit draining a supply."},{"id":"vocab_236","entry":"exhausting","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Extremely tiring; causing great fatigue.","secondaryMeaning":"Causing one to feel completely drained.","passage":"The two-hour calculus exam was so ______ that several students fell asleep on the bus home rather than review the questions while the material was fresh.","correctAnswer":"exhausting","distractors":["exhaustive","exhilarating","easy"],"explanation":"Exhausting means tiring. 'Fell asleep on the bus home' shows the exam drained them. Exhaustive (thorough) is the confusable near-synonym but describes completeness, not tiringness. Exhilarating (invigorating) and easy are opposites."},{"id":"vocab_237","entry":"formally","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In a formal manner; officially or according to convention.","secondaryMeaning":"With due form or ceremony; in a refined way.","passage":"Although the council had voted to adopt the new rule, it agreed to delay ______ implementing it for six months so that departments could adjust their procedures.","correctAnswer":"formally","distractors":["formerly","formerly","formatively"],"explanation":"Formally means officially. 'Delay [officially] implementing it for six months' shows the official act was postponed. Formerly (in the past) is the confusable near-homophone but makes no sense with implementing. The duplicate 'formerly' is a distractor; formatively (in a formative way) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_238","entry":"formerly","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"In the past; previously.","secondaryMeaning":"Earlier; before a change of name, status, or ownership.","passage":"The largest city in Turkey is now called Istanbul, but it was ______ known as Constantinople, the capital of the Byzantine Empire for over a thousand years.","correctAnswer":"formerly","distractors":["formally","formidably","formatively"],"explanation":"Formerly means in the past. 'Now called Istanbul, but it was [previously] known as Constantinople' shows a former name. Formally (officially) is the confusable near-homophone but doesn't fit a former name. Formidably and formatively are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_239","entry":"whole","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A thing that is complete in itself; the entirety of something.","secondaryMeaning":"Not broken or divided (adjective sense).","passage":"The critic argued that the novel must be judged as a ______, not by the quality of any single chapter taken in isolation.","correctAnswer":"whole","distractors":["hole","wholly","hollow"],"explanation":"Whole means the complete entity. 'Not by any single chapter taken in isolation' contrasts a part with the complete work. Hole (an opening) is a near-homophone with an unrelated meaning. Wholly (entirely) is an adverb; hollow (empty) is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_240","entry":"hole","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"A hollow place in a solid body or surface; an opening.","secondaryMeaning":"A cavity or pit in the ground.","passage":"The gardener discovered a ______ in the trunk of the old oak, and inside it, to his surprise, a cache of coins wrapped in a cloth that had all but disintegrated.","correctAnswer":"hole","distractors":["whole","hollow","hale"],"explanation":"Hole is an opening or cavity. 'A [cavity] in the trunk' fits a hollow in the wood. Whole (complete) is a near-homophone; hollow (empty) and hale (healthy) are related but don't name the opening itself."},{"id":"vocab_241","entry":"complicit","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Involved with others in an illegal or morally wrong act; associated in wrongdoing.","secondaryMeaning":"Colluding or participating in something bad.","passage":"At the trial, the clerk insisted he was not ______ in the fraud, claiming he had merely followed his supervisor's instructions without understanding their purpose.","correctAnswer":"complicit","distractors":["implicit","explicit","compliant"],"explanation":"Complicit means involved in wrongdoing. 'Not [involved] in the fraud' fits a claim of innocence. Implicit (implied) is a confusable near-synonym but means not directly stated, not 'involved in crime'. Explicit (clearly stated) and compliant (obedient) don't fit involvement in fraud."},{"id":"vocab_242","entry":"ingenuous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Innocent and unsuspecting; overly trusting, sometimes to the point of naïveté.","secondaryMeaning":"Frank and open; without guile.","passage":"The young prince was so ______ that he believed every flatterer at court, never suspecting that the same men who praised him in public plotted against him in private.","correctAnswer":"ingenuous","distractors":["ingenious","inglorious","industrious"],"explanation":"Ingenuous means naïvely trusting. 'Believed every flatterer…never suspecting' shows innocence. Ingenious (clever) is the confusable near-synonym but means inventive. Inglorious (shameful) and industrious (hardworking) are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_243","entry":"interdependent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Mutually dependent; relying on one another.","secondaryMeaning":"Depending on each other in a reciprocal relationship.","passage":"Ecologists argue that the plants and animals of a forest are so ______ that the loss of any single species can unravel the entire system.","correctAnswer":"interdependent","distractors":["interpersonal","independent","inanimate"],"explanation":"Interdependent means mutually reliant. 'The loss of any single species can unravel the entire system' shows mutual reliance. Interpersonal (between people) is the confusable near-synonym but describes relations, not ecological reliance. Independent and inanimate are opposites or unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_244","entry":"interpersonal","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Relating to relationships or communication between people.","secondaryMeaning":"Occurring between individuals.","passage":"The new manager's ______ skills won over even the staff who had opposed her appointment, and within a month the whole department was working more smoothly.","correctAnswer":"interpersonal","distractors":["interdependent","intravenous","interpretive"],"explanation":"Interpersonal means between people. '[Between-people] skills won over the staff' fits managing relationships. Interdependent (mutually reliant) is a confusable near-synonym but describes systems, not people skills. Intravenous (into veins) and interpretive (explanatory) are unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_245","entry":"lay","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To put or set something down; takes a direct object.","secondaryMeaning":"Of birds, to produce an egg; past tense of 'lie' (older senses).","passage":"Before going to sleep each night, the old sailor would ______ his pipe on the table beside the bed, a habit of fifty years he could not break.","correctAnswer":"lay","distractors":["lie","laid","lain"],"explanation":"Lay means to put something down (transitive, takes an object 'his pipe'). Lie (to recline) is intransitive and takes no object. Laid (past tense of lay) and lain (past participle of lie) are wrong tense/voice for a present-tense habit."},{"id":"vocab_246","entry":"lie","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"To recline or rest in a horizontal position; takes no direct object.","secondaryMeaning":"To be situated; to tell a falsehood (homonym, unrelated sense).","passage":"After hiking for nearly five hours, the climbers were relieved to ______ down on the soft grass at the trail's end and stare at the clouds.","correctAnswer":"lie","distractors":["lay","lain","laid"],"explanation":"Lie means to recline (intransitive, no object—'lie down'). Lay (to put something down) requires an object. Lain (past participle of lie) and laid (past of lay) are wrong tense for the infinitive after 'to'."},{"id":"vocab_247","entry":"proceed","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To go forward, continue, or carry on after a pause or interruption.","secondaryMeaning":"To begin or move in a particular direction.","passage":"After insisting throughout the campaign that he would never raise taxes, the newly elected governor ______ to do exactly that in his first budget.","correctAnswer":"proceeded","distractors":["preceded","processed","proposed"],"explanation":"Proceed means to go forward or carry on ('proceeded to do exactly that'). Precede (to come before) is the confusable near-synonym but reverses the order. Processed (handled) and proposed (suggested) don't fit 'went on to do'."},{"id":"vocab_248","entry":"used to","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"(1) Accustomed to; (2) indicates a frequently performed action in the past.","secondaryMeaning":"Note: 'use to' is not a valid phrase; the 'd' is always present.","passage":"As a child, I ______ wake up at six every Saturday to watch the cartoons, but I have not seen one in years and could not tell you what is on now.","correctAnswer":"used to","distractors":["use to","using to","use too"],"explanation":"'Used to' marks a past habitual action ('I [used to] wake up at six every Saturday'). 'Use to' is a common error—'use to' is not a valid construction. 'Using to' and 'use too' are grammatically wrong."},{"id":"vocab_249","entry":"in addition","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In addition signals that the second clause adds a further benefit, the same category as the first (what the library offers).","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The new library will house a quarter of a million books. ______, it will offer free coding classes to neighborhood children every Saturday morning.","correctAnswer":"In addition","distractors":["Therefore","Nevertheless","Likewise"],"explanation":"In addition signals that the second clause adds a further benefit, the same category as the first (what the library offers). Therefore marks cause-and-effect; nevertheless marks reversal; likewise marks comparison between two similar items, not the addition of a new point."},{"id":"vocab_250","entry":"furthermore","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Furthermore adds a second supporting point in the same direction.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The proposed bridge would cut the commute by half an hour. ______, it would connect two neighborhoods that have been divided by the river for over a century.","correctAnswer":"Furthermore","distractors":["However","Consequently","Instead"],"explanation":"Furthermore adds a second supporting point in the same direction. However would reverse the first clause; consequently would imply the bridge exists because of the commute; instead would substitute one option for another, not add a point."},{"id":"vocab_251","entry":"also","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Also continues the list of the committee's actions.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The committee voted to expand the park. It ______ approved funding for a new community garden on the park's western edge, to be planted by volunteers in the spring.","correctAnswer":"also","distractors":["thus","yet","namely"],"explanation":"Also continues the list of the committee's actions. Thus marks consequence; yet marks contrast; namely introduces a specification, none of which fits adding a parallel action."},{"id":"vocab_252","entry":"and","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"And joins two parallel contents of the exhibit (paintings and letters).","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The exhibit features paintings from the early period of the artist's career ______ a selection of letters that shed light on his friendships with other modernists.","correctAnswer":"and","distractors":["but","so","or"],"explanation":"And joins two parallel contents of the exhibit (paintings and letters). But would contrast them; so would make the letters a consequence; or would present them as alternatives."},{"id":"vocab_253","entry":"besides","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Besides adds a second, supporting reason for the same conclusion (too few members + schedule conflict).","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The club has too few members to field a team this season. ______, most of the remaining players have evening jobs that conflict with practice.","correctAnswer":"Besides","distractors":["Therefore","Conversely","In contrast"],"explanation":"Besides adds a second, supporting reason for the same conclusion (too few members + schedule conflict). Therefore would mark the second clause as a consequence of the first; conversely and in contrast would signal reversal."},{"id":"vocab_254","entry":"for example","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"For example introduces a specific illustration of the general claim.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"Many common English words come from trades. ______, the word 'tawdry' derives from 'Saint Audrey's lace,' a cheap necktie sold at a medieval fair.","correctAnswer":"For example","distractors":["Therefore","However","Similarly"],"explanation":"For example introduces a specific illustration of the general claim. Therefore marks consequence; however marks reversal; similarly would require a second parallel item, not an example."},{"id":"vocab_255","entry":"indeed","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Indeed intensifies and confirms the previous point.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The new policy has saved the city millions in energy costs. ______, the savings in the first year alone exceeded the entire budget of the conservation office.","correctAnswer":"Indeed","distractors":["However","Instead","Subsequently"],"explanation":"Indeed intensifies and confirms the previous point. However would reverse; instead would substitute; subsequently would mark a later event in time."},{"id":"vocab_256","entry":"in particular","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In particular singles out one especially relevant instance.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The report found problems across the district's schools. ______, the middle schools had not updated their science labs in nearly two decades.","correctAnswer":"In particular","distractors":["Nevertheless","For example","Meanwhile"],"explanation":"In particular singles out one especially relevant instance. Nevertheless reverses; for example gives a representative instance (close, but 'in particular' stresses importance, not mere illustration); meanwhile marks a simultaneous event."},{"id":"vocab_257","entry":"similarly","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Similarly marks a parallel between two writers' methods.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The first author used folktales to structure her novel. ______, the second writer borrowed the rhythm of oral storytelling to shape his memoir.","correctAnswer":"Similarly","distractors":["Nevertheless","Consequently","Namely"],"explanation":"Similarly marks a parallel between two writers' methods. Nevertheless reverses; consequently marks cause-and-effect; namely introduces a specification rather than a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_258","entry":"likewise","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Likewise marks a parallel between the two colonies.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The northern colony relied on shipbuilding for its wealth. ______, the southern colony grew rich on the export of tobacco and cotton to European markets.","correctAnswer":"Likewise","distractors":["However","Therefore","Conversely"],"explanation":"Likewise marks a parallel between the two colonies. However and conversely mark reversal; therefore marks consequence."},{"id":"vocab_259","entry":"by the same token","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"By the same token applies the same reasoning to a parallel case.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"A tax on imported steel raises the price of cars. By the same token, a tariff on lumber raises the cost of new housing.","correctAnswer":"By the same token","distractors":["On the contrary","As a result","For instance"],"explanation":"By the same token applies the same reasoning to a parallel case. On the contrary reverses; as a result marks consequence; for instance gives a single example rather than a parallel argument."},{"id":"vocab_260","entry":"alternatively","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Alternatively presents a second option.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The council could raise property taxes to close the deficit. ______, it could sell the unused land on the western edge of town to a developer.","correctAnswer":"Alternatively","distractors":["Therefore","Moreover","Likewise"],"explanation":"Alternatively presents a second option. Therefore marks consequence; moreover adds a further point in the same vein; likewise draws a parallel rather than offering an option."},{"id":"vocab_261","entry":"instead","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Instead marks a substitution—replacing the rejected option with another.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The architect did not propose a taller building. ______, she redesigned the lobby to bring in more natural light without adding a single floor.","correctAnswer":"Instead","distractors":["Therefore","Furthermore","Meanwhile"],"explanation":"Instead marks a substitution—replacing the rejected option with another. Therefore marks consequence; furthermore adds a parallel point; meanwhile marks a simultaneous event."},{"id":"vocab_262","entry":"on the contrary","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"On the contrary corrects a mistaken assumption, replacing it with the opposite.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The critic's review was not a dismissal of the young novelist. ______, it praised the book as the most promising debut of the decade.","correctAnswer":"On the contrary","distractors":["As a result","In addition","For instance"],"explanation":"On the contrary corrects a mistaken assumption, replacing it with the opposite. As a result marks consequence; in addition adds a point; for instance gives an example."},{"id":"vocab_263","entry":"on the other hand","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"On the other hand introduces a counter-consideration to the previous benefits.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The new software is faster and cheaper to maintain than the old one. ______, it requires a complete retraining of the staff who have used the old system for fifteen years.","correctAnswer":"On the other hand","distractors":["Therefore","Likewise","Namely"],"explanation":"On the other hand introduces a counter-consideration to the previous benefits. Therefore marks consequence; likewise draws a parallel; namely specifies."},{"id":"vocab_264","entry":"whereas","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Whereas marks a direct contrast between two clauses.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The desert tortoise can survive for months without water, ______ the frog must keep its skin moist or it will die within a day.","correctAnswer":"whereas","distractors":["because","although","so"],"explanation":"Whereas marks a direct contrast between two clauses. Because marks cause; although marks concession; so marks consequence."},{"id":"vocab_265","entry":"while","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"While marks a contrast between two simultaneous situations.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the north received record rainfall this summer, the south endured a drought that parched the crops and emptied the reservoirs.","correctAnswer":"While","distractors":["Because","Since","Therefore"],"explanation":"While marks a contrast between two simultaneous situations. Because and since mark cause; therefore marks consequence."},{"id":"vocab_266","entry":"yet","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Yet marks a contrast that runs against expectation.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The team had practiced for months, ______ it lost every match of the season, finishing last in the league.","correctAnswer":"yet","distractors":["so","and","since"],"explanation":"Yet marks a contrast that runs against expectation. So marks consequence; and merely joins; since marks cause."},{"id":"vocab_267","entry":"still","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Still marks a contrast with what precedes while admitting its truth.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The bridge is over a century old and shows its age in every beam. ______, it remains the safest crossing on the river, having withstood every flood in the region's recorded history.","correctAnswer":"Still","distractors":["Therefore","Moreover","Likewise"],"explanation":"Still marks a contrast with what precedes while admitting its truth. Therefore marks consequence; moreover adds a point; likewise draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_268","entry":"rather","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Rather signals a correction or substitution for what one might expect.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The committee did not reject the plan outright. ______, it asked the architects to revise the budget and resubmit the proposal in the spring.","correctAnswer":"Rather","distractors":["Thus","Furthermore","Meanwhile"],"explanation":"Rather signals a correction or substitution for what one might expect. Thus marks consequence; furthermore adds a point; meanwhile marks a simultaneous event."},{"id":"vocab_269","entry":"even so","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Even so marks a concession that nonetheless holds.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The forecast called for clear skies. ______, the captain ordered the sails reefed before nightfall, having learned long ago not to trust the weather at this time of year.","correctAnswer":"Even so","distractors":["Thus","Besides","For instance"],"explanation":"Even so marks a concession that nonetheless holds. Thus marks consequence; besides adds a reason; for instance gives an example."},{"id":"vocab_270","entry":"nonetheless","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Nonetheless marks a concession (despite the loss, they pressed on).","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The expedition had lost two of its three sleds in the crevasse. ______, the remaining team pressed on toward the pole, determined to reach it before winter closed in.","correctAnswer":"Nonetheless","distractors":["Accordingly","In addition","For example"],"explanation":"Nonetheless marks a concession (despite the loss, they pressed on). Accordingly marks consequence; in addition adds a point; for example gives an instance."},{"id":"vocab_271","entry":"in contrast","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In contrast marks a sharp opposition between two cases.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The northern counties voted overwhelmingly for the reform. ______, the southern counties rejected it by an equally large margin.","correctAnswer":"In contrast","distractors":["Likewise","Consequently","Moreover"],"explanation":"In contrast marks a sharp opposition between two cases. Likewise draws a parallel; consequently marks consequence; moreover adds a point."},{"id":"vocab_272","entry":"in spite of","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In spite of marks a concession (despite the warnings, they approved it).","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the warnings of the geologists, the town council approved the development on the hillside, a decision many would regret within the year.","correctAnswer":"In spite of","distractors":["Because of","As a result of","In addition to"],"explanation":"In spite of marks a concession (despite the warnings, they approved it). Because of and as a result of mark cause; in addition to adds a parallel item."},{"id":"vocab_273","entry":"despite","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional preposition","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Despite marks a concession.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ a budget smaller than any of its rivals, the small studio produced three of the year's most acclaimed films.","correctAnswer":"Despite","distractors":["Because of","Accordingly","Therefore"],"explanation":"Despite marks a concession. Because of marks cause; accordingly and therefore mark consequence."},{"id":"vocab_274","entry":"although","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Although marks a concession.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the manuscript was incomplete, the publisher offered the author a contract, persuaded by the strength of the first three chapters.","correctAnswer":"Although","distractors":["Because","Since","Therefore"],"explanation":"Although marks a concession. Because and since mark cause; therefore marks consequence."},{"id":"vocab_275","entry":"even though","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Even though marks a strong concession.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the league had suspended him for the season, the pitcher continued to train every morning, determined to return the next year stronger than before.","correctAnswer":"Even though","distractors":["Since","Thus","Moreover"],"explanation":"Even though marks a strong concession. Since marks cause; thus marks consequence; moreover adds a point."},{"id":"vocab_276","entry":"otherwise","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Otherwise marks the consequence if the condition is not met.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The hikers had to reach the shelter before nightfall; ______, they would be exposed to temperatures that could freeze the water in their bottles within an hour.","correctAnswer":"otherwise","distractors":["therefore","likewise","meanwhile"],"explanation":"Otherwise marks the consequence if the condition is not met. Therefore marks an actual consequence; likewise draws a parallel; meanwhile marks a simultaneous event."},{"id":"vocab_277","entry":"thus","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Thus marks a consequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The drought destroyed nearly half of the region's harvest. ______, the price of bread rose threefold in the markets of the capital by the end of the summer.","correctAnswer":"Thus","distractors":["However","Moreover","Similarly"],"explanation":"Thus marks a consequence. However reverses; moreover adds a point; similarly draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_278","entry":"therefore","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Therefore marks a consequence of the study's finding.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The study found that the new drug had no effect on the disease. ______, the company halted the trials and returned the unused funds to its investors.","correctAnswer":"Therefore","distractors":["Nevertheless","Furthermore","For instance"],"explanation":"Therefore marks a consequence of the study's finding. Nevertheless reverses; furthermore adds; for instance gives an example."},{"id":"vocab_279","entry":"as a result","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"As a result marks a consequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The factory had released untreated waste into the river for a decade. ______, the fish population downstream had collapsed to a tenth of its former size.","correctAnswer":"As a result","distractors":["On the other hand","In addition","Likewise"],"explanation":"As a result marks a consequence. On the other hand marks contrast; in addition adds; likewise draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_280","entry":"as such","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"As such applies the preceding definition to draw a consequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The committee is an advisory body with no power to enact laws. ______, its recommendations must pass the full legislature before they take effect.","correctAnswer":"As such","distractors":["Nevertheless","Besides","Instead"],"explanation":"As such applies the preceding definition to draw a consequence. Nevertheless reverses; besides adds a reason; instead substitutes an option."},{"id":"vocab_281","entry":"because","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Because marks a cause.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The harbor was closed for the winter, ______ the merchant ships had to anchor offshore and wait for the ice to break up in the spring.","correctAnswer":"because","distractors":["although","yet","therefore"],"explanation":"Because marks a cause. Although marks concession; yet marks contrast; therefore marks consequence (and would reverse the order of the clauses)."},{"id":"vocab_282","entry":"since","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Since marks a cause.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the museum had outgrown its old building, the trustees began a campaign to raise funds for a larger one near the city center.","correctAnswer":"Since","distractors":["Although","Yet","In contrast"],"explanation":"Since marks a cause. Although marks concession; yet marks contrast; in contrast marks opposition between two items."},{"id":"vocab_283","entry":"so","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"So marks a consequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The reservoir was nearly empty after three dry winters, ______ the town imposed strict limits on every household's daily use of water.","correctAnswer":"so","distractors":["although","but","while"],"explanation":"So marks a consequence. Although marks concession; but marks contrast; while marks a contrast between simultaneous events."},{"id":"vocab_284","entry":"thereby","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Thereby marks a means-by-which consequence ('by doing so, cutting the time').","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The company reorganized its factories around a single assembly line, ______ cutting the time to build each car by more than half.","correctAnswer":"thereby","distractors":["however","nevertheless","instead"],"explanation":"Thereby marks a means-by-which consequence ('by doing so, cutting the time'). However, nevertheless, and instead all mark reversal or substitution."},{"id":"vocab_285","entry":"next","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","History Passages"],"definition":"Next marks the following step in a sequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The chemists dissolved the compound in warm acid and filtered out the residue. ______, they heated the remaining liquid until crystals began to form along the bottom of the flask.","correctAnswer":"Next","distractors":["Therefore","However","Likewise"],"explanation":"Next marks the following step in a sequence. Therefore marks consequence; however reverses; likewise draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_286","entry":"then","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","History Passages"],"definition":"Then marks the next event in a sequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The explorers crossed the pass in late October and reached the lake a week later. ______, having restocked their provisions, they turned south toward the uncharted valley.","correctAnswer":"Then","distractors":["Meanwhile","Conversely","Thus"],"explanation":"Then marks the next event in a sequence. Meanwhile marks a simultaneous event; conversely reverses; thus marks consequence."},{"id":"vocab_287","entry":"finally","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Finally marks the last item in a sequence.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The committee reviewed the budget, debated the staffing, and ______ voted to approve the proposal with only a single dissenting voice.","correctAnswer":"finally","distractors":["instead","therefore","similarly"],"explanation":"Finally marks the last item in a sequence. Instead substitutes; therefore marks consequence; similarly draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_288","entry":"ultimately","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Ultimately marks the end result of a long process.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"After years of failed negotiations, the two nations ______ signed a treaty that divided the disputed territory along the river.","correctAnswer":"Ultimately","distractors":["Nevertheless","For example","Meanwhile"],"explanation":"Ultimately marks the end result of a long process. Nevertheless reverses; for example gives an instance; meanwhile marks a simultaneous event."},{"id":"vocab_289","entry":"meanwhile","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Meanwhile marks a simultaneous event.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The general laid siege to the city. ______, the king's envoys were already riding north to seek an alliance that might break the siege before winter.","correctAnswer":"Meanwhile","distractors":["Therefore","Moreover","Conversely"],"explanation":"Meanwhile marks a simultaneous event. Therefore marks consequence; moreover adds a point; conversely reverses."},{"id":"vocab_290","entry":"as","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional conjunction","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"As marks a simultaneous event.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______ the architect revised the plans, the builders began to pour the foundation, so that no time would be lost before the new drawings arrived.","correctAnswer":"As","distractors":["Although","Yet","So that"],"explanation":"As marks a simultaneous event. Although marks concession; yet marks contrast; 'so that' (a separate subordinator) marks purpose, not simultaneity."},{"id":"vocab_291","entry":"that is","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"That is introduces a restatement or definition.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The treaty required both nations to reduce their arsenals by a fixed percentage. That is, each ______ had to destroy a specific share of its weapons within five years.","correctAnswer":"that is","distractors":["namely","for example","however"],"explanation":"That is introduces a restatement or definition. Namely introduces a specific item (close, but 'that is' rephrases rather than lists); for example gives an instance; however reverses."},{"id":"vocab_292","entry":"namely","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional adverb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Namely introduces a specific list of the items just mentioned.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The club awards two prizes each year. ______, one for fiction and one for non-fiction, each judged by a separate panel of three writers.","correctAnswer":"namely","distractors":["therefore","nevertheless","similarly"],"explanation":"Namely introduces a specific list of the items just mentioned. Therefore marks consequence; nevertheless reverses; similarly draws a parallel."},{"id":"vocab_293","entry":"abate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To become less intense, widespread, or numerous; to subside.","secondaryMeaning":"To reduce in amount or degree.","passage":"After three days of relentless rain, the storm finally began to ______, leaving behind flooded streets but a clearing sky.","correctAnswer":"abate","distractors":["amplify","persist","intensify"],"explanation":"'Abate' means to lessen or subside. The clue 'after three days of relentless rain' is followed by 'a clearing sky,' signaling the storm is weakening, not amplifying or persisting."},{"id":"vocab_294","entry":"acumen","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Keenness and depth of perception, discernment, or insight, especially in practical matters.","secondaryMeaning":"Sharpness in dealing with business or strategy.","passage":"The investor's financial ______ allowed her to spot the startup's potential years before its competitors even noticed the sector.","correctAnswer":"acumen","distractors":["apathy","naivety","doubt"],"explanation":"'Acumen' denotes sharp insight or keen judgment. The fact that she 'spotted the startup's potential years before' others shows exceptional discernment, not apathy, naivety, or doubt."},{"id":"vocab_295","entry":"affable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Friendly, good-natured, and easy to talk to.","secondaryMeaning":"Pleasant and approachable in manner.","passage":"Despite running a Fortune 500 company, the CEO remained remarkably ______, chatting warmly with interns and remembering their names months later.","correctAnswer":"affable","distractors":["surly","aloof","gruff"],"explanation":"'Affable' means warm and approachable. 'Chatting warmly with interns' contrasts with the title 'Fortune 500 CEO,' showing friendliness rather than being surly, aloof, or gruff."},{"id":"vocab_296","entry":"alleviate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To make suffering, hardship, or a problem less severe; to ease.","secondaryMeaning":"To lighten or reduce the intensity of something unpleasant.","passage":"The new trade policy was designed to ______ the economic strain on rural families, though critics argued it merely postponed the underlying crisis.","correctAnswer":"alleviate","distractors":["worsen","compound","intensify"],"explanation":"'Alleviate' means to ease or make less severe. The phrase 'economic strain' pairs with 'designed to lessen,' the opposite of worsening, compounding, or intensifying it."},{"id":"vocab_297","entry":"analogous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Comparable in certain respects, similar in a way that permits a useful comparison.","secondaryMeaning":"Corresponding in function or relationship, though not in form.","passage":"The scientist argued that the human immune system is ______ to a nation's defense network, each deploying specialized agents that respond only to recognized threats.","correctAnswer":"analogous","distractors":["disparate","unrelated","contrary"],"explanation":"'Analogous' means comparable in a meaningful way. The comparison 'immune system' to 'a nation's defense network' is an explicit analogy—both deploy 'specialized agents'—not a claim of unrelated or contrary systems."},{"id":"vocab_298","entry":"arbitrary","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Based on personal whim or random choice rather than on any reason or system.","secondaryMeaning":"Determined by individual discretion rather than rule or law.","passage":"The committee's selection of finalists struck many as ______, since the judges offered no criteria and rejected a candidate with identical credentials for a previously rejected one.","correctAnswer":"arbitrary","distractors":["methodical","impartial","deliberate"],"explanation":"'Arbitrary' means based on whim, not reason. 'No criteria' and 'rejected a candidate with identical credentials' show random decision-making, the opposite of methodical, impartial, or deliberate judgment."},{"id":"vocab_299","entry":"benevolent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Well-meaning and kindly; showing goodwill or a desire to help others.","secondaryMeaning":"Characterized by charitable intent rather than self-interest.","passage":"The factory owner was remembered as a ______ employer who, during the Depression, kept paying his workers even as his own profits vanished.","correctAnswer":"benevolent","distractors":["malevolent","cynical","spiteful"],"explanation":"'Benevolent' means kindly and well-meaning. 'Kept paying his workers even as his own profits vanished' shows goodwill at personal cost, the opposite of malevolent, cynical, or spiteful behavior."},{"id":"vocab_300","entry":"bolster","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To support, strengthen, or prop up something.","secondaryMeaning":"To give a boost to confidence, an argument, or a structure.","passage":"The newly declassified memos ______ the historian's claim that the administration knew of the famine months before issuing any public statement.","correctAnswer":"bolster","distractors":["undermine","deplete","contradict"],"explanation":"'Bolster' means to support or strengthen. 'Newly declassified memos' provide supporting evidence for the claim; they do not undermine, deplete, or contradict it."},{"id":"vocab_301","entry":"brevity","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Concise and exact use of words in writing or speech; shortness of duration.","secondaryMeaning":"The quality of being brief.","passage":"The chief admired the lieutenant's ______: her field reports rarely exceeded a page yet contained every detail a command decision required.","correctAnswer":"brevity","distractors":["verbosity","redundancy","digression"],"explanation":"'Brevity' means shortness or conciseness. The report 'rarely exceeded a page' yet contained 'every detail,' signaling concise completeness, the opposite of verbosity, redundancy, or digression."},{"id":"vocab_302","entry":"byzantine","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Extremely complicated, intricate, and secretive, like the bureaucracy of the Byzantine Empire.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or relating to the Byzantine Empire or its style.","passage":"The grant application process was so ______ that three researchers spent six months and still could not identify which office held final approval.","correctAnswer":"byzantine","distractors":["transparent","straightforward","minimal"],"explanation":"'Byzantine' describes an overly complex, convoluted system. 'Six months' and 'could not identify which office held final approval' show labyrinthine complexity, the opposite of a transparent, straightforward, or minimal process."},{"id":"vocab_303","entry":"castigate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To reprimand or criticize someone severely.","secondaryMeaning":"To punish or rebuke harshly.","passage":"The editorial did not merely disagree with the senator; it ______ her in the harshest terms, calling her conduct a betrayal of the public trust.","correctAnswer":"castigate","distractors":["commend","vindicate","excuse"],"explanation":"'Castigate' means to criticize severely. 'Harshest terms' and 'betrayal of the public trust' signal a severe rebuke, not commendation, vindication, or excuse-making."},{"id":"vocab_304","entry":"candor","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"The quality of being open, honest, and frank in speech or expression.","secondaryMeaning":"Sincerity; unreserved honesty.","passage":"The memoirist's ______ surprised even her family: she described her father's addiction and her own failures with no attempt to soften the account.","correctAnswer":"candor","distractors":["evasion","deceit","reticence"],"explanation":"'Candor' means frank honesty. 'Described her father's addiction and her own failures' with 'no attempt to soften' shows unreserved openness, the opposite of evasion, deceit, or reticence."},{"id":"vocab_305","entry":"coarse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Rough in texture; crude or unrefined in manner or language.","secondaryMeaning":"Vulgar or lacking delicacy.","passage":"The foreman's ______ language shocked the visiting executives, who had assumed the entire crew would observe the company's new professionalism guidelines.","correctAnswer":"coarse","distractors":["refined","delicate","urbane"],"explanation":"'Coarse' means crude or unrefined. 'Shocked the visiting executives' and the contrast with 'new professionalism guidelines' show rough, vulgar language, the opposite of refined, delicate, or urbane manners."},{"id":"vocab_306","entry":"concise","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Giving a lot of information clearly and in a few words; brief and to the point.","secondaryMeaning":"Succinct; expressing much in few words.","passage":"The professor demanded ______ answers: any essay exceeding the word limit, she warned, would be returned unread.","correctAnswer":"concise","distractors":["verbose","rambling","prolix"],"explanation":"'Concise' means brief and to the point. The rule that essays 'exceeding the word limit' would be 'returned unread' enforces brevity, the opposite of verbose, rambling, or prolix writing."},{"id":"vocab_307","entry":"contemplate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To think profoundly and at length; to consider thoughtfully.","secondaryMeaning":"To look at attentively and thoughtfully.","passage":"Rather than answer at once, the architect sat in silence for a full minute to ______ how the slope of the roof would behave under heavy snow.","correctAnswer":"contemplate","distractors":["disregard","dismiss","ignore"],"explanation":"'Contemplate' means to consider thoughtfully. 'Sat in silence for a full minute' before answering shows deep reflection on the roof slope, not disregard, dismissal, or ignoring it."},{"id":"vocab_308","entry":"demonstrate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To show clearly and deliberately; to prove or make evident by reasoning or evidence.","secondaryMeaning":"To display or illustrate.","passage":"The trial results ______ that the new drug reduces recovery time by nearly half, a finding the researchers replicated across three independent cohorts.","correctAnswer":"demonstrate","distractors":["conceal","obscure","contradict"],"explanation":"'Demonstrate' means to show or prove clearly. 'Reduces recovery time by nearly half' and 'replicated across three independent cohorts' constitute evidence that shows the result, the opposite of concealing, obscuring, or contradicting it."},{"id":"vocab_309","entry":"diligent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Showing careful and persistent effort in work or duty.","secondaryMeaning":"Hardworking and attentive to detail.","passage":"The archivist was so ______ that she cross-checked every catalog entry against three sources before allowing any document to leave the vault.","correctAnswer":"diligent","distractors":["negligent","indolent","careless"],"explanation":"'Diligent' means carefully and persistently hardworking. 'Cross-checked every catalog entry against three sources' shows meticulous effort, the opposite of negligent, indolent, or careless behavior."},{"id":"vocab_310","entry":"dubious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Hesitating or doubtful; not to be relied upon.","secondaryMeaning":"Suspicious or questionable in quality.","passage":"The auditor regarded the company's sudden profit reports as ______, noting that its cash reserves had shrunk even as its ledgers showed record gains.","correctAnswer":"dubious","distractors":["certain","unfailing","indisputable"],"explanation":"'Dubious' means doubtful or suspect. The contradiction between 'record gains' and 'shrinking cash reserves' makes the reports suspect, the opposite of certain, unfailing, or indisputable figures."},{"id":"vocab_311","entry":"eclectic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Deriving ideas or style from a broad and diverse range of sources.","secondaryMeaning":"Composed of elements drawn from various sources.","passage":"Her reading list was ______: on a single weekend she worked through a quantum physics primer, a medieval chronicle, and a collection of contemporary poems.","correctAnswer":"eclectic","distractors":["uniform","monolithic","homogeneous"],"explanation":"'Eclectic' means drawn from diverse sources. The mix of 'quantum physics primer,' 'medieval chronicle,' and 'contemporary poems' spans unrelated fields, the opposite of uniform, monolithic, or homogeneous reading."},{"id":"vocab_312","entry":"egalitarian","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Believing in the principle that all people are equal and deserve equal rights and opportunities.","secondaryMeaning":"Favoring social equality.","passage":"The founder insisted on an ______ structure: every employee, from janitor to vice president, ate in the same cafeteria and sat in on the same strategy meetings.","correctAnswer":"egalitarian","distractors":["hierarchical","elitist","authoritarian"],"explanation":"'Egalitarian' describes commitment to equality. 'Every employee...ate in the same cafeteria' and sat in 'the same strategy meetings' removes hierarchy, the opposite of hierarchical, elitist, or authoritarian structures."},{"id":"vocab_313","entry":"empathy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"The ability to understand and share the feelings of another.","secondaryMeaning":"Vicarious identification with another's experience.","passage":"The counselor's ______ was her greatest asset: patients said she made them feel not just heard but genuinely understood.","correctAnswer":"empathy","distractors":["apathy","antipathy","indifference"],"explanation":"'Empathy' is the ability to share another's feelings. 'Made them feel not just heard but genuinely understood' signals shared understanding, the opposite of apathy, antipathy, or indifference."},{"id":"vocab_314","entry":"expedient","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Convenient and practical, although possibly improper or immoral.","secondaryMeaning":"Suitable for achieving a particular end, often regardless of principle.","passage":"The mayor's decision to release the controversial memo late on a Friday was politically ______, burying the story over the weekend even if it violated the council's transparency pledge.","correctAnswer":"expedient","distractors":["principled","honorable","idealistic"],"explanation":"'Expedient' means practically convenient but often morally questionable. 'Burying the story' while 'violating the transparency pledge' shows convenience over principle, the opposite of principled, honorable, or idealistic action."},{"id":"vocab_315","entry":"extol","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To praise enthusiastically and publicly.","secondaryMeaning":"To laud or glorify.","passage":"The commencement speaker did not merely thank the donors; she ______ them at length, crediting their gifts with making the entire research wing possible.","correctAnswer":"extol","distractors":["denounce","disparage","censure"],"explanation":"'Extol' means to praise enthusiastically. 'Crediting their gifts with making the entire research wing possible' is lavish praise, the opposite of denouncing, disparaging, or censuring them."},{"id":"vocab_316","entry":"facilitate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To make an action or process easier; to help bring about.","secondaryMeaning":"To assist the progress of.","passage":"The mediator's job was to ______ dialogue between the two sides, not to impose a settlement that neither party would accept.","correctAnswer":"facilitate","distractors":["impede","hinder","obstruct"],"explanation":"'Facilitate' means to make easier or assist progress. 'Make dialogue between the two sides' easier contrasts with imposing a settlement and is the opposite of impeding, hindering, or obstructing it."},{"id":"vocab_317","entry":"fervor","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Intense and passionate feeling; zeal.","secondaryMeaning":"Intense heat or glow.","passage":"The campaign volunteer spoke with such ______ that passersby who had ignored canvassers all summer stopped to sign her petition.","correctAnswer":"fervor","distractors":["apathy","composure","indifference"],"explanation":"'Fervor' means intense passion. 'Stopped to sign her petition' after 'ignoring canvassers all summer' shows her passion converted the indifferent, the opposite of apathy, composure, or indifference."},{"id":"vocab_318","entry":"frugal","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Sparing or economical with money or resources; thrifty.","secondaryMeaning":"Simple and plain, costing little.","passage":"Despite her fortune, the philanthropist lived ______, mending the same wool coat for twenty years while donating millions to the local library.","correctAnswer":"frugal","distractors":["extravagant","lavish","improvident"],"explanation":"'Frugal' means thrifty and economical. 'Mending the same wool coat for twenty years' while rich shows thrift, the opposite of extravagant, lavish, or improvident spending."},{"id":"vocab_319","entry":"germane","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Relevant to a subject under consideration; pertinent.","secondaryMeaning":"Closely related to the matter at hand.","passage":"The judge repeatedly warned the witness to keep her testimony ______, reminding her that her grievances with the prosecutor had no bearing on the charge at hand.","correctAnswer":"germane","distractors":["irrelevant","tangential","extraneous"],"explanation":"'Germane' means relevant and pertinent. 'No bearing on the charge at hand' defines the off-topic testimony the judge is warning against, the opposite of irrelevant, tangential, or extraneous."},{"id":"vocab_320","entry":"guile","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Sly or cunning intelligence; deceitful cunning.","secondaryMeaning":"Treacherous craftiness.","passage":"The defendant used considerable ______ to hide his assets, transferring ownership to shell companies he had established years before any audit.","correctAnswer":"guile","distractors":["candor","sincerity","honesty"],"explanation":"'Guile' means cunning deceit. 'Transferring ownership to shell companies' to 'hide his assets' is calculated deception, the opposite of candor, sincerity, or honesty."},{"id":"vocab_321","entry":"haughty","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Arrogantly superior and disdainful.","secondaryMeaning":"Proud and contemptuous of others.","passage":"The duchess swept past the guests with a ______ glance that acknowledged none of them, as if they were staff who had wandered into the wrong room.","correctAnswer":"haughty","distractors":["humble","gracious","modest"],"explanation":"'Haughty' means arrogantly disdainful. 'Acknowledged none of them' as if they were 'staff' shows superiority and disdain, the opposite of humble, gracious, or modest behavior."},{"id":"vocab_322","entry":"hinder","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To create difficulties for someone or something, resulting in delay or obstruction.","secondaryMeaning":"To impede or hamper.","passage":"Poor road conditions continued to ______ the relief effort, leaving supply trucks stranded for hours on routes that should have taken minutes.","correctAnswer":"hinder","distractors":["facilitate","advance","accelerate"],"explanation":"'Hinder' means to obstruct or delay. 'Supply trucks stranded for hours' on 'routes that should have taken minutes' shows delay, the opposite of facilitating, advancing, or accelerating the effort."},{"id":"vocab_323","entry":"hyperbole","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Exaggerated statements or claims not meant to be taken literally, used for emphasis.","secondaryMeaning":"A figure of speech using deliberate exaggeration.","passage":"Calling the storm 'the worst disaster in human history' was pure ______, as records showed at least a dozen comparable hurricanes within the past century.","correctAnswer":"hyperbole","distractors":["understatement","litotes","fact"],"explanation":"'Hyperbole' is deliberate exaggeration. 'At least a dozen comparable hurricanes' shows the claim is not literally true but overstated for effect, the opposite of understatement, litotes, or fact."},{"id":"vocab_324","entry":"illuminate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To help to clarify or explain; to light up.","secondaryMeaning":"To decorate or make bright with light.","passage":"The professor's new book ______ the subtle economics behind the treaty, showing how a clause about grain tariffs had quietly redrawn the borders of two nations.","correctAnswer":"illuminate","distractors":["obfuscate","muddle","confuse"],"explanation":"'Illuminate' means to clarify. 'Showing how a clause about grain tariffs had quietly redrawn the borders' clarifies the subtle economics, the opposite of obfuscating, muddling, or confusing it."},{"id":"vocab_325","entry":"impartial","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Treating all rivals or disputants equally; fair and just.","secondaryMeaning":"Not favoring one side over another.","passage":"The ombudsman prided herself on being ______, recusing herself from any case involving a former colleague, no matter how minor the connection.","correctAnswer":"impartial","distractors":["biased","partisan","prejudiced"],"explanation":"'Impartial' means fair and unbiased. 'Recusing herself from any case involving a former colleague' shows deliberate neutrality, the opposite of biased, partisan, or prejudiced conduct."},{"id":"vocab_326","entry":"impede","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To delay or prevent someone or something by obstructing them; to hinder.","secondaryMeaning":"To stand in the way of.","passage":"Layers of bureaucratic paperwork continued to ______ the rollout, so that a vaccine approved in January did not reach the first patient until June.","correctAnswer":"impede","distractors":["accelerate","facilitate","promote"],"explanation":"'Impede' means to obstruct or delay. The vaccine 'approved in January' reached 'the first patient' only 'in June' shows severe delay, the opposite of accelerating, facilitating, or promoting the rollout."},{"id":"vocab_327","entry":"inadvertent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Not resulting from or achieved through deliberate planning; unintentional.","secondaryMeaning":"Accidental; heedless.","passage":"The aide insisted his email to the rival campaign was ______, the result of an autocomplete filling in the wrong address as he rushed to send the memo.","correctAnswer":"inadvertent","distractors":["deliberate","calculated","intentional"],"explanation":"'Inadvertent' means unintentional. 'Autocomplete filling in the wrong address' as he 'rushed' frames the email as an accident, the opposite of deliberate, calculated, or intentional action."},{"id":"vocab_328","entry":"indolent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Wanting or enjoying idleness; lazy and disliking effort.","secondaryMeaning":"Slow to develop or grow.","passage":"The board fired the ______ manager, who had let inventory rot in the warehouse for months while spending his afternoons at the racetrack.","correctAnswer":"indolent","distractors":["industrious","assiduous","diligent"],"explanation":"'Indolent' means lazy. 'Let inventory rot for months' while 'at the racetrack' shows idleness, the opposite of industrious, assiduous, or diligent management."},{"id":"vocab_329","entry":"insidious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Proceeding in a gradual, subtle way but with harmful effects; treacherous.","secondaryMeaning":"Awaiting a favorable opportunity to cause harm; stealthy.","passage":"The mold in the tenants' walls was ______, releasing spores so slowly that no one noticed until several children were hospitalized for respiratory failure.","correctAnswer":"insidious","distractors":["overt","blatant","manifest"],"explanation":"'Insidious' means gradually harmful in a stealthy way. 'Releasing spores so slowly that no one noticed' until 'hospitalized' shows gradual, hidden harm, the opposite of overt, blatant, or manifest damage."},{"id":"vocab_330","entry":"judicious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Showing, done with, or resulting from good judgment or sense.","secondaryMeaning":"Prudent and sensible.","passage":"By making ______ cuts to nonessential programs rather than slashing across the board, the mayor balanced the budget without laying off a single teacher.","correctAnswer":"judicious","distractors":["reckless","imprudent","rash"],"explanation":"'Judicious' means showing good judgment. 'Cuts to nonessential programs' rather than 'slashing across the board' shows careful discrimination, the opposite of reckless, imprudent, or rash cuts."},{"id":"vocab_331","entry":"lucid","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Expressed clearly and easy to understand; marked by clarity of thought.","secondaryMeaning":"Bright or luminous.","passage":"Even at ninety-four, the physicist's prose remained ______, able to explain quantum entanglement to a general audience in a single paragraph.","correctAnswer":"lucid","distractors":["opaque","muddled","abstruse"],"explanation":"'Lucid' means clear and easily understood. 'Explain quantum entanglement to a general audience in a single paragraph' shows clarity, the opposite of opaque, muddled, or abstruse writing."},{"id":"vocab_332","entry":"manifest","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Clear or obvious to the eye or mind; plainly apparent.","secondaryMeaning":"To display or show (verb form).","passage":"The cracks in the bridge's supports were ______ to any passing engineer, yet the city waited two more years before ordering a full inspection.","correctAnswer":"manifest","distractors":["obscure","covert","ambiguous"],"explanation":"'Manifest' means clearly apparent. 'To any passing engineer' shows the cracks were plainly visible to all, even though 'the city waited two more years,' the opposite of obscure, covert, or ambiguous signs."},{"id":"vocab_333","entry":"nuance","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"A subtle difference in or shade of meaning, expression, or sound.","secondaryMeaning":"A fine distinction.","passage":"The translator struggled to preserve every ______ of the original poem, knowing that a single shade of feeling in one language might have no equivalent word in the other.","correctAnswer":"nuance","distractors":["generalization","simplification","stereotype"],"explanation":"'Nuance' is a subtle shade of meaning. 'A single shade of feeling...might have no equivalent word' signals fine distinctions the translator must preserve, the opposite of generalization, simplification, or stereotype."},{"id":"vocab_334","entry":"obstinate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Stubbornly refusing to change one's opinion or course of action.","secondaryMeaning":"Difficult to manage or treat.","passage":"The negotiator found the delegate utterly ______, refusing to concede a single clause even when his own advisors urged a compromise that would save the treaty.","correctAnswer":"obstinate","distractors":["compliant","yielding","tractable"],"explanation":"'Obstinate' means stubbornly unyielding. 'Refusing to concede a single clause' against 'his own advisors' advice' shows stubbornness, the opposite of compliant, yielding, or tractable negotiation."},{"id":"vocab_335","entry":"opaque","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Not transparent or translucent; hard to understand or explain.","secondaryMeaning":"Dull or stupid (rare).","passage":"The prime minister's statement on the trade deficit was deliberately ______, vague enough to satisfy both free-traders and protectionists without committing to either.","correctAnswer":"opaque","distractors":["transparent","limpid","lucid"],"explanation":"'Opaque' means hard to understand. 'Vague enough to satisfy both free-traders and protectionists' shows deliberate unclarity, the opposite of transparent, limpid, or lucid statements."},{"id":"vocab_336","entry":"pervasive","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Spreading widely throughout an area or group of people; prevalent.","secondaryMeaning":"Permeating; found everywhere.","passage":"A sense of unease became ______ throughout the city after the third unexplained blackout in a month, even though officials insisted the grid was sound.","correctAnswer":"pervasive","distractors":["marginal","isolated","scarce"],"explanation":"'Pervasive' means spreading widely. 'Throughout the city' after repeated blackouts shows the unease spread everywhere, the opposite of marginal, isolated, or scarce worry."},{"id":"vocab_337","entry":"plausible","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Seeming reasonable or probable; believable.","secondaryMeaning":"Superficially fair or valid though perhaps not actually so.","passage":"The detective dismissed three of the four suspects' alibis as fantasy, but the fourth account was ______ enough that she assigned two officers to verify every detail.","correctAnswer":"plausible","distractors":["implausible","preposterous","absurd"],"explanation":"'Plausible' means believable. 'Assigned two officers to verify every detail' shows the alibi merited serious investigation, the opposite of implausible, preposterous, or absurd stories that were dismissed at once."},{"id":"vocab_338","entry":"preclude","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To prevent something from happening or someone from doing something; to make impossible.","secondaryMeaning":"To exclude or rule out in advance.","passage":"The bylaws did not ______ a candidate's relatives from voting, a loophole the challenger exploited by seating three cousins on the election board.","correctAnswer":"preclude","distractors":["enable","permit","allow"],"explanation":"'Preclude' means to prevent or make impossible. 'A loophole' that allowed 'relatives from voting' shows the bylaws did NOT prevent it, the opposite of enabling, permitting, or allowing (which describe what the loophole did, not what it prevented)."},{"id":"vocab_339","entry":"profound","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":1,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Very great or intense; deep in knowledge or insight.","secondaryMeaning":"Going far beneath the surface.","passage":"The novelist's essay had a ______ effect on the debate, reframing a question of traffic policy as one of who in a city has the right to be heard.","correctAnswer":"profound","distractors":["superficial","trivial","shallow"],"explanation":"'Profound' means deep or far-reaching. 'Reframing a question of traffic policy' into a question of civic rights shows deep, far-reaching impact, the opposite of superficial, trivial, or shallow influence."},{"id":"vocab_340","entry":"reconcile","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To restore friendly relations between; to make consistent or compatible.","secondaryMeaning":"To settle or resolve a conflict.","passage":"After years of silence, the two brothers agreed to ______ over their father's grave, each admitting the grudges they had carried had cost them more than the original quarrel.","correctAnswer":"reconcile","distractors":["alienate","estrange","divide"],"explanation":"'Reconcile' means to restore friendly relations. 'Admitting the grudges they had carried had cost them' shows two sides repairing their relationship, the opposite of alienating, estranging, or dividing each other further."},{"id":"vocab_341","entry":"reprehensible","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Deserving strong censure or condemnation; morally objectionable.","secondaryMeaning":"Blameworthy; disgraceful.","passage":"The court found the company's decision to dump waste into the river ______, fining it an amount that exceeded its profits from the entire venture.","correctAnswer":"reprehensible","distractors":["commendable","laudable","exemplary"],"explanation":"'Reprehensible' means morally blameworthy. 'Dumping waste into the river' that drew a fine 'exceeding profits' shows conduct the court condemned, the opposite of commendable, laudable, or exemplary behavior."},{"id":"vocab_342","entry":"stringent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Strict, precise, and exacting; demanding rigorous compliance.","secondaryMeaning":"Tightly controlled or regulated.","passage":"Because the lab handled live pathogens, it followed ______ safety protocols: a single unsealed container would trigger an automatic shutdown of the entire wing.","correctAnswer":"stringent","distractors":["lax","lenient","permissive"],"explanation":"'Stringent' means strict and exacting. 'A single unsealed container' triggering 'automatic shutdown' shows rigorously enforced rules, the opposite of lax, lenient, or permissive protocols."},{"id":"vocab_343","entry":"succinct","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Briefly and clearly expressed; concise and to the point.","secondaryMeaning":"Compressed into a small space.","passage":"The judge's instructions to the jury were ______: two sentences that named the charge, the burden of proof, and the consequence of reasonable doubt, and nothing more.","correctAnswer":"succinct","distractors":["verbose","prolix","diffuse"],"explanation":"'Succinct' means concise. 'Two sentences' covering 'the charge, the burden of proof, and the consequence' with 'nothing more' shows compression, the opposite of verbose, prolix, or diffuse instructions."},{"id":"vocab_344","entry":"vacillate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To waver between different opinions or actions; to be indecisive.","secondaryMeaning":"To swing indecisively from one position to another.","passage":"The council continued to ______ for weeks, voting to fund the bridge on Tuesday and to delay it on Thursday, until the federal grant expired entirely.","correctAnswer":"vacillate","distractors":["persevere","persist","resolve"],"explanation":"'Vacillate' means to waver indecisively. 'Voting to fund the bridge on Tuesday and to delay it on Thursday' shows oscillation, the opposite of persevering, persisting, or resolving on a course."},{"id":"vocab_345","entry":"vindicate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To clear someone of blame or suspicion; to justify or prove right.","secondaryMeaning":"To defend or maintain against objection.","passage":"Years after her dismissal, the release of the internal audit ______ the whistleblower, showing that the numbers she had questioned had indeed been falsified.","correctAnswer":"vindicate","distractors":["incriminate","condemn","implicate"],"explanation":"'Vindicate' means to clear of blame. 'Showing that the numbers she had questioned had indeed been falsified' proves she was right all along, the opposite of incriminating, condemning, or implicating her."},{"id":"vocab_346","entry":"zealous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Showing or characterized by great energy or enthusiasm in pursuit of a cause.","secondaryMeaning":"Fervently devoted.","passage":"The librarian was a ______ collector of first editions, traveling to estate sales across three continents to outbid rivals for a single fragile volume.","correctAnswer":"zealous","distractors":["apathetic","indifferent","lethargic"],"explanation":"'Zealous' means fervently enthusiastic. 'Traveling across three continents to outbid rivals for a single volume' shows passionate devotion, the opposite of apathetic, indifferent, or lethargic collecting."},{"id":"vocab_347","entry":"mitigate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To make less severe, intense, or painful; to moderate or soften.","secondaryMeaning":"To lessen the force or intensity of something harmful.","passage":"The mayor argued the new transit policy would ______ the worst of the congestion, though critics insisted it would merely shift traffic onto narrower side streets.","correctAnswer":"mitigate","distractors":["exacerbate","amplify","aggravate"],"explanation":"'Mitigate' means to make less severe. 'The worst of the congestion' being lessened is the goal; exacerbating, amplifying, or aggravating the congestion would all make it worse, not better."},{"id":"vocab_348","entry":"understate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To describe or represent as less important or smaller than in reality; to minimize.","secondaryMeaning":"To state with insufficient emphasis.","passage":"To call the wildfire 'a little smoky' was to ______ the disaster: forty thousand acres had burned and three towns stood under mandatory evacuation.","correctAnswer":"understate","distractors":["exaggerate","overstate","magnify"],"explanation":"'Understate' means to represent as less than it is. 'A little smoky' for forty thousand acres of fire is a gross minimization, the opposite of exaggerating, overstating, or magnifying it."},{"id":"vocab_349","entry":"condone","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To accept or allow behavior that is morally wrong to continue; to overlook or forgive.","secondaryMeaning":"To regard as harmless or permissible.","passage":"The school refused to ______ the bullying, expelling two students and rewriting its code of conduct so that harassment became an automatic, non-negotiable offense.","correctAnswer":"condone","distractors":["condemn","prohibit","censure"],"explanation":"'Condone' means to accept or overlook wrongdoing. The school 'expelling two students and rewriting its code' shows it would NOT tolerate the bullying, the opposite of condemning, prohibiting, or censuring it (all of which also oppose it)."},{"id":"vocab_350","entry":"eschew","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To deliberately avoid or abstain from; to shun.","secondaryMeaning":"To habitually reject or stay away from.","passage":"The architect chose to ______ ornament entirely, designing a chapel of plain concrete and unadorned wood that let the mountain light do all the work.","correctAnswer":"eschew","distractors":["embrace","adopt","pursue"],"explanation":"'Eschew' means to deliberately avoid. 'Plain concrete and unadorned wood' shows the architect rejected ornament, the opposite of embracing, adopting, or pursuing it."},{"id":"vocab_351","entry":"obfuscate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To make unclear, obscure, or confused; to intentionally make something hard to understand.","secondaryMeaning":"To render confusing or evasive.","passage":"The witness's answers seemed designed to ______ rather than inform, piling irrelevant detail onto every question until the jury could not recall what had been asked.","correctAnswer":"obfuscate","distractors":["clarify","elucidate","illuminate"],"explanation":"'Obfuscate' means to make unclear. 'Piling irrelevant detail' until the jury 'could not recall what had been asked' shows deliberate confusion, the opposite of clarifying, elucidating, or illuminating the matter."},{"id":"vocab_352","entry":"vilify","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To speak or write about in an abusively disparaging manner; to defame.","secondaryMeaning":"To attack with abusive language.","passage":"After the famine, opposition newspapers began to ______ the minister of agriculture, blaming him personally for every death the relief agencies could not reach in time.","correctAnswer":"vilify","distractors":["praise","extol","vindicate"],"explanation":"'Vilify' means to defame or attack verbally. 'Blaming him personally for every death' is harsh public disparagement, the opposite of praising, extolling, or vindicating him."},{"id":"vocab_353","entry":"conflate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To combine two or more different things, often erroneously, into a single one; to fail to distinguish.","secondaryMeaning":"To merge together things that should be kept separate.","passage":"The report tended to ______ correlation with causation, concluding that because heavy readers earned more, buying books would raise a child's future income.","correctAnswer":"conflate","distractors":["distinguish","separate","differentiate"],"explanation":"'Conflate' means to wrongly merge distinct ideas. Treating 'correlation' (a statistical link) as 'causation' (a direct cause) merges two different relationships, the opposite of distinguishing, separating, or differentiating them."},{"id":"vocab_354","entry":"amalgamate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To combine or unite to form one organization or structure; to merge.","secondaryMeaning":"To blend into a unified whole.","passage":"The two rival railways voted to ______, a merger that would create a single line running from the coast to the mountains and end a century of duplicated schedules.","correctAnswer":"amalgamate","distractors":["separate","divide","splinter"],"explanation":"'Amalgamate' means to merge into one. 'A single line' ending 'a century of duplicated schedules' is a merger, the opposite of separating, dividing, or splintering the railways."},{"id":"vocab_355","entry":"juxtapose","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To place close together, especially to compare or contrast; to set side by side.","secondaryMeaning":"To bring together for the sake of comparison.","passage":"The photographer chose to ______ the gleaming new skyscraper with the crumbling tenement beside it, framing both in a single shot that became the cover of the housing report.","correctAnswer":"juxtapose","distractors":["isolate","separate","conceal"],"explanation":"'Juxtapose' means to place side by side for comparison. Framing 'the gleaming new skyscraper' with 'the crumbling tenement' in 'a single shot' sets them side by side, the opposite of isolating, separating, or concealing them."},{"id":"vocab_356","entry":"recapitulate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To summarize and restate the main points of; to review briefly.","secondaryMeaning":"To repeat the principal points of an argument.","passage":"At the close of every lecture, the professor would ______ each major argument in three sentences, a habit her students relied on before each examination.","correctAnswer":"recapitulate","distractors":["expand","elaborate","digress"],"explanation":"'Recapitulate' means to summarize. Restating 'each major argument in three sentences' at the close compresses the lecture, the opposite of expanding, elaborating, or digressing from it."},{"id":"vocab_357","entry":"explicate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To analyze and develop in detail; to give a thorough explanation of.","secondaryMeaning":"To unfold the meaning of an obscure or difficult passage.","passage":"The seminar asked students not merely to read the poem but to ______ its imagery line by line, explaining how each metaphor reinforced the speaker's grief.","correctAnswer":"explicate","distractors":["gloss","obscure","skim"],"explanation":"'Explicate' means to analyze in detail. 'Line by line, explaining how each metaphor reinforced the speaker's grief' is thorough analysis, the opposite of glossing over, obscuring, or skimming the poem."},{"id":"vocab_358","entry":"gainsay","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To deny or contradict; to declare to be untrue.","secondaryMeaning":"To oppose or speak against.","passage":"No witness dared ______ the general's account of the battle, though several officers knew his timeline of the retreat was wrong by a full day.","correctAnswer":"gainsay","distractors":["affirm","confirm","concede"],"explanation":"'Gainsay' means to deny or contradict. The officers 'knew his timeline was wrong' but 'no witness dared' deny it openly; affirming, confirming, or conceding it would mean agreeing with the false account."},{"id":"vocab_359","entry":"palliate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To make a disease, pain, or problem less severe without curing it; to alleviate temporarily.","secondaryMeaning":"To cover or excuse with a show of reason.","passage":"The clinic could only ______ the patient's pain with morphine, since the underlying cancer had spread beyond any treatment the hospital could offer.","correctAnswer":"palliate","distractors":["worsen","aggravate","exacerbate"],"explanation":"'Palliate' means to lessen without curing. Morphine easing pain while 'the underlying cancer had spread beyond any treatment' is relief without a cure, the opposite of worsening, aggravating, or exacerbating it."},{"id":"vocab_360","entry":"discount","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To deduct from an account; to disregard or dismiss as unreliable or exaggerated.","secondaryMeaning":"To reduce the significance of; to sell below full price.","passage":"The historian warned readers not to ______ the diarist's account simply because she was a servant, arguing that her view of the household was in some ways more honest than the master's.","correctAnswer":"discount","distractors":["credit","accept","trust"],"explanation":"'Discount' here means to disregard as unreliable. The historian argues readers should NOT dismiss the servant's account merely for her station; crediting, accepting, or trusting it would mean taking it seriously rather than dismissing it."},{"id":"vocab_361","entry":"deflect","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To turn aside from a straight direction or course; to turn attention away from an awkward subject.","secondaryMeaning":"To cause to change direction.","passage":"Asked about the missing funds, the treasurer tried to ______ with a long anecdote about office renovations that had nothing to do with the question.","correctAnswer":"deflect","distractors":["address","confront","engage"],"explanation":"'Deflect' means to turn attention aside. The treasurer's 'long anecdote about office renovations' dodges the question about 'missing funds', the opposite of addressing, confronting, or engaging the subject directly."},{"id":"vocab_362","entry":"undergird","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To strengthen, support, or hold up from below; to provide a firm foundation for.","secondaryMeaning":"To bolster from underneath.","passage":"A shared suspicion of distant government continued to ______ the town's politics, holding together factions that agreed on almost nothing else.","correctAnswer":"undergird","distractors":["undermine","weaken","topple"],"explanation":"'Undergird' means to support from below. 'Holding together factions that agreed on almost nothing else' shows the suspicion is the structural support, the opposite of undermining, weakening, or toppling that unity."},{"id":"vocab_363","entry":"calibrate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To adjust precisely for a particular function; to assess or measure carefully against a standard.","secondaryMeaning":"To mark the graduations of a measuring instrument.","passage":"Before releasing the poll, the firm had to ______ its sample for the undercount of voters without landlines, a group that skewed a decade younger than those it reached by phone.","correctAnswer":"calibrate","distractors":["estimate","guess","approximate"],"explanation":"'Calibrate' means to adjust precisely. 'Adjust its sample for the undercount' requires exact correction against the standard population, the opposite of merely estimating, guessing, or approximating it."},{"id":"vocab_364","entry":"denigrate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To criticize unfairly; to disparage or belittle, especially to damage reputation.","secondaryMeaning":"To blacken someone's character.","passage":"The rival physicist did not merely disagree with the theory; he tried to ______ its author, suggesting in print that the results had been fabricated rather than merely mistaken.","correctAnswer":"denigrate","distractors":["praise","extol","laud"],"explanation":"'Denigrate' means to belittle unfairly. 'Suggesting in print that the results had been fabricated' attacks the author's character, the opposite of praising, extolling, or lauding him."},{"id":"vocab_365","entry":"stigmatize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To describe or regard as worthy of disgrace or great disapproval; to brand with shame.","secondaryMeaning":"To mark out as discreditable.","passage":"In earlier centuries, societies would ______ unwed mothers so thoroughly that their children were barred from schools and the mothers from any respectable work.","correctAnswer":"stigmatize","distractors":["normalize","accept","endorse"],"explanation":"'Stigmatize' means to mark with disgrace. 'Children barred from schools' and mothers excluded 'from any respectable work' show the mothers branded with shame, the opposite of normalizing, accepting, or endorsing their situation."},{"id":"vocab_366","entry":"lionize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"To treat as a celebrity; to give a great deal of public attention and approval to.","secondaryMeaning":"To elevate to the status of a hero.","passage":"Returning with the gold medal, the skier found her hometown ready to ______ her, renaming the main street in her honor and scheduling a parade for the first snow.","correctAnswer":"lionize","distractors":["ignore","scorn","belittle"],"explanation":"'Lionize' means to treat as a celebrity. 'Renaming the main street' and 'scheduling a parade' are the gestures of public adoration, the opposite of ignoring, scorning, or belittling her."},{"id":"vocab_367","entry":"affect","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To influence or make a difference to; to have an effect on.","secondaryMeaning":"(noun, psychology) An expressed or observed emotional response.","passage":"How a teacher frames a question can ______ a student's confidence for years, turning a single classroom exchange into either a beginning or an ending.","correctAnswer":"affect","distractors":["effect","inflict","infect"],"explanation":"'Affect' (verb) means to influence. 'A student's confidence' being shaped by 'how a teacher frames a question' is influence; 'effect' (the noun 'result') is the confusable partner but does not fit the verb slot. 'Inflict' and 'infect' do not mean to influence generally."},{"id":"vocab_368","entry":"effect","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A result or consequence; a change produced by a cause.","secondaryMeaning":"(verb) To bring about or accomplish.","passage":"The ______ of the new law was almost invisible at first: only after a year did the rate of teenage employment begin its slow, steady climb.","correctAnswer":"effect","distractors":["affect","outcome","intent"],"explanation":"'Effect' (noun) means a result. 'The rate of teenage employment began its climb' is the consequence of the law; 'affect' (the verb 'influence') is the confusable partner but cannot occupy a noun slot. 'Outcome' is a synonym that would lose the precision, and 'intent' names a goal, not a result."},{"id":"vocab_369","entry":"complement","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Something that completes or brings to perfection; one of two parts that fit together.","secondaryMeaning":"(verb) To add to something so as to enhance or complete it.","passage":"The dry acidity of the lemon was the perfect ______ to the rich sweetness of the butter, each making the other taste sharper than it could alone.","correctAnswer":"complement","distractors":["compliment","supplement","substitute"],"explanation":"'Complement' means something that completes perfectly. The lemon and butter 'each making the other taste sharper' are completing partners; 'compliment' (praise) is the confusable partner. 'Supplement' (an added extra) and 'substitute' (a replacement) do not capture the mutual completion."},{"id":"vocab_370","entry":"compliment","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A polite expression of praise or admiration; a courteous remark.","secondaryMeaning":"(verb) To politely praise or congratulate.","passage":"The chef accepted the diner's ______ with a small bow, then returned to the kitchen where the night's real work was only half finished.","correctAnswer":"compliment","distractors":["complement","criticism","rebuke"],"explanation":"'Compliment' means an expression of praise. 'The chef accepted' the diner's remark 'with a small bow' fits a praising remark; 'complement' (a completing part) is the confusable partner. 'Criticism' and 'rebuke' are the opposite of praise."},{"id":"vocab_371","entry":"principal","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"The most important person in an organization; a head of a school; a leading financial sum.","secondaryMeaning":"(adjective) First in order of importance.","passage":"When the fight spilled into the hallway, the ______ suspended both students before noon and called each family before the buses had even left the lot.","correctAnswer":"principal","distractors":["principle","teacher","parent"],"explanation":"'Principal' (noun) here means the head of a school. 'Suspended both students and called each family' is the school head's authority; 'principle' (a rule) is the confusable partner but cannot act. 'Teacher' and 'parent' do not have suspension authority."},{"id":"vocab_372","entry":"principle","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A fundamental truth or rule of conduct; a moral belief guiding behavior.","secondaryMeaning":"A general law or doctrine from which others are derived.","passage":"Refusing the bribe was a matter of ______ for the judge, who said she would rather lose the bench than rule against her conscience for any amount of money.","correctAnswer":"principle","distractors":["principal","prejudice","profit"],"explanation":"'Principle' means a guiding moral belief. 'Rather lose the bench than rule against her conscience' is conduct guided by conviction; 'principal' (school head) is the confusable partner. 'Prejudice' is a bias and 'profit' is gain, neither a moral rule."},{"id":"vocab_373","entry":"council","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"An advisory, deliberative, or legislative body of people meeting to decide or advise.","secondaryMeaning":"An assembly convened for consultation.","passage":"The city ______ voted six to three to close the library on Sundays, a decision the mayor signed the next morning without comment.","correctAnswer":"council","distractors":["counsel","committee","clerk"],"explanation":"'Council' means a deliberative body. 'Voted six to three' is an assembly's action; 'counsel' (advice) is the confusable partner but cannot vote. 'Committee' is near but loses the elected-body sense, and 'clerk' is a single staffer."},{"id":"vocab_374","entry":"counsel","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Advice given formally; the legal advocate or advocates representing a party.","secondaryMeaning":"(verb) To give advice to.","passage":"Against the ______ of every advisor, the founder rejected the buyout offer, convinced the company would be worth ten times as much within a decade.","correctAnswer":"counsel","distractors":["council","command","consent"],"explanation":"'Counsel' means advice. 'Against the advice of every advisor' is the sense; 'council' (a body) is the confusable partner but cannot be 'followed against'. 'Command' is an order and 'consent' is agreement, neither advice."},{"id":"vocab_375","entry":"discreet","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Careful and circumspect in one's speech or actions, especially to avoid causing offense or drawing attention.","secondaryMeaning":"Intentionally unobtrusive; judiciously tactful.","passage":"The detective made a ______ inquiry at the hotel, asking about room numbers in the tone of a tired traveler rather than a man pursuing a suspect.","correctAnswer":"discreet","distractors":["discrete","indiscreet","obvious"],"explanation":"'Discreet' means tactful and unobtrusive. 'In the tone of a tired traveler' shows care to avoid attention; 'discrete' (separate) is the confusable partner. 'Indiscreet' and 'obvious' are the opposite of careful."},{"id":"vocab_376","entry":"discrete","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Individually separate and distinct; not continuous or blended.","secondaryMeaning":"Consisting of distinct, unconnected parts.","passage":"Theorists of the new physics treated light as a series of ______ packets of energy, rather than as one unbroken wave rolling through space.","correctAnswer":"discrete","distractors":["discreet","blended","continuous"],"explanation":"'Discrete' means separate and distinct. 'Series of packets' rather than 'one unbroken wave' shows separateness; 'discreet' (tactful) is the confusable partner. 'Blended' and 'continuous' are the opposite of separate."},{"id":"vocab_377","entry":"stationary","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Not moving or not intended to be moved; staying in one place.","secondaryMeaning":"Unchanging in condition or quantity.","passage":"For three hours the herd remained ______ at the waterhole, the calves sleeping while the adults stood motionless against the heat of the afternoon.","correctAnswer":"stationary","distractors":["stationery","mobile","moving"],"explanation":"'Stationary' means not moving. 'Stood motionless' for three hours shows no movement; 'stationery' (writing paper) is the confusable partner. 'Mobile' and 'moving' are the opposite of still."},{"id":"vocab_378","entry":"stationery","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Writing materials, such as paper, envelopes, and matching cards.","secondaryMeaning":"Paper for writing, often with matching envelopes.","passage":"Before the wedding, the bride spent two afternoons choosing the ______, settling at last on cream paper with her initials pressed in gold.","correctAnswer":"stationery","distractors":["stationary","station","stationer"],"explanation":"'Stationery' means writing materials. 'Cream paper with her initials pressed in gold' are wedding papers; 'stationary' (not moving) is the confusable partner. 'Station' (a stop) and 'stationer' (a seller) do not fit the noun slot for the paper itself."},{"id":"vocab_379","entry":"allusion","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"An indirect or passing reference to a person, work, event, or idea without explicit mention.","secondaryMeaning":"A brief, indirect mention.","passage":"The novel's opening sentence was an ______ to Genesis, naming neither the garden nor the fruit but letting the reader hear both in the wording.","correctAnswer":"allusion","distractors":["illusion","reference","quote"],"explanation":"'Allusion' means an indirect reference. 'Naming neither the garden nor the fruit' but evoking Genesis indirectly fits; 'illusion' (a false perception) is the confusable partner. 'Reference' is too direct and 'quote' is verbatim, neither indirect."},{"id":"vocab_380","entry":"illusion","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A false perception or interpretation; something that appears real but is not.","secondaryMeaning":"A deceptive appearance or impression.","passage":"The cool water shimmering at the end of the highway was only an ______, a trick of heated air that retreated as fast as the truck could chase it.","correctAnswer":"illusion","distractors":["allusion","delusion","fact"],"explanation":"'Illusion' means a false perception. 'A trick of heated air that retreated' shows a mirage, not real water; 'allusion' (a reference) is the confusable partner. 'Delusion' is a fixed false belief in the mind, not a visual trick, and 'fact' is the opposite of a false appearance."},{"id":"vocab_381","entry":"flaunt","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To display ostentatiously; to show off in a way meant to attract attention.","secondaryMeaning":"To exhibit in a conspicuous, often defiant manner.","passage":"The heir liked to ______ his new fortune at every dinner, arriving in a different car each week and naming its price before the first course was served.","correctAnswer":"flaunt","distractors":["flout","conceal","downplay"],"explanation":"'Flaunt' means to show off. 'Arriving in a different car each week and naming its price' is ostentatious display; 'flout' (to disregard a rule) is the confusable partner. 'Conceal' and 'downplay' are the opposite of showing off."},{"id":"vocab_382","entry":"flout","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To openly disregard a rule, law, or convention; to show contempt for.","secondaryMeaning":"To treat with disdainful disregard.","passage":"The factory continued to ______ the emissions limits even after three fines, releasing the same gray plume each night that the inspectors had come to measure.","correctAnswer":"flout","distractors":["flaunt","follow","respect"],"explanation":"'Flout' means to openly disregard a rule. 'Even after three fines' releasing the same plume shows contempt for the emissions limits; 'flaunt' (to show off) is the confusable partner. 'Follow' and 'respect' are the opposite of disregarding."},{"id":"vocab_383","entry":"loath","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Reluctant or unwilling to do something; disinclined.","secondaryMeaning":"Strongly averse (often 'nothing loath', willing).","passage":"The general was ______ to commit his reserves before dawn, suspecting the morning fog was concealing an enemy line his scouts had not yet found.","correctAnswer":"loath","distractors":["loathe","eager","willing"],"explanation":"'Loath' (adjective) means reluctant. 'Suspecting the morning fog was concealing an enemy line' explains the general's unwillingness to commit; 'loathe' (to hate) is the confusable partner. 'Eager' and 'willing' are the opposite of reluctant."},{"id":"vocab_384","entry":"loathe","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To feel intense dislike or disgust for; to detest.","secondaryMeaning":"To abhor.","passage":"By the end of the campaign, the soldiers had come to ______ the mud more than the enemy, since the mud never slept and the enemy at least sometimes did.","correctAnswer":"loathe","distractors":["loath","adore","admire"],"explanation":"'Loathe' (verb) means to hate. 'Had come to hate the mud more than the enemy' shows intense dislike; 'loath' (reluctant, an adjective) is the confusable partner. 'Adore' and 'admire' are the opposite of hating."},{"id":"vocab_385","entry":"faze","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To disturb, disconcert, or cause to lose composure; to put off.","secondaryMeaning":"To daunt or discompose.","passage":"Nothing seemed to ______ the surgeon, not the long hours nor the sudden bleating of the alarm; she would glance at the monitor and resume her sutures as if it were birdsong.","correctAnswer":"faze","distractors":["phase","calm","steady"],"explanation":"'Faze' means to disconcert. 'Nothing seemed to disturb the surgeon' fits; 'phase' (a stage) is the confusable partner. 'Calm' and 'steady' are the opposite of disturbing."},{"id":"vocab_386","entry":"phase","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A distinct period or stage in a process of change or development.","secondaryMeaning":"(verb) To carry out in stages.","passage":"The country had entered the most dangerous ______ of the epidemic, when the first wave had passed but the hospitals had not yet restocked for the second.","correctAnswer":"phase","distractors":["faze","stage","face"],"explanation":"'Phase' means a distinct stage. 'The most dangerous period between the first wave and the restock' fits; 'faze' (to disturb) is the confusable partner. 'Stage' is a near-synonym that loses the 'phase of a process' precision, and 'face' is unrelated."},{"id":"vocab_387","entry":"above all","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Most importantly; preeminently; more than anything else.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The historian distrusted the official record, the private letters, and even the survivors' testimony; ______, she distrusted her own tendency to find a story where only a list of dates might be.","correctAnswer":"above all","distractors":["For example","By comparison","In contrast"],"explanation":"'Above all' marks the preeminent point in a list. 'Most importantly' fits the climactic final distrust; 'For example' (illustration), 'By comparison' (contrast by measure), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not mark a climax of emphasis."},{"id":"vocab_388","entry":"in other words","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce a rephrasing that makes a preceding point clearer.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The committee found the proposal underfunded, understaffed, and unbound by any deadline. ______, it had no chance of finishing by the summit.","correctAnswer":"In other words","distractors":["For example","Moreover","In contrast"],"explanation":"'In other words' signals a rephrasing. The second sentence restates the first more bluntly; 'For example' (illustration), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not restate."},{"id":"vocab_389","entry":"in short","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to summarize briefly what has just been said.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The court heard seven days of testimony, four hundred pages of evidence, and three separate timelines from the three suspects. ______, no one agreed on what had happened in the warehouse that night.","correctAnswer":"In short","distractors":["For example","Moreover","Nevertheless"],"explanation":"'In short' marks a brief summary. The single sentence compresses seven days of testimony; 'For example' (illustration), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'Nevertheless' (concession) do not summarize."},{"id":"vocab_390","entry":"to be sure","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to admit or concede a point before presenting a counter-argument.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the new bridge will cost more than the estimate; but the cost of doing nothing, measured in lost commerce and emergency detours, will be far greater within a decade.","correctAnswer":"To be sure","distractors":["Moreover","Consequently","Likewise"],"explanation":"'To be sure' concedes a point before a counter-argument. 'The cost will be more than the estimate' is the concession; 'but the cost of doing nothing' is the counter; 'Moreover' (addition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'Likewise' (parallel) do not concede."},{"id":"vocab_391","entry":"by comparison","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce a statement that measures one thing against another.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The first colony reached self-sufficiency within a generation. ______, the second, founded only a decade later, took nearly a century to feed itself without imported grain.","correctAnswer":"By comparison","distractors":["Likewise","Moreover","Consequently"],"explanation":"'By comparison' introduces a contrastive measure. The second colony's century against the first's generation is a measured contrast; 'Likewise' (parallel), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'Consequently' (result) do not contrast by measure."},{"id":"vocab_392","entry":"in turn","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to mark a sequence in which one thing leads to or is followed by another; each in proper order.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The rainfall fed the river, the river fed the delta, and the delta, ______, fed the shrimp that supported the village through every monsoon season.","correctAnswer":"In turn","distractors":["For instance","In contrast","Namely"],"explanation":"'In turn' marks a sequential consequence within a chain. The delta 'in its own turn' fed the shrimp after the river fed the delta; 'For instance' (example), 'In contrast' (opposition), and 'Namely' (specification) do not mark sequence."},{"id":"vocab_393","entry":"in effect","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In essence; for all practical purposes, though not formally or explicitly.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"By placing the brakes entirely under software control, the company had, ______, made every train a single key press away from disaster, whatever the engineers still nominally signed off.","correctAnswer":"In effect","distractors":["For example","In contrast","Likewise"],"explanation":"'In effect' means 'for practical purposes'. The trains were practically one key press from disaster though not formally so; 'For example' (illustration), 'In contrast' (opposition), and 'Likewise' (parallel) do not express practical equivalence."},{"id":"vocab_394","entry":"for one thing","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to introduce one reason or item among several, often before 'and' or 'also'.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The mayor refused the plan, and ______ was right to: the cost alone would have bankrupted the town before a single rail was laid.","correctAnswer":"For one thing","distractors":["In contrast","Consequently","In short"],"explanation":"'For one thing' introduces one reason among others. The cost being one such reason the mayor had; 'In contrast' (opposition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'In short' (summary) do not introduce a reason."},{"id":"vocab_395","entry":"that said","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"However; conceding or qualifying a point just made.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The report praised the school's reading scores as the highest in the district. ______, its math scores had fallen below the state average for the third year running.","correctAnswer":"That said","distractors":["Moreover","Consequently","Likewise"],"explanation":"'That said' qualifies the preceding point. The math scores falling qualifies the praise for the reading scores; 'Moreover' (addition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'Likewise' (parallel) do not qualify."},{"id":"vocab_396","entry":"in any case","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Regardless of other considerations; whatever the circumstances.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The funders might extend the deadline, or they might not, or they might split the difference; ______, the team would have to finish the prototype within the next six weeks.","correctAnswer":"In any case","distractors":["For example","Moreover","In contrast"],"explanation":"'In any case' means 'regardless'. Whatever the funders decide, the team must finish in six weeks; 'For example' (illustration), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not express 'regardless'."},{"id":"vocab_397","entry":"somber","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Dark or dull in color or tone; having or conveying a serious, gloomy mood.","secondaryMeaning":"Gloomy; grave in manner.","passage":"After the funeral, the family ate in ______ silence, the only sounds the clink of spoons and the rain against the kitchen window.","correctAnswer":"somber","distractors":["cheerful","vibrant","frivolous"],"explanation":"'Somber' means dark and gloomy in mood. 'Silence' broken only by 'spoons and rain' shows a heavy, gloomy mood; cheerful, vibrant, and frivolous are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_398","entry":"vibrant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Full of energy, life, and enthusiasm; (of color) bright and striking.","secondaryMeaning":"Pulsing with life; vivid.","passage":"Even at eighty the painter remained ______, walking daily to the harbor to sketch the boats before the morning fog had lifted.","correctAnswer":"vibrant","distractors":["dull","muted","lethargic"],"explanation":"'Vibrant' means full of life and energy. Walking daily at eighty 'before the morning fog had lifted' shows lively energy; dull, muted, and lethargic are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_399","entry":"insular","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Ignorant of or uninterested in cultures, ideas, or peoples outside one's own experience; narrow-minded.","secondaryMeaning":"Geographically isolated; of an island.","passage":"The village's ______ traditions puzzled the visiting anthropologist, who could not find a single household willing to discuss the customs of the valley next door.","correctAnswer":"insular","distractors":["cosmopolitan","curious","open"],"explanation":"'Insular' means narrow-minded and inward-looking. 'Not a single household willing to discuss' the neighboring valley shows closed-off attitudes; cosmopolitan, curious, and open describe the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_400","entry":"wry","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Using or expressing dry, especially mocking, humor in a way that suggests one is both amused and a little cynical.","secondaryMeaning":"Dryly humorous; twisted or crooked.","passage":"With a ______ smile the comedian noted that the airline's definition of 'on time' now seemed to include any flight that left the same month it was scheduled.","correctAnswer":"wry","distractors":["solemn","sincere","indignant"],"explanation":"'Wry' means dryly, mockingly humorous. The joke about an airline redefining 'on time' to mean the same month is sardonic humor; solemn, sincere, and indignant are not dryly mocking."},{"id":"vocab_401","entry":"idyllic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Extremely happy, peaceful, or picturesque, like a rustic scene from a poem.","secondaryMeaning":"Of an idealized, peaceful rural scene.","passage":"The memoir describes an ______ childhood spent wading in the creek and reading under the apple tree, before the family lost the farm and moved to the city.","correctAnswer":"idyllic","distractors":["harsh","turbulent","urban"],"explanation":"'Idyllic' means peacefully idealized. 'Wading in the creek and reading under the apple tree' paints a serene rural scene; harsh, turbulent, and urban are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_402","entry":"synthesis","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"The combination of parts or elements into a new whole; the production of a compound from simpler substances.","secondaryMeaning":"A bringing together of ideas into a unified whole.","passage":"The research team's ______ of two decades of climate data revealed a pattern no single study had been able to see on its own.","correctAnswer":"synthesis","distractors":["analysis","fragment","isolation"],"explanation":"'Synthesis' means combining into a whole. Combining 'two decades of climate data' to reveal a pattern merges parts into a whole; analysis, fragment, and isolation keep things apart."},{"id":"vocab_403","entry":"discrepancy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A lack of compatibility or agreement between facts or figures; an inconsistency.","secondaryMeaning":"A divergence or disagreement.","passage":"A small ______ between the two measurements became the clue that led the team to a flaw in the instrument's calibration.","correctAnswer":"discrepancy","distractors":["agreement","consistency","harmony"],"explanation":"'Discrepancy' means an inconsistency. A 'small' inconsistency leading to a 'flaw in the calibration' fits; agreement, consistency, and harmony are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_404","entry":"correlation","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"A mutual relationship or connection between two or more things; a statistical link between variables.","secondaryMeaning":"A correspondence in which changes in one variable accompany changes in another.","passage":"The study found a strong ______ between regular sleep and improved memory, though the researchers were careful to note it did not prove one caused the other.","correctAnswer":"correlation","distractors":["coincidence","irrelevance","chance"],"explanation":"'Correlation' means a statistical link. 'Regular sleep and improved memory' moving together is a link the team 'did not prove' was causal; coincidence, irrelevance, and chance do not name a measured statistical link."},{"id":"vocab_405","entry":"inherent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Existing in something as a permanent, essential, or characteristic attribute; built-in.","secondaryMeaning":"Vested in or inseparable from.","passage":"Some risk is ______ in any clinical trial, which is why ethics boards insist on informed consent before a single patient is enrolled.","correctAnswer":"inherent","distractors":["imposed","incidental","external"],"explanation":"'Inherent' means built-in and inseparable. Risk that comes 'with any clinical trial' as part of its nature is built-in; imposed, incidental, and external suggest risk added from outside."},{"id":"vocab_406","entry":"postulate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To suggest or assume the existence, fact, or truth of something as a basis for reasoning.","secondaryMeaning":"To take as a given; a starting assumption (noun).","passage":"To explain why light bent near the sun, Einstein had to ______ that space itself could curve under the weight of matter.","correctAnswer":"postulate","distractors":["refute","disprove","discard"],"explanation":"'Postulate' means to assume as a basis for reasoning. Assuming 'that space itself could curve' to explain the bending of light is a foundational assumption; refuting, disproving, or discarding the idea would oppose it."},{"id":"vocab_407","entry":"extrapolate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To extend the application of a method or conclusion to an unknown situation by assuming that existing trends will continue.","secondaryMeaning":"To infer from known data to unknown cases.","passage":"From three years of data, the team dared to ______ a century-long forecast, a leap that several reviewers found too bold to publish without caveats.","correctAnswer":"extrapolate","distractors":["withhold","abandon","restrict"],"explanation":"'Extrapolate' means to extend a trend beyond the data. 'From three years of data' to 'a century-long forecast' is exactly extending a trend; withholding, abandoning, or restricting the trend does not extend it."},{"id":"vocab_408","entry":"empirical","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Based on, concerned with, or verifiable by observation or experience rather than theory or logic.","secondaryMeaning":"Derived from experiment or observation.","passage":"The committee refused to fund the trial until it had ______ evidence that the drug was safe in animals, not merely plausible reasoning from the molecule's structure.","correctAnswer":"empirical","distractors":["theoretical","speculative","hypothetical"],"explanation":"'Empirical' means based on observation. Evidence from safety tests 'in animals' is observed data; theoretical, speculative, and hypothetical are forms of reasoning without observation."},{"id":"vocab_409","entry":"hegemony","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Leadership or dominance, especially by one country or social group over others.","secondaryMeaning":"Political or cultural dominance of one group over others.","passage":"For most of the century, the empire's ______ over the region went unchallenged, its language and currency accepted even by states that had never been conquered.","correctAnswer":"hegemony","distractors":["subjugation","rebellion","anarchy"],"explanation":"'Hegemony' means dominant leadership. 'Language and currency accepted' across the region shows cultural dominance; subjugation is being dominated (the empire holds hegemony, it is not subject to it), rebellion resists dominance, and anarchy is no dominance."},{"id":"vocab_410","entry":"ratify","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To sign or give formal consent to a treaty, contract, or agreement, making it officially valid.","secondaryMeaning":"To confirm or sanction formally.","passage":"Twelve states had to ______ the new constitution before it could take effect, and the last of those did not agree until the spring of the second year.","correctAnswer":"ratify","distractors":["reject","void","dissolve"],"explanation":"'Ratify' means to formally approve. 'Twelve states' formally agreeing so the constitution 'could take effect' is approval; rejecting, voiding, or dissolving the constitution would prevent it."},{"id":"vocab_411","entry":"emancipate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To set free, especially from legal, social, or political restrictions; to liberate.","secondaryMeaning":"To free from bondage or restraint.","passage":"The reformers argued the only way to ______ the laborers was to abolish the debt system that bound each generation to the last.","correctAnswer":"emancipate","distractors":["enslave","restrain","confine"],"explanation":"'Emancipate' means to set free. Abolishing 'the debt system that bound each generation' frees the laborers; enslaving, restraining, or confining them would keep them bound."},{"id":"vocab_412","entry":"subjugate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To bring under complete control or subjection; to conquer and make subservient.","secondaryMeaning":"To force submission upon.","passage":"To ______ the province, the empire forbade its language in schools and replaced every local judge with one appointed from the capital.","correctAnswer":"subjugate","distractors":["liberate","emancipate","free"],"explanation":"Subjugate means to bring under control. Forbidding the local language and replacing judges forces submission; liberating, emancipating, or freeing the province are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_413","entry":"sanction","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To give official permission or approval for an action; to ratify.","secondaryMeaning":"(noun) An economic or legal penalty imposed by one country on another.","passage":"The council voted to ______ the new trade route, granting its ships the right to dock without paying the harbor tax the older routes still owed.","correctAnswer":"sanction","distractors":["forbid","penalize","condemn"],"explanation":"'Sanction' here means to officially approve. Voting 'to grant its ships the right to dock' is approval; forbidding, penalizing, or condemning the route would oppose it."},{"id":"vocab_414","entry":"delineate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To describe or portray something precisely; to indicate the exact position or boundaries of.","secondaryMeaning":"To trace the outline of; to set forth clearly.","passage":"The author's first chapter carefully ______ the boundaries of her argument, so that readers would know in advance which questions the book would not try to answer.","correctAnswer":"delineate","distractors":["obscure","confuse","blur"],"explanation":"'Delineate' means to set forth precisely. Tracing 'the boundaries of her argument' so readers 'would know in advance' is precise outlining; obscuring, confusing, or blurring the boundaries would hide them."},{"id":"vocab_415","entry":"contextualize","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To place an event, idea, or work within its surrounding circumstances so its meaning can be understood.","secondaryMeaning":"To set within a context.","passage":"The professor insisted her students ______ every poem by the political events of its decade, not read it as if it had been written in a vacuum.","correctAnswer":"contextualize","distractors":["isolate","abstract","detach"],"explanation":"'Contextualize' means to place in context. Reading poems 'by the political events of their decade' rather than 'in a vacuum' sets them in context; isolating, abstracting, or detaching them does the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_416","entry":"imperious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Arrogant and domineering; expecting obedience without question.","secondaryMeaning":"Commanding; overbearing.","passage":"The dowager's ______ tone silenced the whole table, and even her grown sons waited to be addressed before daring to speak.","correctAnswer":"imperious","distractors":["humble","timid","deferential"],"explanation":"'Imperious' means domineering. 'Silenced the whole table' and sons waiting 'before daring to speak' show commanding arrogance; humble, timid, and deferential are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_417","entry":"tractable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Easy to control, influence, or work with; docile and manageable.","secondaryMeaning":"Easily handled or shaped.","passage":"The younger horse proved ______ from the first lesson, taking the saddle without protest while the older mare still refused the bridle.","correctAnswer":"tractable","distractors":["stubborn","obstinate","defiant"],"explanation":"'Tractable' means easily managed. Taking 'the saddle without protest' shows easy handling; stubborn, obstinate, and defiant describe resistance."},{"id":"vocab_418","entry":"genteel","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Polite, refined, and elegant in manner, often in a way that is deliberately cultivated.","secondaryMeaning":"Aristocratic; affectingly polite.","passage":"The boarding school produced young ladies of ______ manners who could discuss poetry in three languages but had never boiled an egg.","correctAnswer":"genteel","distractors":["vulgar","coarse","rough"],"explanation":"'Genteel' means refined and polite. 'Discuss poetry in three languages' fits refined manners; vulgar, coarse, and rough are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_419","entry":"quiescent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"In a state or period of inactivity or dormancy; quiet and still.","secondaryMeaning":"At rest; temporarily inactive.","passage":"For decades the volcano had been ______, and whole towns had grown up on its slopes before it woke again in a single spring.","correctAnswer":"quiescent","distractors":["active","turbulent","agitated"],"explanation":"'Quiescent' means dormant and still. 'For decades' quiet before 'it woke again' is dormancy; active, turbulent, and agitated are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_420","entry":"symptomatic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Serving as a symptom; indicative of a particular condition or disease.","secondaryMeaning":"Constituting a symptom or sign.","passage":"The patient's fatigue was ______ of a deeper iron deficiency, not, as she had assumed, the result of working two jobs.","correctAnswer":"symptomatic","distractors":["unrelated","incidental","random"],"explanation":"'Symptomatic' means serving as a sign of a condition. Fatigue indicating 'a deeper iron deficiency' is a symptom; unrelated, incidental, and random suggest no link to the underlying cause."},{"id":"vocab_421","entry":"converge","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To come together from different directions so as to meet; to tend toward a common point or result.","secondaryMeaning":"To approach the same limit.","passage":"After years of unrelated experiments, the two laboratories' results began to ______ on the same surprising conclusion, convincing neither team that it was an accident.","correctAnswer":"converge","distractors":["diverge","scatter","split"],"explanation":"'Converge' means to come together. Two labs' results tending 'on the same' conclusion come together; diverging, scattering, and splitting all move apart."},{"id":"vocab_422","entry":"invariant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"Never changing; remaining the same under all conditions or transformations.","secondaryMeaning":"Constant; unchanging.","passage":"The speed of light is ______ in every frame of reference, a fact Einstein took as the foundation of his theory rather than a puzzle to solve.","correctAnswer":"invariant","distractors":["variable","fluctuating","mutable"],"explanation":"'Invariant' means never changing. 'The same in every frame of reference' is the sense; variable, fluctuating, and mutable describe what changes."},{"id":"vocab_423","entry":"dissident","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A person who opposes the policy or government of a state, especially one officially in power; a political dissenter.","secondaryMeaning":"One who disagrees, especially politically.","passage":"After the speech, the playwright was branded a ______ and barred from publishing for the rest of the decade, though his plays continued to circulate in private.","correctAnswer":"dissident","distractors":["loyalist","partisan","sympathizer"],"explanation":"'Dissident' means a political opponent. 'Branded' and 'barred from publishing' for opposing the regime fits; loyalist, partisan, and sympathizer support the regime."},{"id":"vocab_424","entry":"annex","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To add or attach territory to a larger state, usually by force or formal decree.","secondaryMeaning":"To append or incorporate.","passage":"Eager to secure the harbor, the empire moved to ______ the small island, signing the treaty under threat of bombardment and ignoring the protests of its fishermen.","correctAnswer":"annex","distractors":["relinquish","liberate","cede"],"explanation":"'Annex' means to take territory. 'Signing the treaty under threat of bombardment' to secure the harbor is forcible annexation; relinquishing, liberating, or ceding territory would give it away."},{"id":"vocab_425","entry":"credence","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"Belief in or acceptance of something as true; mental assent.","secondaryMeaning":"Trustworthiness; belief.","passage":"The forger's skill gave ______ to documents that should have raised suspicion at once, fooling three archivists before the watermark was finally tested.","correctAnswer":"credence","distractors":["skepticism","doubt","dismissal"],"explanation":"'Credence' means acceptance as true. The forger's skill 'fooling three archivists' gave the fakes acceptance; skepticism, doubt, and dismissal would withhold it."},{"id":"vocab_426","entry":"mandate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"An official order or commission to carry out a specific policy; the authority to act that voters give to a winner.","secondaryMeaning":"A command from a higher authority.","passage":"The reform party claimed its huge victory as a ______ to rewrite the tax code, arguing that any opposition now ignored the will of the voters.","correctAnswer":"mandate","distractors":["prohibition","suggestion","option"],"explanation":"'Mandate' means an authoritative commission. A 'huge victory' treated 'as' authority 'to rewrite the tax code' is the voters' commission; prohibition, suggestion, and option lack that authority."},{"id":"vocab_427","entry":"expound","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To present and explain a theory or idea in detail; to set forth systematically.","secondaryMeaning":"To explain at length.","passage":"For three hours the philosopher would ______ on the nature of justice, until even his admirers wished he would say it in fewer words.","correctAnswer":"expound","distractors":["gloss","condense","summarize"],"explanation":"'Expound' means to explain at length. 'For three hours' until admirers 'wished he would say it in fewer words' is lengthy explanation; glossing, condensing, or summarizing all shorten it."},{"id":"vocab_428","entry":"accentuate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To make something more noticeable or prominent; to emphasize.","secondaryMeaning":"To stress or highlight.","passage":"The photographer's use of a single shaft of light served to ______ the worker's hands against the dim wall behind him.","correctAnswer":"accentuate","distractors":["minimize","downplay","obscure"],"explanation":"'Accentuate' means to emphasize. 'A single shaft of light' making 'the worker's hands' prominent highlights them; minimizing, downplaying, or obscuring the hands would hide them."},{"id":"vocab_429","entry":"untenable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Not able to be maintained or defended against attack; indefensible.","secondaryMeaning":"Of an argument that cannot be held.","passage":"Once the letter was dated to a year after the alleged meeting, the prosecutor's whole timeline became ______ and the case collapsed within a week.","correctAnswer":"untenable","distractors":["defensible","sound","plausible"],"explanation":"'Untenable' means indefensible. 'The case collapsed within a week' after the timeline was dated wrong shows it cannot be defended; defensible, sound, and plausible are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_430","entry":"vivacious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Attractively lively and animated; full of energy and high spirits.","secondaryMeaning":"Bubbly; attractively energetic.","passage":"The youngest sister was the ______ one, lighting up every room she entered and outdancing guests half her age at the annual ball.","correctAnswer":"vivacious","distractors":["sullen","lethargic","morose"],"explanation":"'Vivacious' means attractively lively. 'Lighting up every room' and 'outdancing guests half her age' show lively energy; sullen, lethargic, and morose are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_431","entry":"lugubrious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Looking or sounding sad and dismal; mournful, often to an exaggerated degree.","secondaryMeaning":"Exaggeratedly gloomy or mournful.","passage":"The actor delivered the eulogy in a ______ bass that turned even the dead man's favorite joke into something funereal.","correctAnswer":"lugubrious","distractors":["jovial","cheerful","buoyant"],"explanation":"'Lugubrious' means exaggeratedly mournful. Turning 'the dead man's favorite joke into something funereal' is gloomy overstatement; jovial, cheerful, and buoyant are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_432","entry":"insouciant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Showing a casual lack of concern; appearing not to worry or care.","secondaryMeaning":"Lighthearted and unconcerned.","passage":"He played the last set with an ______ smile, as if losing the championship were a small inconvenience and not the end of a decade's work.","correctAnswer":"insouciant","distractors":["anxious","earnest","distressed"],"explanation":"'Insouciant' means casually unconcerned. Playing 'as if losing the championship were a small inconvenience' is careless calm; anxious, earnest, and distressed all show concern."},{"id":"vocab_433","entry":"sagacious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Having or showing keen mental discernment and good judgment; shrewd and wise.","secondaryMeaning":"Wise; of keen judgment.","passage":"The old judge's ______ ruling cut through the years of appeals by granting each party exactly the part of the claim it had honestly earned.","correctAnswer":"sagacious","distractors":["foolish","naive","obtuse"],"explanation":"'Sagacious' means shrewd and wise. The ruling 'cut through the years of appeals' by granting 'exactly' what was honestly earned shows wise judgment; foolish, naive, and obtuse are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_434","entry":"trenchant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Vigorous or incisive in expression or style; sharp and penetrating in insight.","secondaryMeaning":"Keen; sharply effective.","passage":"Her ______ review of the novel exposed weaknesses the author had not known were there, and the praise of its strengths was just as exact.","correctAnswer":"trenchant","distractors":["vague","tedious","feeble"],"explanation":"'Trenchant' means sharp and penetrating. Exposing 'weaknesses the author had not known were there' with 'exact' praise is incisive; vague, tedious, and feeble are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_435","entry":"assure","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To tell someone positively and confidently to remove doubt; to promise.","secondaryMeaning":"To give confidence to (a person).","passage":"The captain tried to ______ the crew that the leak was under control, though the water rising at their ankles told a different story.","correctAnswer":"assure","distractors":["ensure","insure","convince"],"explanation":"'Assure' means to remove someone's doubt (a person). The captain addressing 'the crew' to remove their doubt fits; 'ensure' (to make certain of an outcome) is the confusable partner. 'Insure' (to cover with insurance) and 'convince' (persuade by argument) do not fit 'tell to remove doubt'."},{"id":"vocab_436","entry":"ensure","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To make certain that something will happen or be the case; to guarantee an outcome.","secondaryMeaning":"To make sure.","passage":"The mechanic added a second bolt to ______ the wheel would not work loose on the mountain road, where a failure would mean going over the edge.","correctAnswer":"ensure","distractors":["assure","insure","secure"],"explanation":"'Ensure' means to make an outcome certain. A second bolt 'so the wheel would not work loose' guarantees the outcome; 'assure' (remove a person's doubt) is the confusable partner. 'Insure' (cover against risk) and 'secure' (fasten) do not mean 'make certain'."},{"id":"vocab_437","entry":"insure","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To arrange for financial compensation against the loss of something, by regular payments to an insurer.","secondaryMeaning":"To protect by contract against risk.","passage":"Because the gallery sat on the flood plain, the curator had to ______ every painting against water damage at a premium no other building in town paid.","correctAnswer":"insure","distractors":["ensure","assure","assuage"],"explanation":"'Insure' means to cover with insurance. 'Insure every painting against water damage at a premium' is insurance coverage; 'ensure' (guarantee an outcome) is the confusable partner. 'Assure' (remove doubt) and 'assuage' (soothe) do not fit the insurance context."},{"id":"vocab_438","entry":"adverse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Unfavorable, harmful, or opposing; acting against one's interests.","secondaryMeaning":"Hostile; unfavorable.","passage":"The crop failed under ______ weather for the third straight season, and the bank began to press the farm's loans before the harvest was even in.","correctAnswer":"adverse","distractors":["averse","favorable","supportive"],"explanation":"'Adverse' means unfavorable (of conditions). 'Adverse weather' causing the crop to fail fits; 'averse' (reluctant, of feelings) is the confusable partner. 'Favorable' and 'supportive' are the opposite of unfavorable."},{"id":"vocab_439","entry":"averse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Having a strong dislike of or opposition to something; reluctant.","secondaryMeaning":"Strongly disinclined (often 'averse to').","passage":"The composer was ______ to performing in public, preferring the silence of the studio to any applause he could imagine.","correctAnswer":"averse","distractors":["adverse","eager","inclined"],"explanation":"'Averse' means reluctant (of a person). 'Preferring the silence of the studio to applause' shows reluctance; 'adverse' (unfavorable, of conditions) is the confusable partner. 'Eager' and 'inclined' are the opposite of reluctant."},{"id":"vocab_440","entry":"persecute","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To subject someone to hostility and ill-treatment, especially because of race, religion, or political beliefs.","secondaryMeaning":"To oppress or hound persistently.","passage":"The regime began to ______ the minority not for any crime but for the language they spoke at home, dismissing teachers who would not switch to the official tongue.","correctAnswer":"persecute","distractors":["prosecute","protect","shelter"],"explanation":"'Persecute' means to oppress for identity. Dismissing teachers 'for the language they spoke at home' is identity-based oppression; 'prosecute' (to take to court for a crime) is the confusable partner. 'Protect' and 'shelter' are the opposite of oppression."},{"id":"vocab_441","entry":"prosecute","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To institute and pursue legal proceedings against a person or organization for a crime.","secondaryMeaning":"To conduct a criminal case against.","passage":"The state chose to ______ the company for the spill, taking it to court under laws that had not been used against an oil firm in thirty years.","correctAnswer":"prosecute","distractors":["persecute","defend","pardon"],"explanation":"'Prosecute' means to take to court. 'Taking it to court under laws' is a legal case; 'persecute' (to oppress for identity) is the confusable partner. 'Defend' and 'pardon' both oppose prosecution."},{"id":"vocab_442","entry":"deplore","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To strongly disapprove of something and criticize it; to regard as regrettable or wrong.","secondaryMeaning":"To express sorrow or deep disapproval of.","passage":"The bishop rose to ______ the violence in the strongest terms the cathedral had heard in a generation, his voice shaking with the weight of the words.","correctAnswer":"deplore","distractors":["deploy","commend","endorse"],"explanation":"'Deplore' means to strongly disapprove. 'Strongest terms' with 'a voice shaking' is strong criticism; 'deploy' (to position troops or resources) is the confusable partner. 'Commend' and 'endorse' are approval, the opposite of deplore."},{"id":"vocab_443","entry":"deploy","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To move troops or resources into position so they can be used; to bring into action.","secondaryMeaning":"To strategically place for use.","passage":"Within hours of the quake, the relief agency was able to ______ doctors and tents to the valley before the road was closed by the next aftershock.","correctAnswer":"deploy","distractors":["deplore","withdraw","store"],"explanation":"'Deploy' means to position for use. 'Position doctors and tents' to the valley is resource placement; 'deplore' (disapprove) is the confusable partner. 'Withdraw' and 'store' take resources away, the opposite of deploying them."},{"id":"vocab_444","entry":"populous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Having a large population; densely inhabited.","secondaryMeaning":"Heavily populated.","passage":"The capital was the most ______ city the empire had ever built, its streets so crowded at dusk that a cart could not move a block in an hour.","correctAnswer":"populous","distractors":["populace","deserted","empty"],"explanation":"'Populous' means densely inhabited. 'Streets so crowded' a cart cannot move shows dense population; 'populace' (the people, a noun) is the confusable partner. 'Deserted' and 'empty' are the opposite of densely inhabited."},{"id":"vocab_445","entry":"populace","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"The people living in a particular country or area; the general public.","secondaryMeaning":"The common people of a region.","passage":"When the price of bread doubled overnight, the angry ______ broke the bakery doors before the guard could reach the square.","correctAnswer":"populace","distractors":["populous","elite","nobility"],"explanation":"'Populace' means the general public. 'The angry public broke the bakery doors' fits; 'populous' (densely inhabited, an adjective) is the confusable partner. 'Elite' and 'nobility' are the upper classes, not the general public."},{"id":"vocab_446","entry":"deprecate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To express disapproval of; to belittle or speak slightingly of (often 'self-deprecating').","secondaryMeaning":"To plead against; to deplore.","passage":"The senior scientist was refreshingly ______ about his own Nobel, insisting that luck had more to do with it than any brilliance of his own.","correctAnswer":"deprecate","distractors":["depreciate","praise","commend"],"explanation":"'Deprecate' (here self-deprecate) means to belittle oneself. 'Insisting that luck had more to do with it' downplays his own brilliance; 'depreciate' (to lose monetary value) is the confusable partner. 'Praise' and 'commend' are the opposite of belittling."},{"id":"vocab_447","entry":"depreciate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To diminish in value over time; to lower the price or worth of.","secondaryMeaning":"To reduce the value of an asset.","passage":"New cars ______ so steeply in the first year that some buyers lease simply to avoid owning the steepest part of the loss.","correctAnswer":"depreciate","distractors":["deprecate","appreciate","escalate"],"explanation":"'Depreciate' means to lose monetary value. 'New cars losing value steeply in the first year' fits; 'deprecate' (to belittle, often oneself) is the confusable partner. 'Appreciate' and 'escalate' mean to rise in value, the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_448","entry":"capitol","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"The building where a legislature meets; (Capitol) the U.S. Congress building.","secondaryMeaning":"A legislative building.","passage":"Protesters gathered on the steps of the state ______, where the legislature was about to vote on the bill the crowd had come to oppose.","correctAnswer":"capitol","distractors":["capital","palace","courthouse"],"explanation":"'Capitol' means the legislative building. 'The steps of the state' building where 'the legislature was about to vote' fits; 'capital' (the city or financial wealth) is the confusable partner. 'Palace' and 'courthouse' are different buildings."},{"id":"vocab_449","entry":"capital","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"The most important city or town of a country or region; the wealth or money used to start a business.","secondaryMeaning":"(adjective) Excellent; involving punishment by death.","passage":"With the bridge washed out, the rescue teams could not reach the ______ for three days, and the governor's office sat unreachable inside it.","correctAnswer":"capital","distractors":["capitol","province","suburb"],"explanation":"'Capital' here means the chief city. 'The governor's office' inside it marks the seat of government; 'capitol' (the legislative building) is the confusable partner. 'Province' and 'suburb' are not the chief city."},{"id":"vocab_450","entry":"dissemble","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To conceal one's real motives, feelings, or beliefs; to put on a false appearance.","secondaryMeaning":"To feign; to disguise under a false appearance.","passage":"The envoy had to ______ through three courses of dinner, smiling at the very ministers whose secret correspondence he had come to obtain.","correctAnswer":"dissemble","distractors":["disassemble","reveal","declare"],"explanation":"'Dissemble' means to put on a false appearance. 'Smiling at the very ministers whose correspondence he had come to steal' is feigned friendliness; 'disassemble' (to take apart) is the confusable partner. 'Reveal' and 'declare' are the opposite of concealing."},{"id":"vocab_451","entry":"disassemble","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To take something apart into its component pieces; to take to pieces.","secondaryMeaning":"To take apart.","passage":"To clean the rifle properly, the soldier had to ______ it down to its smallest spring, a process the manual warned could take an hour the first time.","correctAnswer":"disassemble","distractors":["dissemble","assemble","construct"],"explanation":"'Disassemble' means to take apart. Taking the rifle 'down to its smallest spring' is taking apart; 'dissemble' (to feign) is the confusable partner. 'Assemble' and 'construct' are the opposite of taking apart."},{"id":"vocab_452","entry":"foreword","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A short introduction to a book, typically by someone other than the author.","secondaryMeaning":"An introductory passage.","passage":"The novelist asked her old teacher to write the ______, hoping his name would lend the debut the weight its quiet pages might not earn on their own.","correctAnswer":"foreword","distractors":["forward","afterword","index"],"explanation":"'Foreword' means an introductory passage. 'A short introduction by someone other than the author' fits; 'forward' (a direction) is the confusable partner. 'Afterword' comes at the end and 'index' is a reference list, neither an introduction."},{"id":"vocab_453","entry":"forward","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Directed or moving ahead; toward the front; (of a person) presumptuously bold.","secondaryMeaning":"Onward; toward the front.","passage":"The general ordered his lightest companies ______ through the woods to seize the bridge before the enemy could blow it at dawn.","correctAnswer":"forward","distractors":["foreword","backward","rear"],"explanation":"'Forward' here means toward the front. Ordering companies 'through the woods to seize the bridge' is movement ahead; 'foreword' (an introduction) is the confusable partner. 'Backward' and 'rear' are the opposite of ahead."},{"id":"vocab_454","entry":"amuse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To entertain or cause someone to find something funny; to occupy pleasantly.","secondaryMeaning":"To make laugh or smile.","passage":"The juggler's offbeat routine was enough to ______ the children for an hour, though it left the adults in the front row staring in polite confusion.","correctAnswer":"amuse","distractors":["muse","bore","worry"],"explanation":"'Amuse' means to entertain. 'Enough to entertain the children for an hour' fits; 'muse' (to ponder) is the confusable partner. 'Bore' and 'worry' are the opposite of entertaining."},{"id":"vocab_455","entry":"muse","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To think about something carefully and at length; to consider thoughtfully.","secondaryMeaning":"To ponder or reflect.","passage":"Alone on the dock at dawn, the old fisherman would ______ on the seasons he had seen, until the first gull pulled him back to the present.","correctAnswer":"muse","distractors":["amuse","ignore","dismiss"],"explanation":"'Muse' means to ponder. 'To think on the seasons he had seen at dawn' is reflection; 'amuse' (to entertain) is the confusable partner. 'Ignore' and 'dismiss' are the opposite of thoughtful consideration."},{"id":"vocab_456","entry":"hoard","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To accumulate and hide a stock or store of something, often secretively and in excess.","secondaryMeaning":"To amass and keep jealously.","passage":"During the shortage the shopkeeper was caught trying to ______ bags of flour in the cellar, expecting to sell them at ten times the price once the roads closed.","correctAnswer":"hoard","distractors":["horde","share","donate"],"explanation":"'Hoard' means to stockpile secretly. 'Bags of flour in the cellar' to sell 'at ten times the price' is hidden stockpiling; 'horde' (a large crowd, a noun) is the confusable partner. 'Share' and 'donate' are the opposite of stockpiling."},{"id":"vocab_457","entry":"horde","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A large, often unruly group of people; a moving swarm or crowd.","secondaryMeaning":"A vast, loosely organized crowd.","passage":"After the gates opened, a ______ of shoppers poured into the sale, sweeping the first table clean before the staff could put up the price signs.","correctAnswer":"horde","distractors":["hoard","handful","individual"],"explanation":"'Horde' means a large unruly crowd. 'Poured into the sale, sweeping the first table clean' fits; 'hoard' (a hidden stockpile, a verb/noun) is the confusable partner. 'Handful' and 'individual' are the opposite of a large crowd."},{"id":"vocab_458","entry":"for that matter","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to add a remark that applies equally to what has just been said, often extending a point.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The library had no copies of the novel—and, ______, no copies of any book by that publisher, whose catalog it had stopped buying years ago.","correctAnswer":"For that matter","distractors":["For example","Nevertheless","Likewise"],"explanation":"'For that matter' adds an equal-extent point. The library has no copies 'of any book by that publisher', an equal-strength extension; 'For example' (illustration), 'Nevertheless' (concession), and 'Likewise' (parallel between two cases) do not add an equal-extent point."},{"id":"vocab_459","entry":"on the whole","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Considering everything; in general, allowing for exceptions.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the reforms did more good than harm, though the textile towns that lost their mills would not have agreed in the year they lost them.","correctAnswer":"On the whole","distractors":["In contrast","Namely","For instance"],"explanation":"'On the whole' means 'considering everything'. Weighing 'more good than harm' while admitting the textile towns lost out is a general balance; 'In contrast' (opposition), 'Namely' (specification), and 'For instance' (example) do not sum up."},{"id":"vocab_460","entry":"by and large","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"On the whole; generally speaking, despite minor exceptions.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the expedition had been a success, even if three of the ten porters had quit before the second mountain and the photographs had all been lost in the river.","correctAnswer":"By and large","distractors":["For example","Moreover","In contrast"],"explanation":"'By and large' means 'generally'. Calling the expedition a success despite 'three porters' quitting and the photographs 'lost in the river' is a general balance; 'For example' (illustration), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not generalize."},{"id":"vocab_461","entry":"in essence","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Fundamentally; used to state the core of a matter, ignoring details.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the proposal would tax the rich to fund the schools, however the lawyers chose to dress up the language of the bill.","correctAnswer":"In essence","distractors":["For instance","In contrast","Moreover"],"explanation":"'In essence' means 'fundamentally'. Stating 'tax the rich to fund the schools' however the lawyers 'dress up the language' captures the core; 'For instance' (example), 'In contrast' (opposition), and 'Moreover' (addition) do not state a core."},{"id":"vocab_462","entry":"as a rule","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Generally; in most cases, though there may be exceptions.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the council meets on the first Tuesday of each month, though any member may call an emergency session before then if three others agree.","correctAnswer":"As a rule","distractors":["For example","Nevertheless","In contrast"],"explanation":"'As a rule' means 'generally'. 'The first Tuesday of each month' is the norm with exceptions for emergencies; 'For example' (illustration), 'Nevertheless' (concession), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not state a norm."},{"id":"vocab_463","entry":"needless to say","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Obviously; used to introduce something that should already be clear or expected.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the team that had practiced through the holidays won the cup, while the one that had skipped training could not finish the final match.","correctAnswer":"Needless to say","distractors":["Moreover","Consequently","In contrast"],"explanation":"'Needless to say' introduces the obvious. The holiday-practice team winning is the expected outcome; 'Moreover' (addition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not mark the obvious."},{"id":"vocab_464","entry":"at any rate","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"In any case; used to move on or to qualify, regardless of what came before.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The strike may last a week, or it may drag on all winter; ______, the factory will not reopen before the new contract is signed.","correctAnswer":"At any rate","distractors":["For instance","Moreover","In contrast"],"explanation":"'At any rate' means 'in any case'. Whether the strike lasts a week or all winter, the factory stays closed — the point holds either way; 'For instance' (example), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not move on."},{"id":"vocab_465","entry":"so to speak","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to mark that one is speaking figuratively, not literally.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The lawyer had, ______, painted the defendant into a corner of his own making, though no brush or canvas was anywhere in the courtroom.","correctAnswer":"so to speak","distractors":["Moreover","Consequently","In contrast"],"explanation":"'So to speak' marks figurative speech. 'Painted into a corner' though 'no brush or canvas' is in the room is figurative; 'Moreover' (addition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not mark figurative use."},{"id":"vocab_466","entry":"or rather","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Used to correct or restate more accurately something just said.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"The trip took six days—______, five and a half, since the last train pulled in just before midnight on the sixth.","correctAnswer":"or rather","distractors":["Moreover","Consequently","In contrast"],"explanation":"'Or rather' introduces a correction. Restating 'six days' more precisely as 'five and a half' corrects the figure; 'Moreover' (addition), 'Consequently' (result), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not correct."},{"id":"vocab_467","entry":"all things considered","type":"phrase","partOfSpeech":"transitional phrase","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Transitions","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Taking everything into account; weighing all the factors.","secondaryMeaning":"","passage":"______, the patient was recovering faster than the doctors had predicted, despite the infection that had set back his progress by a week.","correctAnswer":"All things considered","distractors":["For example","Moreover","In contrast"],"explanation":"'All things considered' means 'weighing everything'. Recovery 'faster than predicted' 'despite the infection' is a balanced overall judgment; 'For example' (illustration), 'Moreover' (addition), and 'In contrast' (opposition) do not sum up."},{"id":"vocab_468","entry":"didactic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Intended or inclined to teach, often excessively morally instructive; preachy.","secondaryMeaning":"Inclined to teach, especially morally.","passage":"The novel's ______ tone wore on the reader by the third chapter, every scene ending with a neatly drawn moral that left no feeling unexplained.","correctAnswer":"didactic","distractors":["entertaining","playful","decorative"],"explanation":"'Didactic' means intended to morally instruct. 'Every scene ending with a neatly drawn moral' shows moral teaching; entertaining, playful, and decorative do not describe teaching intent."},{"id":"vocab_469","entry":"tendentious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Expressing or tending to promote a partisan cause or point of view; biased.","secondaryMeaning":"Promoting a particular cause.","passage":"The pamphlet was so ______ that even readers who agreed with its cause winced at the way it stacked every fact to suit one side.","correctAnswer":"tendentious","distractors":["neutral","balanced","impartial"],"explanation":"'Tendentious' means biased for a cause. 'Stacked every fact to suit one side' so even allies 'winced' is bias; neutral, balanced, and impartial are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_470","entry":"polemic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"A strong verbal or written attack on someone or something; an aggressive argument against an opinion.","secondaryMeaning":"A controversial argument.","passage":"The journalist's new book was less a history than a ______, attacking the previous administration with a zeal that left no room for its few real successes.","correctAnswer":"polemic","distractors":["tribute","endorsement","eulogy"],"explanation":"'Polemic' means an aggressive attack. 'Attacking the previous administration with a zeal that left no room for its successes' is a hostile argument; tribute, endorsement, and eulogy are praise, the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_471","entry":"encomium","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"A speech or piece of writing that praises someone or something highly.","secondaryMeaning":"A formal expression of praise.","passage":"The dean's ______ at the memorial named every one of the professor's virtues and not a single fault, as the occasion required.","correctAnswer":"encomium","distractors":["diatribe","denunciation","tirade"],"explanation":"'Encomium' means a high praise. Naming 'every virtue and not a single fault, as the occasion required' fits a praising speech; diatribe, denunciation, and tirade are attacks, the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_472","entry":"obsequious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Obedient or attentive to an excessive or servile degree; fawning.","secondaryMeaning":"Excessively eager to please.","passage":"The new courtier's ______ bowing before the queen embarrassed even the lords who owed her their titles, so eager was he to please that he forgot his own rank.","correctAnswer":"obsequious","distractors":["assertive","defiant","aloof"],"explanation":"'Obsequious' means servile to please. 'Bowing' so eager 'he forgot his own rank' is fawning; assertive, defiant, and aloof are the opposite of servile."},{"id":"vocab_473","entry":"supercilious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Behaving or looking as though one thinks one is superior to others; arrogant and dismissive.","secondaryMeaning":"Haughty and condescending.","passage":"The maître d' gave the couple a ______ glance at their worn coats and showed them to the worst table, near the kitchen door.","correctAnswer":"supercilious","distractors":["humble","cordial","deferential"],"explanation":"'Supercilious' means arrogantly dismissive. A dismissive 'glance at their worn coats' leading to 'the worst table' is condescension; humble, cordial, and deferential are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_474","entry":"truculent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Eager or quick to argue or fight; aggressively defiant.","secondaryMeaning":"Fiercely combative.","passage":"After three days without sleep the suspect grew ______, answering even the simplest question with a challenge of his own.","correctAnswer":"truculent","distractors":["peaceful","conciliatory","amiable"],"explanation":"'Truculent' means quick to fight. 'Answering even the simplest question with a challenge' shows combative defiance; peaceful, conciliatory, and amiable are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_475","entry":"captious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Tending to raise petty or ill-natured objections; fault-finding over small points.","secondaryMeaning":"Given to catching at trivial faults.","passage":"The reviewer was so ______ that he devoted two pages to a misplaced comma and three lines to the book's actual argument.","correctAnswer":"captious","distractors":["forgiving","lenient","indulgent"],"explanation":"'Captious' means fault-finding over trifles. 'Two pages to a misplaced comma and three lines to the argument' is petty fault-finding; forgiving, lenient, and indulgent are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_476","entry":"redoubtable","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_3","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Text Structure and Purpose"],"definition":"Formidable or intimidating, especially as an opponent; worthy of respect and a little fear.","secondaryMeaning":"Awe-inspiring; formidable.","passage":"The challenger was a ______ opponent, with thirty years of cases behind him and not a single verdict reversed on appeal.","correctAnswer":"redoubtable","distractors":["feeble","negligible","trivial"],"explanation":"'Redoubtable' means formidable. 'Thirty years of cases' and 'not a single verdict reversed' make an intimidating opponent; feeble, negligible, and trivial are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_477","entry":"pious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Deeply religious or devout; (derogatory) making a hypocritical show of virtue.","secondaryMeaning":"Devoutly religious.","passage":"The widow's ______ attendance at morning mass impressed even the priest, who had begun to worry that the front pews would empty entirely within a generation.","correctAnswer":"pious","distractors":["profane","irreverent","cynical"],"explanation":"'Pious' means devoutly religious. 'Attendance at morning mass' that impresses the priest shows devotion; profane, irreverent, and cynical are the opposite of devout."},{"id":"vocab_478","entry":"exacting","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Making great demands on one's skill, attention, or resources; severely demanding.","secondaryMeaning":"Rigorous; inflexibly demanding.","passage":"The editor's ______ standards meant a single misplaced semicolon could send an article back for revision, and several writers left the magazine rather than meet them.","correctAnswer":"exacting","distractors":["lenient","casual","easygoing"],"explanation":"'Exacting' means severely demanding. 'A single misplaced semicolon' sending an article back is rigorous; lenient, casual, and easygoing are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_479","entry":"perpetuate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To make something continue indefinitely; to preserve from extinction or oblivion.","secondaryMeaning":"To cause to continue indefinitely.","passage":"The school's policy of grouping children by their parents' income tended to ______ the very inequality its founders had hoped education would erase.","correctAnswer":"perpetuate","distractors":["eradicate","abolish","extinguish"],"explanation":"'Perpetuate' means to cause to continue indefinitely. The policy 'tended to continue the inequality' it hoped to erase prolongs it; eradicating, abolishing, or extinguishing it would end it."},{"id":"vocab_480","entry":"hallow","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To honor as holy or sacred; to give great respect and reverence to.","secondaryMeaning":"To consecrate; to revere.","passage":"The battlefield was ______ by the graves of the fallen, so that even the generals who had ordered the charge could not bring themselves to build on it.","correctAnswer":"hallow","distractors":["profane","desecrate","defile"],"explanation":"'Hallow' means to honor as holy. The generals unable 'to build on' the graves shows reverence; profaning, desecrating, or defiling the field would dishonor it."},{"id":"vocab_481","entry":"promulgate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"To make widely known; to put a law or decree into effect by formal public announcement.","secondaryMeaning":"To publish or make known officially.","passage":"Once the council voted, the king had only to ______ the new tax, and within a month every harbor in the kingdom would be collecting it.","correctAnswer":"promulgate","distractors":["conceal","suppress","repeal"],"explanation":"'Promulgate' means to make known officially. The king announcing 'so every harbor would be collecting it' is official publication; concealing, suppressing, or repealing it would not spread it."},{"id":"vocab_482","entry":"salient","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":2,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Most noticeable or important; prominent or striking in significance.","secondaryMeaning":"Most prominent; jumping out.","passage":"The defense lawyer stressed only the ______ facts of the case, letting the minor contradictions pass unmentioned rather than draw the jury's attention to them.","correctAnswer":"salient","distractors":["trivial","obscure","marginal"],"explanation":"'Salient' means most prominent or important. Stressing only 'the most prominent facts' while 'letting the minor contradictions pass' fits; trivial, obscure, and marginal are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_483","entry":"climactic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Relating to or forming a climax; the most intense or decisive point of a story or series of events.","secondaryMeaning":"Of or forming a climax.","passage":"The novel's ______ chapter pays off every subplot at once, as the runaway returns home on the very night the harvest festival goes up in flames.","correctAnswer":"climactic","distractors":["climatic","trivial","gradual"],"explanation":"'Climactic' means forming a climax. The chapter where 'every subplot pays off at once' is the peak; 'climatic' (relating to the climate) is the confusable partner. 'Trivial' and 'gradual' are the opposite of a peak."},{"id":"vocab_484","entry":"climatic","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Relating to the climate, especially long-term weather conditions of a region.","secondaryMeaning":"Of the prevailing weather conditions.","passage":"A slight but steady ______ shift toward drier summers has forced the vineyards to dig wells twice as deep as their fathers ever needed.","correctAnswer":"climatic","distractors":["climactic","emotional","dramatic"],"explanation":"'Climatic' means relating to climate. 'Drier summers' forcing wells 'twice as deep' is a weather-climate shift; 'climactic' (forming a climax) is the confusable partner. 'Emotional' and 'dramatic' describe a climax, not weather."},{"id":"vocab_485","entry":"tortuous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Full of twists, turns, or bends; (of reasoning or arguments) excessively complex and hard to follow.","secondaryMeaning":"Winding; convoluted.","passage":"The mountain road was so ______ that locals drove it only at dawn, when the fog made the hundred blind curves at least feel deliberate.","correctAnswer":"tortuous","distractors":["torturous","straight","direct"],"explanation":"'Tortuous' means full of twists. 'A hundred blind curves' on the road is winding; 'torturous' (causing torture) is the confusable partner. 'Straight' and 'direct' are the opposite of winding."},{"id":"vocab_486","entry":"torturous","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Involving or causing severe suffering; extremely painful or difficult to endure.","secondaryMeaning":"Causing torture; agonizing.","passage":"The three-day hike became a ______ ordeal once the water ran out, and two of the scouts had to be carried the last mile into camp.","correctAnswer":"torturous","distractors":["tortuous","pleasant","trivial"],"explanation":"'Torturous' means causing severe suffering. 'Once the water ran out' two scouts 'had to be carried' is agony; 'tortuous' (winding) is the confusable partner. 'Pleasant' and 'trivial' are the opposite of agonizing."},{"id":"vocab_487","entry":"venal","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Showing or motivated by willingness to be bribed or corrupted; open to bribery.","secondaryMeaning":"Corruptible for pay.","passage":"The judge was so ______ that a defendant could buy an acquittal for the price of a used car, and several lawyers had built their practices on the fact.","correctAnswer":"venal","distractors":["venial","honest","upright"],"explanation":"'Venal' means corruptible for pay. 'Buy an acquittal for the price of a used car' is bribery; 'venial' (a forgivable sin) is the confusable partner. 'Honest' and 'upright' are the opposite of corruptible."},{"id":"vocab_488","entry":"venial","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Of a sin or offense) slight and forgivable; not serious enough to warrant severe punishment.","secondaryMeaning":"Pardonable; excusable.","passage":"Missing a single deadline was a ______ offense in that newsroom, but inventing a source was enough to end a career in an afternoon.","correctAnswer":"venial","distractors":["venal","mortal","grave"],"explanation":"'Venial' means a slight, forgivable offense. Missing 'a single deadline' vs 'inventing a source' ending a career shows the slightness; 'venal' (corruptible for pay) is the confusable partner. 'Mortal' and 'grave' are the opposite of slight."},{"id":"vocab_489","entry":"meretricious","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Apparently attractive but having in reality no value or substance; gaudy and false.","secondaryMeaning":"Tawdrily alluring.","passage":"The casino's chandeliers gave the lobby a ______ brilliance, all flash and no warmth, like a stage set built to be admired only from the door.","correctAnswer":"meretricious","distractors":["genuine","substantial","authentic"],"explanation":"'Meretricious' means flashy but without substance. 'All flash and no warmth, like a stage set' is false glitter; genuine, substantial, and authentic are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_490","entry":"inchoate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Just begun and so not fully formed; rudimentary or embryonic.","secondaryMeaning":"Not yet fully developed; incipient.","passage":"The committee's plan was still ______ at the first meeting, a cluster of hopes more than a strategy, with no dates, no budget, and no names attached.","correctAnswer":"inchoate","distractors":["fully-formed","mature","complete"],"explanation":"'Inchoate' means not yet fully formed. 'A cluster of hopes more than a strategy, with no dates, no budget' is rudimentary; fully-formed, mature, and complete are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_491","entry":"recalcitrant","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"Having an obstinately uncooperative attitude toward authority or discipline; stubbornly defiant.","secondaryMeaning":"Stubbornly resisting authority.","passage":"The ______ patient refused the simplest instructions, fighting the nurses who tried to raise the head of his bed even after the doctor explained why he should.","correctAnswer":"recalcitrant","distractors":["obedient","compliant","docile"],"explanation":"'Recalcitrant' means stubbornly defiant. Refusing 'the simplest instructions' and 'fighting the nurses' is defiance; obedient, compliant, and docile are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_492","entry":"vitiate","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Science Passages"],"definition":"To spoil or reduce the quality or effectiveness of; to impair or invalidate.","secondaryMeaning":"To weaken or corrupt.","passage":"A single contaminated sample was enough to ______ the whole experiment, forcing the team to repeat three months of work before they could trust a single result.","correctAnswer":"vitiate","distractors":["strengthen","improve","restore"],"explanation":"'Vitiate' means to spoil or impair. A contaminated sample 'forcing the team to repeat three months' spoils the experiment; strengthening, improving, or restoring it would help it."},{"id":"vocab_493","entry":"redolent","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"adjective","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Literature"],"definition":"Strongly reminiscent of something; fragrant or strongly scented.","secondaryMeaning":"Evocative; filled with a scent.","passage":"The old library was ______ with the smell of binding glue and damp paper, a scent the alumni insisted could not be found in any building built after 1950.","correctAnswer":"redolent","distractors":["scentless","unrelated","neutral"],"explanation":"'Redolent' means strongly scented or evocative. 'The smell of binding glue and damp paper' that alumni 'could not find' elsewhere is a strong scent; scentless, unrelated, and neutral are the opposite."},{"id":"vocab_494","entry":"desuetude","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_1","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","History Passages"],"definition":"A state of disuse or inactivity; the condition of no longer being used or practiced.","secondaryMeaning":"Disuse; cessation of use.","passage":"The old dialect had fallen into ______, and the linguist had to record its last three speakers before the year was out or lose the language entirely.","correctAnswer":"desuetude","distractors":["use","practice","currency"],"explanation":"'Desuetude' means a state of disuse. The dialect 'fallen into' disuse with 'only three speakers' left fits; use, practice, and currency are the opposite of disuse."},{"id":"vocab_495","entry":"egress","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"noun","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"A way out; the action of leaving or going out.","secondaryMeaning":"An exit; the act of exiting.","passage":"The fire inspector demanded a wider ______ from the balcony, noting that the present door would let only one person through at a time in a panic.","correctAnswer":"egress","distractors":["digress","ingress","entrance"],"explanation":"'Egress' means a way out. A 'wider way out' for fire safety fits; 'digress' (to wander off the subject) is the confusable partner. 'Ingress' and 'entrance' are ways in, the opposite of out."},{"id":"vocab_496","entry":"prescribe","type":"word","partOfSpeech":"verb","tier":"tier_2","difficulty":3,"sat_context":["Words in Context","Rhetorical Synthesis"],"definition":"To advise and authorize the use of, especially as a remedy; to set down as a rule.","secondaryMeaning":"To ordain or direct authoritatively.","passage":"The doctor would ______ only rest and water for the fever, refusing to add anything stronger until the lab had identified what was causing it.","correctAnswer":"prescribe","distractors":["proscribe","permit","allow"],"explanation":"'Prescribe' means to authorize as a remedy. 'Rest and water for the fever' authorized by the doctor is a remedy; 'proscribe' (to forbid) is the confusable partner. 'Permit' and 'allow' do not name the act of authorizing a treatment."}];</script>

